M127.addLongAnswer([
  {id:"CN2.5.1-XR1", src:"Course notes 2.5, Exercise 1", lec:"L12", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 86",
   q:"Complete the table converting between degrees and radians. The degree row is $0^\\circ, 30^\\circ, 45^\\circ, 60^\\circ, \\_\\_, 120^\\circ, 135^\\circ, \\_\\_, 180^\\circ, 270^\\circ, 360^\\circ$. The two blank degree entries have radian measures $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and $\\frac{5\\pi}{6}$. Fill in the two missing degree values and the radian measure of every entry.",
   sol:[
    "Use $360^\\circ = 2\\pi$ rad, so $1^\\circ = \\frac{\\pi}{180}$ rad and $1 \\text{ rad} = \\frac{180^\\circ}{\\pi}$.",
    "Degrees to radians: multiply by $\\frac{\\pi}{180}$. For example $30^\\circ = 30\\cdot\\frac{\\pi}{180} = \\frac{\\pi}{6}$ and $135^\\circ = 135\\cdot\\frac{\\pi}{180} = \\frac{3\\pi}{4}$.",
    "Radians to degrees: multiply by $\\frac{180^\\circ}{\\pi}$. So $\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\cdot\\frac{180^\\circ}{\\pi} = 90^\\circ$ and $\\frac{5\\pi}{6}\\cdot\\frac{180^\\circ}{\\pi} = 150^\\circ$.",
    "$\\begin{array}{c|cccccc} \\text{deg} & 0 & 30 & 45 & 60 & 90 & 120 \\\\ \\hline \\text{rad} & 0 & \\frac{\\pi}{6} & \\frac{\\pi}{4} & \\frac{\\pi}{3} & \\frac{\\pi}{2} & \\frac{2\\pi}{3} \\end{array}$",
    "$\\begin{array}{c|ccccc} \\text{deg} & 135 & 150 & 180 & 270 & 360 \\\\ \\hline \\text{rad} & \\frac{3\\pi}{4} & \\frac{5\\pi}{6} & \\pi & \\frac{3\\pi}{2} & 2\\pi \\end{array}$"],
   ans:"Missing degrees: $90^\\circ$ and $150^\\circ$. Radians in order: $0, \\frac{\\pi}{6}, \\frac{\\pi}{4}, \\frac{\\pi}{3}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{2\\pi}{3}, \\frac{3\\pi}{4}, \\frac{5\\pi}{6}, \\pi, \\frac{3\\pi}{2}, 2\\pi$."},
  {id:"CN2.5.1-XR2", src:"Course notes 2.5, Exercise 2", lec:"L12", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 87",
   q:"Fill in the values of $\\sin(\\theta)$, $\\cos(\\theta)$ and $\\tan(\\theta)$ for $\\theta = 0, \\frac{\\pi}{6}, \\frac{\\pi}{4}, \\frac{\\pi}{3}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{2\\pi}{3}, \\frac{3\\pi}{4}, \\frac{5\\pi}{6}, \\pi, \\frac{3\\pi}{2}, \\frac{11\\pi}{6}, 2\\pi$.",
   sol:[
    "First quadrant values come from the special triangles: the $\\frac{\\pi}{6}$, $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$, $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ triangle with sides $1, \\sqrt3, 2$ and the $\\frac{\\pi}{4}$, $\\frac{\\pi}{4}$, $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ triangle with sides $1, 1, \\sqrt2$.",
    "Other quadrants: use the reference angle and the CAST rule for the sign (all positive in quadrant 1, only $\\sin$ in quadrant 2, only $\\tan$ in quadrant 3, only $\\cos$ in quadrant 4). The axis angles come from the unit circle: $(\\cos(\\theta), \\sin(\\theta)) = (1,0), (0,1), (-1,0), (0,-1)$ at $\\theta = 0, \\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\pi, \\frac{3\\pi}{2}$.",
    "$\\tan(\\theta) = \\frac{\\sin(\\theta)}{\\cos(\\theta)}$, which does not exist (DNE) when $\\cos(\\theta) = 0$.",
    "$\\begin{array}{c|cccccc} \\theta & 0 & \\frac{\\pi}{6} & \\frac{\\pi}{4} & \\frac{\\pi}{3} & \\frac{\\pi}{2} & \\frac{2\\pi}{3} \\\\ \\hline \\sin & 0 & \\frac12 & \\frac{\\sqrt2}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt3}{2} & 1 & \\frac{\\sqrt3}{2} \\\\ \\cos & 1 & \\frac{\\sqrt3}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt2}{2} & \\frac12 & 0 & -\\frac12 \\\\ \\tan & 0 & \\frac{\\sqrt3}{3} & 1 & \\sqrt3 & \\text{DNE} & -\\sqrt3 \\end{array}$",
    "$\\begin{array}{c|cccccc} \\theta & \\frac{3\\pi}{4} & \\frac{5\\pi}{6} & \\pi & \\frac{3\\pi}{2} & \\frac{11\\pi}{6} & 2\\pi \\\\ \\hline \\sin & \\frac{\\sqrt2}{2} & \\frac12 & 0 & -1 & -\\frac12 & 0 \\\\ \\cos & -\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2} & -1 & 0 & \\frac{\\sqrt3}{2} & 1 \\\\ \\tan & -1 & -\\frac{\\sqrt3}{3} & 0 & \\text{DNE} & -\\frac{\\sqrt3}{3} & 0 \\end{array}$"],
   ans:"See the two tables. $\\tan(\\theta)$ does not exist at $\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and $\\theta = \\frac{3\\pi}{2}$."},
  {id:"CN2.5.1-XR3-a-d", src:"Course notes 2.5, Exercise 3, parts (a) to (d)", lec:"L12", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 88",
   q:"Write all angles $\\theta$ with $0 \\le \\theta \\le 4\\pi$ such that<br>(a) $\\sin(\\theta) = \\frac12$<br>(b) $\\cos(\\theta) = \\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}$<br>(c) $\\tan(\\theta) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt3}$<br>(d) $\\sec(\\theta) = 2$",
   sol:[
    "(a) $\\sin(\\theta) > 0$ in quadrants 1 and 2. The reference angle is $\\frac{\\pi}{6}$ since $\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) = \\frac12$.",
    "In $[0, 2\\pi]$: $\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{6}$ and $\\theta = \\pi - \\frac{\\pi}{6} = \\frac{5\\pi}{6}$. Adding $2\\pi$ gives the solutions in $[2\\pi, 4\\pi]$: $\\frac{13\\pi}{6}, \\frac{17\\pi}{6}$.",
    "(b) $\\cos(\\theta) > 0$ in quadrants 1 and 4. Reference angle $\\frac{\\pi}{6}$.",
    "In $[0,2\\pi]$: $\\frac{\\pi}{6}$ and $2\\pi - \\frac{\\pi}{6} = \\frac{11\\pi}{6}$. Add $2\\pi$: $\\frac{13\\pi}{6}, \\frac{23\\pi}{6}$.",
    "(c) $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt3} = \\frac{\\sqrt3}{3}$. $\\tan(\\theta) > 0$ in quadrants 1 and 3. Reference angle $\\frac{\\pi}{6}$.",
    "$\\tan$ has period $\\pi$, so the solutions are $\\frac{\\pi}{6} + k\\pi$: $\\frac{\\pi}{6}, \\frac{7\\pi}{6}, \\frac{13\\pi}{6}, \\frac{19\\pi}{6}$.",
    "(d) $\\sec(\\theta) = \\frac{1}{\\cos(\\theta)} = 2$ means $\\cos(\\theta) = \\frac12$. Quadrants 1 and 4, reference angle $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$.",
    "In $[0,2\\pi]$: $\\frac{\\pi}{3}, \\frac{5\\pi}{3}$. Add $2\\pi$: $\\frac{7\\pi}{3}, \\frac{11\\pi}{3}$."],
   ans:"(a) $\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{6}, \\frac{5\\pi}{6}, \\frac{13\\pi}{6}, \\frac{17\\pi}{6}$; (b) $\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{6}, \\frac{11\\pi}{6}, \\frac{13\\pi}{6}, \\frac{23\\pi}{6}$; (c) $\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{6}, \\frac{7\\pi}{6}, \\frac{13\\pi}{6}, \\frac{19\\pi}{6}$; (d) $\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{3}, \\frac{5\\pi}{3}, \\frac{7\\pi}{3}, \\frac{11\\pi}{3}$"},
  {id:"CN2.5.1-XR3-e-h", src:"Course notes 2.5, Exercise 3, parts (e) to (h)", lec:"L12", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 88",
   q:"Write all angles $\\theta$ with $0 \\le \\theta \\le 4\\pi$ such that<br>(e) $\\cot(\\theta) = 1$<br>(f) $\\sin(\\theta) = 0$<br>(g) $\\cos(\\theta) = 0$<br>(h) $\\cot(\\theta) = 0$",
   sol:[
    "(e) $\\cot(\\theta) = \\frac{1}{\\tan(\\theta)} = 1$ means $\\tan(\\theta) = 1$. Quadrants 1 and 3, reference angle $\\frac{\\pi}{4}$.",
    "Solutions $\\frac{\\pi}{4} + k\\pi$: $\\frac{\\pi}{4}, \\frac{5\\pi}{4}, \\frac{9\\pi}{4}, \\frac{13\\pi}{4}$.",
    "(f) $\\sin(\\theta) = 0$ where the unit circle point is $(\\pm1, 0)$, that is at integer multiples of $\\pi$.",
    "(g) $\\cos(\\theta) = 0$ where the unit circle point is $(0, \\pm1)$, that is at $\\frac{\\pi}{2} + k\\pi$.",
    "(h) $\\cot(\\theta) = \\frac{\\cos(\\theta)}{\\sin(\\theta)} = 0$ exactly when $\\cos(\\theta) = 0$ (then $\\sin(\\theta) = \\pm1 \\ne 0$, so $\\cot$ is defined).",
    "Same solutions as (g)."],
   ans:"(e) $\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{4}, \\frac{5\\pi}{4}, \\frac{9\\pi}{4}, \\frac{13\\pi}{4}$; (f) $\\theta = 0, \\pi, 2\\pi, 3\\pi, 4\\pi$; (g) $\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{3\\pi}{2}, \\frac{5\\pi}{2}, \\frac{7\\pi}{2}$; (h) $\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{3\\pi}{2}, \\frac{5\\pi}{2}, \\frac{7\\pi}{2}$"},
  {id:"CN2.5.1-EX1", src:"Course notes 2.5, Example 1", lec:"L12", kind:"example", page:"p. 88",
   q:"Given that $\\tan(\\theta) = \\frac{2}{3}$ and $0 < \\theta < \\frac{\\pi}{2}$, find $\\cos(\\theta)$.",
   sol:[
    "$\\tan(\\theta) = \\frac{o}{a} = \\frac23$, so draw a right triangle with opposite side $o = 2$ and adjacent side $a = 3$. Both are positive since $\\theta$ is in the first quadrant.",
    "Pythagoras: $h^2 = o^2 + a^2 = 2^2 + 3^2 = 13$, so $|h| = \\sqrt{13}$, and $h = \\sqrt{13}$ since the hypotenuse is always positive.",
    "$\\cos(\\theta) = \\frac{a}{h} = \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}$."],
   ans:"$\\cos(\\theta) = \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}$"},
  {id:"CN2.5.1-EX2", src:"Course notes 2.5, Example 2", lec:"L12", kind:"example", page:"p. 89",
   q:"Given that $\\sin(\\theta) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt6}$ and $\\frac{\\pi}{2} < \\theta < \\pi$, find $\\tan(\\theta)$.",
   sol:[
    "$\\sin(\\theta) = \\frac{o}{h} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt6}$: opposite $o = 1$, hypotenuse $h = \\sqrt6$.",
    "$h^2 = o^2 + a^2 \\Rightarrow (\\sqrt6)^2 = 1^2 + a^2 \\Rightarrow 6 = 1 + a^2 \\Rightarrow a^2 = 5 \\Rightarrow |a| = \\sqrt5$.",
    "$\\theta$ is in the second quadrant, where the $x$-coordinate (adjacent side) is negative, so $a = -\\sqrt5$.",
    "$\\tan(\\theta) = \\frac{o}{a} = \\frac{1}{-\\sqrt5} = -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt5}$."],
   ans:"$\\tan(\\theta) = -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt5}$"},
  {id:"CN2.5.1-XR4", src:"Course notes 2.5, Exercise 4", lec:"L12", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 89",
   q:"Given that $\\cos(\\theta) = \\frac13$ and $\\frac{3\\pi}{2} < \\theta < 2\\pi$, find $\\tan(\\theta)$.",
   sol:[
    "$\\cos(\\theta) = \\frac{a}{h} = \\frac13$: adjacent $a = 1$, hypotenuse $h = 3$.",
    "$h^2 = o^2 + a^2 \\Rightarrow 3^2 = o^2 + 1^2 \\Rightarrow o^2 = 8 \\Rightarrow |o| = \\sqrt8$.",
    "$\\theta$ is in the fourth quadrant, where the $y$-coordinate (opposite side) is negative, so $o = -\\sqrt8$.",
    "$\\tan(\\theta) = \\frac{o}{a} = \\frac{-\\sqrt8}{1} = -\\sqrt8 = -2\\sqrt2$."],
   ans:"$\\tan(\\theta) = -\\sqrt8 = -2\\sqrt2$"},
  {id:"CN2.5.1-XR5", src:"Course notes 2.5, Exercise 5", lec:"L12", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 89",
   q:"Given that $\\tan(\\theta) = 4$ and $\\pi < \\theta < \\frac{3\\pi}{2}$, find $\\sin(\\theta)$.",
   sol:[
    "$\\tan(\\theta) = \\frac{o}{a} = 4$. In the third quadrant both signed sides are negative, so take $o = -4$ and $a = -1$ (then $\\frac{o}{a} = 4$).",
    "$h^2 = o^2 + a^2 = (-4)^2 + (-1)^2 = 17$, and $h = \\sqrt{17}$ since the hypotenuse is always positive.",
    "$\\sin(\\theta) = \\frac{o}{h} = \\frac{-4}{\\sqrt{17}}$."],
   ans:"$\\sin(\\theta) = -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}$"},
  {id:"CN2.5.1-EX3", src:"Course notes 2.5, Example 3", lec:"L12", kind:"example", page:"p. 89",
   q:"Given that $\\tan(\\theta) = 3x$ and $-\\frac{\\pi}{2} < \\theta < \\frac{\\pi}{2}$, find $\\sec(\\theta)$.",
   sol:[
    "$\\tan(\\theta) = \\frac{3x}{1} = \\frac{o}{a}$: opposite $o = 3x$, adjacent $a = 1$.",
    "On $-\\frac{\\pi}{2} < \\theta < \\frac{\\pi}{2}$ we have $\\cos(\\theta) > 0$, so the adjacent side is positive, which matches $a = 1$. The sign of $x$ only affects the opposite side.",
    "$h^2 = o^2 + a^2 = (3x)^2 + 1^2 = 9x^2 + 1$, so $|h| = \\sqrt{9x^2+1}$ and $h = \\sqrt{9x^2+1}$ since the hypotenuse is always positive.",
    "$\\sec(\\theta) = \\frac{h}{a} = \\frac{\\sqrt{9x^2+1}}{1} = \\sqrt{9x^2+1}$."],
   ans:"$\\sec(\\theta) = \\sqrt{9x^2+1}$"},
  {id:"CN2.5.1-EX4", src:"Course notes 2.5, Example 4", lec:"L12", kind:"example", page:"p. 90",
   q:"Given that $\\sec(\\theta) = \\frac{2x}{3}$ and $0 < \\theta < \\frac{\\pi}{2}$, find $\\tan(\\theta)$.",
   sol:[
    "$\\sec(\\theta) = \\frac{h}{a} = \\frac{2x}{3}$: hypotenuse $h = 2x$, adjacent $a = 3$.",
    "$h^2 = o^2 + a^2 \\Rightarrow (2x)^2 = o^2 + 3^2 \\Rightarrow o^2 = 4x^2 - 9 \\Rightarrow |o| = \\sqrt{4x^2-9}$.",
    "First quadrant, so $o = \\sqrt{4x^2-9}$.",
    "$\\tan(\\theta) = \\frac{o}{a} = \\frac{\\sqrt{4x^2-9}}{3}$."],
   ans:"$\\tan(\\theta) = \\frac{\\sqrt{4x^2-9}}{3}$"},
  {id:"CN2.5.1-XR6", src:"Course notes 2.5, Exercise 6", lec:"L12", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 90",
   q:"Given that $\\sin(\\theta) = \\frac{2x}{3}$ and $-\\frac{\\pi}{2} < \\theta < \\frac{\\pi}{2}$, find $\\cos(\\theta)$.",
   sol:[
    "$\\sin(\\theta) = \\frac{o}{h} = \\frac{2x}{3}$: opposite $o = 2x$, hypotenuse $h = 3$.",
    "$h^2 = o^2 + a^2 \\Rightarrow 9 = 4x^2 + a^2 \\Rightarrow a^2 = 9 - 4x^2 \\Rightarrow |a| = \\sqrt{9-4x^2}$.",
    "On $-\\frac{\\pi}{2} < \\theta < \\frac{\\pi}{2}$, $\\cos(\\theta) > 0$, so $a = \\sqrt{9-4x^2}$.",
    "$\\cos(\\theta) = \\frac{a}{h} = \\frac{\\sqrt{9-4x^2}}{3}$."],
   ans:"$\\cos(\\theta) = \\frac{\\sqrt{9-4x^2}}{3}$"},
  {id:"CN2.5.1-XR7", src:"Course notes 2.5, Exercise 7", lec:"L12", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 90",
   q:"Given that $\\sec(\\theta) = 2x$ and $0 < \\theta < \\frac{\\pi}{2}$, find $\\tan(\\theta)$ and $\\sin(\\theta)$.",
   sol:[
    "$\\sec(\\theta) = \\frac{h}{a} = \\frac{2x}{1}$: hypotenuse $h = 2x$, adjacent $a = 1$.",
    "$h^2 = o^2 + a^2 \\Rightarrow 4x^2 = o^2 + 1 \\Rightarrow o^2 = 4x^2 - 1 \\Rightarrow |o| = \\sqrt{4x^2-1}$, and $o = \\sqrt{4x^2-1}$ in the first quadrant.",
    "$\\tan(\\theta) = \\frac{o}{a} = \\sqrt{4x^2-1}$ and $\\sin(\\theta) = \\frac{o}{h} = \\frac{\\sqrt{4x^2-1}}{2x}$."],
   ans:"$\\tan(\\theta) = \\sqrt{4x^2-1}$, $\\sin(\\theta) = \\frac{\\sqrt{4x^2-1}}{2x}$"},
  {id:"CN2.5.1-EX5", src:"Course notes 2.5, Example 5", lec:"L12", kind:"example", page:"p. 91",
   q:"Show that $\\cos(2\\theta) = \\cos^2(\\theta) - \\sin^2(\\theta)$.",
   sol:[
    "Use $\\cos(x+y) = \\cos(x)\\cos(y) - \\sin(x)\\sin(y)$ with $x = y = \\theta$:",
    "$\\cos(2\\theta) = \\cos(\\theta + \\theta) = \\cos(\\theta)\\cos(\\theta) - \\sin(\\theta)\\sin(\\theta) = \\cos^2(\\theta) - \\sin^2(\\theta)$."],
   ans:"$\\cos(2\\theta) = \\cos^2(\\theta) - \\sin^2(\\theta)$, as required."},
  {id:"CN2.5.1-XR8", src:"Course notes 2.5, Exercise 8", lec:"L12", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 91",
   q:"Show that $\\sec^2(x) = \\tan^2(x) + 1$.",
   sol:[
    "Start from $\\sin^2(x) + \\cos^2(x) = 1$ and divide both sides by $\\cos^2(x)$ (where $\\cos(x) \\ne 0$):",
    "$\\frac{\\sin^2(x)}{\\cos^2(x)} + \\frac{\\cos^2(x)}{\\cos^2(x)} = \\frac{1}{\\cos^2(x)}$.",
    "Since $\\frac{\\sin(x)}{\\cos(x)} = \\tan(x)$ and $\\frac{1}{\\cos(x)} = \\sec(x)$, this is $\\tan^2(x) + 1 = \\sec^2(x)$."],
   ans:"$\\sec^2(x) = \\tan^2(x) + 1$, as required."},
  {id:"CN2.5.1-EX6", src:"Course notes 2.5, Example 6", lec:"L12", kind:"example", page:"p. 91",
   q:"Find all values of $x$ in the interval $[0, 2\\pi]$ that satisfy $2\\cos(x) + \\sin(2x) = 0$.",
   sol:[
    "Use $\\sin(2x) = 2\\sin(x)\\cos(x)$: $2\\cos(x) + 2\\sin(x)\\cos(x) = 0$.",
    "Factor: $2\\cos(x)(1 + \\sin(x)) = 0$, so $\\cos(x) = 0$ or $\\sin(x) = -1$.",
    "$\\cos(x) = 0$ when $x = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$ or $x = \\frac{3\\pi}{2}$. $\\sin(x) = -1$ when $x = \\frac{3\\pi}{2}$."],
   ans:"$x = \\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{3\\pi}{2}$"},
  {id:"CN2.5.1-XR9", src:"Course notes 2.5, Exercise 9", lec:"L12", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 91",
   q:"Find all values of $x$ in the interval $[0, 2\\pi]$ that satisfy $\\sin(x)\\cos(x) = \\frac{\\sqrt3}{4}$.",
   sol:[
    "Use $\\sin(2x) = 2\\sin(x)\\cos(x)$, so $\\sin(x)\\cos(x) = \\frac12\\sin(2x)$.",
    "$\\frac12\\sin(2x) = \\frac{\\sqrt3}{4} \\Rightarrow \\sin(2x) = \\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}$.",
    "Since $0 \\le x \\le 2\\pi$, we need $0 \\le 2x \\le 4\\pi$. $\\sin(u) = \\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}$ for $u = \\frac{\\pi}{3}, \\frac{2\\pi}{3}, \\frac{7\\pi}{3}, \\frac{8\\pi}{3}$ in $[0, 4\\pi]$.",
    "Divide by 2: $x = \\frac{\\pi}{6}, \\frac{\\pi}{3}, \\frac{7\\pi}{6}, \\frac{4\\pi}{3}$."],
   ans:"$x = \\frac{\\pi}{6}, \\frac{\\pi}{3}, \\frac{7\\pi}{6}, \\frac{4\\pi}{3}$"},
  {id:"CN2.5.1-XR10", src:"Course notes 2.5, Exercise 10", lec:"L12", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 91",
   q:"Find all values of $x$ in the interval $[0, 2\\pi]$ that satisfy $2\\sin^2(x) + \\cos(x) = 1$.",
   sol:[
    "Use $\\sin^2(x) = 1 - \\cos^2(x)$: $2(1 - \\cos^2(x)) + \\cos(x) = 1$.",
    "$2 - 2\\cos^2(x) + \\cos(x) - 1 = 0 \\Rightarrow 2\\cos^2(x) - \\cos(x) - 1 = 0$.",
    "Factor as a quadratic in $\\cos(x)$: $(2\\cos(x) + 1)(\\cos(x) - 1) = 0$, so $\\cos(x) = -\\frac12$ or $\\cos(x) = 1$.",
    "$\\cos(x) = -\\frac12$ gives $x = \\frac{2\\pi}{3}, \\frac{4\\pi}{3}$. $\\cos(x) = 1$ gives $x = 0, 2\\pi$."],
   ans:"$x = 0, \\frac{2\\pi}{3}, \\frac{4\\pi}{3}, 2\\pi$"},
  {id:"CN2.5.1-EX7", src:"Course notes 2.5, Example 7", lec:"L12", kind:"example", page:"p. 93",
   q:"Sketch the graph of $f(\\theta) = 2\\sin(3\\theta) + 1$ for $0 \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi$, showing each transformation step.",
   sol:[
    "Step 1, $y = \\sin(3\\theta)$: the $3$ inside is a horizontal compression by a factor of 3, so the period is $\\frac{2\\pi}{3}$. On $[0, 2\\pi]$ there are three full periods, with zeros at $0, \\frac{\\pi}{3}, \\frac{2\\pi}{3}, \\pi, \\frac{4\\pi}{3}, \\frac{5\\pi}{3}, 2\\pi$ and range $[-1, 1]$.",
    "Step 2, $y = 2\\sin(3\\theta)$: vertical stretch by a factor of 2, range $[-2, 2]$.",
    "Step 3, $y = 2\\sin(3\\theta) + 1$: vertical translation up 1 unit, range $[-1, 3]$, centre line $y = 1$.",
    "Key points: starts at $(0, 1)$ and rises. Maximum $3$ at $\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{6}, \\frac{5\\pi}{6}, \\frac{3\\pi}{2}$. Minimum $-1$ at $\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{7\\pi}{6}, \\frac{11\\pi}{6}$. Crosses $y = 1$ at $\\theta = 0, \\frac{\\pi}{3}, \\frac{2\\pi}{3}, \\pi, \\frac{4\\pi}{3}, \\frac{5\\pi}{3}, 2\\pi$."],
   ans:"Three periods of length $\\frac{2\\pi}{3}$ oscillating between $-1$ and $3$ about $y = 1$, starting at $(0,1)$ and increasing."},
  {id:"CN2.5.1-XR11", src:"Course notes 2.5, Exercise 11", lec:"L12", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 94",
   q:"Sketch one period of the graph of $f(\\theta) = 3\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\theta}{2}\\right) - 1$, showing each transformation step.",
   sol:[
    "Step 1, $y = \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\theta}{2}\\right)$: horizontal stretch by a factor of 2, period $4\\pi$. On $[0, 4\\pi]$: $1$ at $0$, $0$ at $\\pi$, $-1$ at $2\\pi$, $0$ at $3\\pi$, $1$ at $4\\pi$.",
    "Step 2, $y = 3\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\theta}{2}\\right)$: vertical stretch by a factor of 3, range $[-3, 3]$.",
    "Step 3, $y = 3\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\theta}{2}\\right) - 1$: vertical translation down 1 unit, range $[-4, 2]$.",
    "Key points: $(0, 2)$, $(\\pi, -1)$, $(2\\pi, -4)$, $(3\\pi, -1)$, $(4\\pi, 2)$."],
   ans:"One period on $[0, 4\\pi]$ from the maximum $(0,2)$ down to the minimum $(2\\pi, -4)$ and back to $(4\\pi, 2)$."},
  {id:"CN2.5.1-XR12", src:"Course notes 2.5, Exercise 12", lec:"L12", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 94",
   q:"Sketch one period of the graph of $f(\\theta) = -\\tan(2\\theta)$, showing each transformation step.",
   sol:[
    "Step 1, $y = \\tan(2\\theta)$: horizontal compression by a factor of 2, period $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$. One period on $\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{4}, \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$ with vertical asymptotes $\\theta = \\pm\\frac{\\pi}{4}$, increasing through the origin.",
    "Step 2, $y = -\\tan(2\\theta)$: reflection in the $\\theta$-axis, so the graph is decreasing on $\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{4}, \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$.",
    "Key points: $\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{8}, 1\\right)$, $(0, 0)$, $\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{8}, -1\\right)$. $f(\\theta) \\to \\infty$ as $\\theta \\to -\\frac{\\pi}{4}^+$ and $f(\\theta) \\to -\\infty$ as $\\theta \\to \\frac{\\pi}{4}^-$."],
   ans:"Decreasing branch through the origin on $\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{4}, \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$ with vertical asymptotes $\\theta = \\pm\\frac{\\pi}{4}$."},
  {id:"CN2.5.1-EX8", src:"Course notes 2.5, Example 8", lec:"L12", kind:"example", page:"p. 95",
   q:"Determine the maximum, minimum, average, amplitude, and period of $f(x) = 4\\cos\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right) + 3$.",
   sol:[
    "For $f(x) = a\\cos(b(x-c)) + d$ or $a\\sin(b(x-c)) + d$: average $= d$, amplitude $= |a|$, max $= d + |a|$, min $= d - |a|$, period $= \\frac{2\\pi}{|b|}$.",
    "The coefficient 4 is a vertical stretch by 4, so $4\\cos\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)$ has range $[-4, 4]$. Translating up 3 gives range $[-1, 7]$.",
    "So max $= 7$, min $= -1$, average $= 3$ (the vertical translation), amplitude $= 7 - 3 = 4$.",
    "$b = \\frac12$ is a horizontal stretch by 2, so the period is $\\frac{2\\pi}{1/2} = 4\\pi$."],
   ans:"Max $7$, min $-1$, average $3$, amplitude $4$, period $4\\pi$"},
  {id:"CN2.5.1-XR13", src:"Course notes 2.5, Exercise 13", lec:"L12", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 95",
   q:"Determine the maximum, minimum, average, amplitude, and period of $f(\\theta) = 3\\sin(2\\theta) + 1$.",
   sol:[
    "For $f(x) = a\\cos(b(x-c)) + d$ or $a\\sin(b(x-c)) + d$: average $= d$, amplitude $= |a|$, max $= d + |a|$, min $= d - |a|$, period $= \\frac{2\\pi}{|b|}$.",
    "$a = 3$, $b = 2$, $d = 1$: average $1$, amplitude $3$, max $1 + 3 = 4$, min $1 - 3 = -2$, period $\\frac{2\\pi}{2} = \\pi$."],
   ans:"Max $4$, min $-2$, average $1$, amplitude $3$, period $\\pi$"},
  {id:"CN2.5.1-XR14", src:"Course notes 2.5, Exercise 14", lec:"L12", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 95",
   q:"Determine the maximum, minimum, average, amplitude, and period of $f(\\theta) = -2\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\theta}{3}\\right) + \\pi$.",
   sol:[
    "For $f(x) = a\\cos(b(x-c)) + d$ or $a\\sin(b(x-c)) + d$: average $= d$, amplitude $= |a|$, max $= d + |a|$, min $= d - |a|$, period $= \\frac{2\\pi}{|b|}$.",
    "$a = -2$, $b = \\frac13$, $d = \\pi$: average $\\pi$, amplitude $|-2| = 2$, max $\\pi + 2$, min $\\pi - 2$, period $\\frac{2\\pi}{1/3} = 6\\pi$.",
    "The negative $a$ is a reflection; it does not change the amplitude."],
   ans:"Max $\\pi + 2$, min $\\pi - 2$, average $\\pi$, amplitude $2$, period $6\\pi$"},
  {id:"CN2.5.2-EX9", src:"Course notes 2.5, Example 9", lec:"L13", kind:"example", page:"p. 95",
   q:"One's ultradian rhythm is a cosine wave with a period of approximately 4 hours, an average value of 0, and an amplitude of 0.4. Write an equation for one's ultradian rhythm $U$ at any time $t$ (in hours).",
   sol:[
    "Look for $U(t) = a\\cos(bt) + c$.",
    "The average is 0, so $c = 0$.",
    "The period of $\\cos$ is normally $2\\pi$. We want period 4, so choose $b$ so that the inside equals $2\\pi$ when $t = 4$: $4b = 2\\pi \\Rightarrow b = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$.",
    "The amplitude is 0.4 and the maximum of $\\cos$ is 1, so $a = 0.4$."],
   ans:"$U(t) = 0.4\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}t\\right)$"},
  {id:"CN2.5.2-EX10", src:"Course notes 2.5, Example 10", lec:"L13", kind:"example", page:"p. 96",
   q:"A city's high tide occurs at midnight and the height of the water $h$ in feet at $t$ hours after midnight is $h(t) = 5 + 4.1\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}t\\right)$.<br>(a) What is the average height of the water?<br>(b) What is the maximum height?<br>(c) What is the period of this function, and what does it represent in terms of tides?<br>(d) When was low tide and what was the water level at that time?",
   sol:[
    "(a) The vertical translation is 5, so the average height is 5 ft.",
    "(b) Max $=$ average $+$ amplitude $= 5 + 4.1 = 9.1$ ft.",
    "(c) The period of $\\cos$ is normally $2\\pi$, so solve $\\frac{\\pi}{6}t = 2\\pi$: $t = 12$. The period is 12 hours, which is the time between consecutive high tides.",
    "(d) Low tide is when $\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}t\\right) = -1$, that is $\\frac{\\pi}{6}t = \\pi + 2\\pi k$. So $t = 6 + 12k$: $t = 6$ (6 am) and $t = 18$ (6 pm).",
    "At low tide $h = 5 + 4.1(-1) = 0.9$ ft."],
   ans:"(a) 5 ft; (b) 9.1 ft; (c) 12 hours, the time between high tides; (d) 6 am and 6 pm, water level 0.9 ft"},
  {id:"CN2.5.2-EX11", src:"Course notes 2.5, Example 11", lec:"L13", kind:"example", page:"p. 96",
   q:"An observer stands 200 m from the launch site of a hot air balloon. The balloon rises vertically at a constant rate of 4 m/s. As the balloon rises, its distance from the ground $y$ and its angle of elevation $\\theta$ (measured at the observer) change simultaneously. The picture is a right triangle with the horizontal side of 200 m from the observer to the launch site, the vertical side $y$ from the launch site up to the balloon, and the angle $\\theta$ at the observer. Find an equation relating the angle of elevation to the distance above the ground. Use it to determine the height of the balloon when the angle is $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$ radians.",
   sol:[
    "Relative to $\\theta$, the 200 m side is adjacent and $y$ is opposite, so use tangent: $\\tan(\\theta) = \\frac{y}{200}$.",
    "When $\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{3}$: $y = 200\\tan\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = 200\\sqrt3$ m."],
   ans:"$\\tan(\\theta) = \\frac{y}{200}$; the height is $200\\sqrt3 \\approx 346$ m"},
  {id:"CN2.5.2-EX12", src:"Course notes 2.5, Example 12", lec:"L13", kind:"example", page:"p. 97",
   q:"A rain gutter is constructed from a 30 cm wide metal sheet by bending up one third of the width on each side by an angle of $\\theta$. The cross-section has a flat bottom of 10 cm and two 10 cm sides, each tilted up at angle $\\theta$ from the horizontal line that extends the bottom. Find the cross-sectional area of the gutter as a function of $\\theta$.",
   sol:[
    "Let $y$ be the height of the gutter and $x$ the horizontal width of each tilted side.",
    "The cross-section is a $10 \\times y$ rectangle plus two right triangles with legs $x$ and $y$: Area $= \\frac12xy + 10y + \\frac12xy = xy + 10y$.",
    "In each triangle the hypotenuse is 10: $\\frac{x}{10} = \\cos(\\theta)$ and $\\frac{y}{10} = \\sin(\\theta)$, so $x = 10\\cos(\\theta)$ and $y = 10\\sin(\\theta)$.",
    "Area $= 10\\cos(\\theta)\\cdot 10\\sin(\\theta) + 10\\cdot 10\\sin(\\theta) = 100\\sin(\\theta)\\cos(\\theta) + 100\\sin(\\theta)$.",
    "Using $2\\sin(\\theta)\\cos(\\theta) = \\sin(2\\theta)$: Area $= 50\\sin(2\\theta) + 100\\sin(\\theta)$."],
   ans:"$A(\\theta) = 50\\sin(2\\theta) + 100\\sin(\\theta)$ cm$^2$"},
  {id:"CN2.5.2-XR15", src:"Course notes 2.5, Exercise 15", lec:"L13", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 97",
   q:"Suppose high tide is at midnight and the water level at high tide is 8.7 feet while the water level at low tide is 0.3 feet. Assuming the next high tide occurs 12 hours later at noon, find a formula for the water level as a function of $t$, where $t$ is the time in hours and midnight is $t = 0$.",
   sol:[
    "Average: $d = \\frac{\\max + \\min}{2} = \\frac{8.7 + 0.3}{2} = 4.5$.",
    "Amplitude: $a = \\max - d = 8.7 - 4.5 = 4.2$.",
    "The function starts at a maximum at $t = 0$, so use cosine with no horizontal shift.",
    "Period 12: solve $12b = 2\\pi$, so $b = \\frac{\\pi}{6}$."],
   ans:"$h(t) = 4.2\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}t\\right) + 4.5$"},
  {id:"CN2.5.2-XR16", src:"Course notes 2.5, Exercise 16", lec:"L13", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 97",
   q:"Data can be modelled by $y = A\\cos(B(t - C)) + D$, where $A$, $B$, $C$, $D$ are constants. A maximum point occurs at $(7, 23)$ and the preceding minimum point occurs at $(2, 7)$. Determine a cosine function that fits this data.",
   sol:[
    "Average: $D = \\frac{23 + 7}{2} = 15$. Amplitude: $A = 23 - 15 = 8$.",
    "From a minimum to the next maximum is half a cycle: $7 - 2 = 5$, so the period is $10$.",
    "Solve $10B = 2\\pi$: $B = \\frac{\\pi}{5}$.",
    "Cosine has a maximum when its input is 0, and the maximum is at $t = 7$, so take $C = 7$.",
    "Check: at $t = 2$, $y = 8\\cos(-\\pi) + 15 = 7$, the minimum."],
   ans:"$y = 8\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{5}(t - 7)\\right) + 15$"},
  {id:"CN2.5.2-XR17", src:"Course notes 2.5, Exercise 17", lec:"L13", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 98",
   q:"A 3 m long ladder rests against a vertical wall. Create a function which represents the distance that the bottom of the ladder is from the wall in terms of the angle between the ladder and the floor.",
   sol:[
    "The ladder is the hypotenuse, 3 m. Let $\\theta$ be the angle between the ladder and the floor and $x$ the distance from the bottom of the ladder to the wall.",
    "$x$ is adjacent to $\\theta$, so $\\cos(\\theta) = \\frac{x}{3}$."],
   ans:"$x(\\theta) = 3\\cos(\\theta)$"},
  {id:"CN2.5.3-EX13", src:"Course notes 2.5, Example 13", lec:"L14", kind:"example", page:"p. 99",
   q:"Evaluate (a) $\\arcsin\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}\\right)$ (b) $\\arcsin\\left(-\\frac12\\right)$.",
   sol:[
    "$\\arcsin(x) = \\theta$ means $\\sin(\\theta) = x$ with $-\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}$.",
    "(a) We want $\\theta \\in \\left[-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$ with $\\sin(\\theta) = \\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}$. Since $\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}$ and $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$ is in that interval, $\\arcsin\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}\\right) = \\frac{\\pi}{3}$.",
    "(b) We want $\\theta \\in \\left[-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$ with $\\sin(\\theta) = -\\frac12$. Since $\\sin\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) = -\\frac12$, $\\arcsin\\left(-\\frac12\\right) = -\\frac{\\pi}{6}$."],
   ans:"(a) $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$; (b) $-\\frac{\\pi}{6}$"},
  {id:"CN2.5.3-XR18", src:"Course notes 2.5, Exercise 18", lec:"L14", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 99",
   q:"Evaluate (a) $\\arcsin\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}\\right)$ (b) $\\arcsin(1)$ (c) $\\arcsin(2)$.",
   sol:[
    "$\\arcsin(x) = \\theta$ means $\\sin(\\theta) = x$ with $-\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}$.",
    "(a) $\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}$ and $\\frac{\\pi}{4}$ is in the range of arcsine, so $\\arcsin\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}\\right) = \\frac{\\pi}{4}$.",
    "(b) $\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = 1$ and $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ is in the range of arcsine, so $\\arcsin(1) = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$.",
    "(c) The domain of arcsine is $[-1, 1]$ and $2$ is not in it (no angle has $\\sin(\\theta) = 2$), so $\\arcsin(2)$ does not exist."],
   ans:"(a) $\\frac{\\pi}{4}$; (b) $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$; (c) does not exist"},
  {id:"CN2.5.3-EX14", src:"Course notes 2.5, Example 14", lec:"L14", kind:"example", page:"p. 99",
   q:"Simplify $\\sin\\left(\\arcsin\\left(\\frac13\\right)\\right)$.",
   sol:[
    "Let $\\theta = \\arcsin\\left(\\frac13\\right)$. We want $\\sin(\\theta)$.",
    "By the definition of inverse functions, $\\theta = \\arcsin\\left(\\frac13\\right) \\iff \\sin(\\theta) = \\frac13$ (and $\\frac13$ is in $[-1,1]$, so $\\theta$ exists).",
    "So $\\sin\\left(\\arcsin\\left(\\frac13\\right)\\right) = \\sin(\\theta) = \\frac13$."],
   ans:"$\\frac13$"},
  {id:"CN2.5.3-EX15", src:"Course notes 2.5, Example 15", lec:"L14", kind:"example", page:"p. 100",
   q:"Evaluate $\\arcsin\\left(\\sin\\left(\\frac{11\\pi}{5}\\right)\\right)$.",
   sol:[
    "The range of arcsine is $\\left[-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$, so the answer cannot be $\\frac{11\\pi}{5}$. The two functions do not cancel here.",
    "$\\sin$ has period $2\\pi$: $\\sin\\left(\\frac{11\\pi}{5}\\right) = \\sin\\left(2\\pi + \\frac{\\pi}{5}\\right) = \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{5}\\right)$.",
    "So $\\arcsin\\left(\\sin\\left(\\frac{11\\pi}{5}\\right)\\right) = \\arcsin\\left(\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{5}\\right)\\right)$.",
    "Let $y = \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{5}\\right)$. Since $\\frac{\\pi}{5} \\in \\left[-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$, the definition gives $\\arcsin(y) = \\frac{\\pi}{5}$."],
   ans:"$\\frac{\\pi}{5}$"},
  {id:"CN2.5.3-XR19", src:"Course notes 2.5, Exercise 19", lec:"L14", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 100",
   q:"What is the domain and range of $\\arcsin(\\sin(x))$? Sketch the graph of $f(x) = \\arcsin(\\sin(x))$.",
   sol:[
    "Domain: $\\sin(x)$ is defined for every real $x$ and always lies in $[-1, 1]$, the domain of arcsine. So the domain is $\\mathbb{R}$.",
    "Range: arcsine only outputs values in $\\left[-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$, and every such value is reached (take $x$ in that interval). So the range is $\\left[-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$.",
    "Graph: on $\\left[-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$ the functions cancel, so $f(x) = x$. On $\\left[\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{3\\pi}{2}\\right]$, $\\sin(x) = \\sin(\\pi - x)$ with $\\pi - x$ in the principal interval, so $f(x) = \\pi - x$. The graph is a triangle (zigzag) wave with period $2\\pi$: up with slope 1 from $\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, -\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$ to $\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$, down with slope $-1$ through $(\\pi, 0)$ to $\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{2}, -\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$, and repeating."],
   ans:"Domain $\\mathbb{R}$, range $\\left[-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$; the graph is a triangle wave of period $2\\pi$"},
  {id:"CN2.5.3-XR20", src:"Course notes 2.5, Exercise 20", lec:"L14", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 100",
   q:"What is the domain and range of $\\sin(\\arcsin(x))$? Sketch the graph of $f(x) = \\sin(\\arcsin(x))$.",
   sol:[
    "Domain: arcsine only accepts inputs in $[-1, 1]$, so the domain is $[-1, 1]$.",
    "Range: for $x \\in [-1,1]$, $\\arcsin(x)$ takes every angle in $\\left[-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$, and $\\sin$ on that interval takes every value in $[-1, 1]$. So the range is $[-1, 1]$.",
    "In fact $\\sin(\\arcsin(x)) = x$ for every $x \\in [-1,1]$, so the graph is the line segment $y = x$ from $(-1, -1)$ to $(1, 1)$."],
   ans:"Domain $[-1, 1]$, range $[-1, 1]$; the graph is the segment $y = x$ from $(-1,-1)$ to $(1,1)$"},
  {id:"CN2.5.3-EX16", src:"Course notes 2.5, Example 16", lec:"L14", kind:"example", page:"p. 101",
   q:"Evaluate (a) $\\arccos\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}\\right)$ (b) $\\arccos(1)$.",
   sol:[
    "$\\arccos(x) = \\theta$ means $\\cos(\\theta) = x$ with $0 \\le \\theta \\le \\pi$.",
    "(a) We want $\\theta \\in [0, \\pi]$ with $\\cos(\\theta) = -\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}$. Since $\\cos\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{6}\\right) = -\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}$, $\\arccos\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}\\right) = \\frac{5\\pi}{6}$.",
    "(b) We want $\\theta \\in [0, \\pi]$ with $\\cos(\\theta) = 1$. Since $\\cos(0) = 1$, $\\arccos(1) = 0$."],
   ans:"(a) $\\frac{5\\pi}{6}$; (b) $0$"},
  {id:"CN2.5.3-XR21", src:"Course notes 2.5, Exercise 21", lec:"L14", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 102",
   q:"Evaluate (a) $\\arccos\\left(\\frac12\\right)$ (b) $\\arccos(-1)$.",
   sol:[
    "$\\arccos(x) = \\theta$ means $\\cos(\\theta) = x$ with $0 \\le \\theta \\le \\pi$.",
    "(a) $\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\frac12$ and $\\frac{\\pi}{3} \\in [0, \\pi]$, so $\\arccos\\left(\\frac12\\right) = \\frac{\\pi}{3}$.",
    "(b) $\\cos(\\pi) = -1$ and $\\pi \\in [0, \\pi]$, so $\\arccos(-1) = \\pi$."],
   ans:"(a) $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$; (b) $\\pi$"},
  {id:"CN2.5.3-EX17", src:"Course notes 2.5, Example 17", lec:"L14", kind:"example", page:"p. 102",
   q:"Evaluate $\\arccos\\left(\\cos\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{7}\\right)\\right)$.",
   sol:[
    "$-\\frac{\\pi}{7}$ is not in $[0, \\pi]$, the range of arccosine, so the functions do not cancel directly.",
    "Cosine is even: $\\cos\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{7}\\right) = \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{7}\\right)$. So $\\arccos\\left(\\cos\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{7}\\right)\\right) = \\arccos\\left(\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{7}\\right)\\right)$.",
    "Let $y = \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{7}\\right)$. Since $\\frac{\\pi}{7} \\in [0, \\pi]$, the definition gives $\\arccos(y) = \\frac{\\pi}{7}$."],
   ans:"$\\frac{\\pi}{7}$"},
  {id:"CN2.5.3-XR22", src:"Course notes 2.5, Exercise 22", lec:"L14", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 102",
   q:"What is the domain and range of $\\arccos(\\cos(x))$? Sketch the graph of $f(x) = \\arccos(\\cos(x))$.",
   sol:[
    "Domain: $\\cos(x)$ is defined for all real $x$ and lies in $[-1,1]$, so the domain is $\\mathbb{R}$.",
    "Range: arccosine outputs exactly the values in $[0, \\pi]$, all reached for $x \\in [0,\\pi]$. So the range is $[0, \\pi]$.",
    "Graph: on $[0, \\pi]$, $f(x) = x$. On $[-\\pi, 0]$, $\\cos(x) = \\cos(-x)$ with $-x \\in [0,\\pi]$, so $f(x) = -x$. So $f(x) = |x|$ on $[-\\pi, \\pi]$, and the pattern repeats with period $2\\pi$: a triangle wave with minimums $0$ at $x = 2k\\pi$ and maximums $\\pi$ at $x = \\pi + 2k\\pi$."],
   ans:"Domain $\\mathbb{R}$, range $[0, \\pi]$; the graph is the triangle wave equal to $|x|$ on $[-\\pi, \\pi]$, period $2\\pi$"},
  {id:"CN2.5.3-XR23", src:"Course notes 2.5, Exercise 23", lec:"L14", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 102",
   q:"What is the domain and range of $\\cos(\\arccos(x))$? Sketch the graph of $f(x) = \\cos(\\arccos(x))$.",
   sol:[
    "Domain: arccosine only accepts inputs in $[-1, 1]$, so the domain is $[-1, 1]$.",
    "Range: $\\arccos(x)$ takes every angle in $[0, \\pi]$, and $\\cos$ on $[0, \\pi]$ takes every value in $[-1, 1]$. So the range is $[-1, 1]$.",
    "$\\cos(\\arccos(x)) = x$ for $x \\in [-1, 1]$, so the graph is the segment $y = x$ from $(-1, -1)$ to $(1, 1)$."],
   ans:"Domain $[-1, 1]$, range $[-1, 1]$; the graph is the segment $y = x$ from $(-1,-1)$ to $(1,1)$"},
  {id:"CN2.5.3-EX18", src:"Course notes 2.5, Example 18", lec:"L14", kind:"example", page:"p. 103",
   q:"Evaluate (a) $\\arctan(1)$ (b) $\\arctan(-\\sqrt3)$.",
   sol:[
    "$\\arctan(x) = \\theta$ means $\\tan(\\theta) = x$ with $-\\frac{\\pi}{2} < \\theta < \\frac{\\pi}{2}$.",
    "(a) We want $\\theta \\in \\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$ with $\\tan(\\theta) = 1$. Since $\\tan\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = 1$, $\\arctan(1) = \\frac{\\pi}{4}$.",
    "(b) We want $\\theta \\in \\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$ with $\\tan(\\theta) = -\\sqrt3$. Since $\\tan\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\sqrt3$, $\\arctan(-\\sqrt3) = -\\frac{\\pi}{3}$."],
   ans:"(a) $\\frac{\\pi}{4}$; (b) $-\\frac{\\pi}{3}$"},
  {id:"CN2.5.3-XR24", src:"Course notes 2.5, Exercise 24", lec:"L14", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 104",
   q:"Evaluate (a) $\\arctan(0)$ (b) $\\arctan\\left(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt3}\\right)$.",
   sol:[
    "$\\arctan(x) = \\theta$ means $\\tan(\\theta) = x$ with $-\\frac{\\pi}{2} < \\theta < \\frac{\\pi}{2}$.",
    "(a) $\\tan(0) = 0$ and $0$ is in the range of arctangent, so $\\arctan(0) = 0$.",
    "(b) $\\tan\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) = -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt3}$ and $-\\frac{\\pi}{6}$ is in the range of arctangent, so $\\arctan\\left(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt3}\\right) = -\\frac{\\pi}{6}$."],
   ans:"(a) $0$; (b) $-\\frac{\\pi}{6}$"},
  {id:"CN2.5.3-XR25", src:"Course notes 2.5, Exercise 25", lec:"L14", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 104",
   q:"What is the domain and range of $\\arctan(\\tan(x))$?",
   sol:[
    "Domain: the inner function $\\tan(x)$ must be defined, so $x \\ne \\frac{\\pi}{2} + k\\pi$ for any integer $k$. Every value of $\\tan(x)$ is a real number, which arctangent accepts.",
    "Range: arctangent outputs values in $\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$, and every one is reached (take $x$ in that interval, where the functions cancel)."],
   ans:"Domain: all real $x$ with $x \\ne \\frac{\\pi}{2} + k\\pi$, $k$ an integer. Range: $\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$."},
  {id:"CN2.5.3-XR26", src:"Course notes 2.5, Exercise 26", lec:"L14", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 104",
   q:"What is the domain and range of $\\tan(\\arctan(x))$?",
   sol:[
    "Domain: arctangent accepts every real number, and its outputs lie in $\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$, where $\\tan$ is defined. So the domain is $(-\\infty, \\infty)$.",
    "Range: $\\tan(\\arctan(x)) = x$, so every real number is an output. The range is $(-\\infty, \\infty)$."],
   ans:"Domain $(-\\infty, \\infty)$, range $(-\\infty, \\infty)$"},
  {id:"CN2.5.3-EX19", src:"Course notes 2.5, Example 19", lec:"L14", kind:"example", page:"p. 104",
   q:"Evaluate $\\sin(\\arctan(7))$.",
   sol:[
    "Let $\\theta = \\arctan(7)$. We want $\\sin(\\theta)$.",
    "By the definition of inverse functions, $\\tan(\\theta) = 7 = \\frac71 = \\frac{o}{a}$ with $-\\frac{\\pi}{2} < \\theta < \\frac{\\pi}{2}$. Since $\\tan(\\theta) > 0$, $\\theta$ is in the first quadrant: $o = 7$, $a = 1$.",
    "$h^2 = o^2 + a^2 = 49 + 1 = 50$, so $h = \\sqrt{50}$ since $h > 0$.",
    "$\\sin(\\arctan(7)) = \\sin(\\theta) = \\frac{o}{h} = \\frac{7}{\\sqrt{50}}$."],
   ans:"$\\frac{7}{\\sqrt{50}} = \\frac{7}{5\\sqrt2}$"},
  {id:"CN2.5.3-EX20", src:"Course notes 2.5, Example 20", lec:"L14", kind:"example", page:"p. 105",
   q:"Evaluate $\\cos\\left(\\arctan\\left(\\frac13\\right)\\right)$.",
   sol:[
    "Let $\\theta = \\arctan\\left(\\frac13\\right)$. We want $\\cos(\\theta)$.",
    "By definition, $\\tan(\\theta) = \\frac13 = \\frac{o}{a}$ with $\\theta$ in the first quadrant (since $\\tan(\\theta) > 0$ and $-\\frac{\\pi}{2} < \\theta < \\frac{\\pi}{2}$): $o = 1$, $a = 3$.",
    "$h^2 = 1^2 + 3^2 = 10$, so $h = \\sqrt{10}$ since $h > 0$.",
    "$\\cos\\left(\\arctan\\left(\\frac13\\right)\\right) = \\cos(\\theta) = \\frac{a}{h} = \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}$."],
   ans:"$\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}$"},
  {id:"CN2.5.3-EX21", src:"Course notes 2.5, Example 21", lec:"L14", kind:"example", page:"p. 105",
   q:"Evaluate $\\sin\\left(\\arccos\\left(-\\frac14\\right)\\right)$.",
   sol:[
    "Let $\\theta = \\arccos\\left(-\\frac14\\right)$. We want $\\sin(\\theta)$.",
    "By definition, $\\cos(\\theta) = -\\frac14 = \\frac{a}{h}$. The hypotenuse is always positive, so $h = 4$ and $a = -1$.",
    "$h^2 = a^2 + o^2 \\Rightarrow 16 = 1 + o^2 \\Rightarrow o^2 = 15 \\Rightarrow |o| = \\sqrt{15}$.",
    "Sign of $o$: the range of arccosine is $[0, \\pi]$, and here $\\cos(\\theta) < 0$, so $\\frac{\\pi}{2} < \\theta < \\pi$, where $\\sin(\\theta) > 0$. So $o = \\sqrt{15}$.",
    "$\\sin\\left(\\arccos\\left(-\\frac14\\right)\\right) = \\frac{o}{h} = \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}$."],
   ans:"$\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}$"},
  {id:"CN2.5.3-XR27", src:"Course notes 2.5, Exercise 27", lec:"L14", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 106",
   q:"Evaluate $\\tan\\left(\\arcsin\\left(\\frac12\\right)\\right)$.",
   sol:[
    "Let $\\theta = \\arcsin\\left(\\frac12\\right)$. Then $\\sin(\\theta) = \\frac12$ with $-\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}$, so $\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{6}$.",
    "$\\tan\\left(\\arcsin\\left(\\frac12\\right)\\right) = \\tan\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt3}$."],
   ans:"$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt3} = \\frac{\\sqrt3}{3}$"},
  {id:"CN2.5.3-XR28", src:"Course notes 2.5, Exercise 28", lec:"L14", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 106",
   q:"Evaluate $\\cos\\left(\\arcsin\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt3}\\right)\\right)$.",
   sol:[
    "Let $\\theta = \\arcsin\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt3}\\right)$. Then $\\sin(\\theta) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt3} = \\frac{o}{h}$. This is not a standard angle, so draw a triangle: $o = 1$, $h = \\sqrt3$.",
    "$h^2 = o^2 + a^2 \\Rightarrow 3 = 1 + a^2 \\Rightarrow a^2 = 2 \\Rightarrow |a| = \\sqrt2$.",
    "Arcsine outputs angles in $\\left[-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$ and $\\sin(\\theta) > 0$, so $0 < \\theta < \\frac{\\pi}{2}$, where cosine is positive. So $a = \\sqrt2$.",
    "$\\cos(\\theta) = \\frac{a}{h} = \\frac{\\sqrt2}{\\sqrt3}$."],
   ans:"$\\frac{\\sqrt2}{\\sqrt3} = \\sqrt{\\frac23}$"},
  {id:"CN2.5.4-EX22", src:"Course notes 2.5, Example 22", lec:"L15", kind:"example", page:"p. 106",
   q:"Determine the derivative of (a) $f(x) = \\tan(x)\\sec(x)$ (b) $g(t) = \\csc(e^t)$ (c) $h(y) = \\ln(|\\sec(y)|)$.",
   sol:[
    "(a) Product rule with $\\frac{d}{dx}\\tan(x) = \\sec^2(x)$ and $\\frac{d}{dx}\\sec(x) = \\sec(x)\\tan(x)$:",
    "$f'(x) = \\sec^2(x)\\sec(x) + \\tan(x)\\sec(x)\\tan(x) = \\sec^3(x) + \\tan^2(x)\\sec(x)$.",
    "(b) Chain rule with $\\frac{d}{du}\\csc(u) = -\\csc(u)\\cot(u)$ and $u = e^t$: $g'(t) = -\\csc(e^t)\\cot(e^t)\\,e^t$.",
    "(c) Chain rule with $\\frac{d}{du}\\ln(|u|) = \\frac{1}{|u|}\\cdot\\frac{u}{|u|}\\cdot u' = \\frac{u'}{u}$ and $u = \\sec(y)$:",
    "$h'(y) = \\frac{\\sec(y)\\tan(y)}{\\sec(y)} = \\tan(y)$."],
   ans:"(a) $f'(x) = \\sec^3(x) + \\tan^2(x)\\sec(x)$; (b) $g'(t) = -e^t\\csc(e^t)\\cot(e^t)$; (c) $h'(y) = \\tan(y)$"},
  {id:"CN2.5.4-XR29", src:"Course notes 2.5, Exercise 29", lec:"L15", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 107",
   q:"Determine the derivative of (a) $f(x) = \\cot(3x) + 2^x$ (b) $g(t) = \\tan(\\sqrt{t+1})$ (c) $h(x) = \\ln(|\\sec(x) + \\tan(x)|)$.",
   sol:[
    "(a) $\\frac{d}{dx}\\cot(3x) = -\\csc^2(3x)\\cdot 3$ and $\\frac{d}{dx}2^x = 2^x\\ln(2)$, so $f'(x) = -3\\csc^2(3x) + 2^x\\ln(2)$.",
    "(b) Chain rule twice: $g'(t) = \\sec^2\\left((t+1)^{1/2}\\right)\\cdot\\frac12(t+1)^{-1/2} = \\frac{\\sec^2(\\sqrt{t+1})}{2\\sqrt{t+1}}$.",
    "(c) With $u = \\sec(x) + \\tan(x)$, $\\frac{d}{dx}\\ln(|u|) = \\frac{u'}{u}$ and $u' = \\sec(x)\\tan(x) + \\sec^2(x) = \\sec(x)(\\tan(x) + \\sec(x))$.",
    "$h'(x) = \\frac{\\sec(x)(\\tan(x) + \\sec(x))}{\\sec(x) + \\tan(x)} = \\sec(x)$."],
   ans:"(a) $-3\\csc^2(3x) + 2^x\\ln(2)$; (b) $\\frac{\\sec^2(\\sqrt{t+1})}{2\\sqrt{t+1}}$; (c) $\\sec(x)$"},
  {id:"CN2.5.4-EX23", src:"Course notes 2.5, Example 23", lec:"L15", kind:"example", page:"p. 107",
   q:"Determine the derivative of (a) $f(x) = x\\arcsin(x+1)$ (b) $g(x) = \\arctan(\\ln(x))$ (c) $h(x) = x\\arcsin(x) + \\sqrt{1-x^2}$.",
   sol:[
    "(a) Product rule with $\\frac{d}{dx}\\arcsin(u) = \\frac{u'}{\\sqrt{1-u^2}}$:",
    "$f'(x) = 1\\cdot\\arcsin(x+1) + x\\cdot\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x+1)^2}} = \\arcsin(x+1) + \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{-x^2-2x}}$.",
    "(b) Chain rule with $\\frac{d}{du}\\arctan(u) = \\frac{1}{1+u^2}$, $u = \\ln(x)$: $g'(x) = \\frac{1}{1+(\\ln(x))^2}\\cdot\\frac1x$.",
    "(c) $h'(x) = \\left(\\arcsin(x) + \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}\\right) + \\frac12(1-x^2)^{-1/2}(-2x) = \\arcsin(x) + \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}} - \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}} = \\arcsin(x)$."],
   ans:"(a) $\\arcsin(x+1) + \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{-x^2-2x}}$; (b) $\\frac{1}{x(1+(\\ln(x))^2)}$; (c) $\\arcsin(x)$"},
  {id:"CN2.5.4-XR30", src:"Course notes 2.5, Exercise 30", lec:"L15", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 108",
   q:"Determine the derivative of (a) $f(x) = e^x\\arctan(x)$ (b) $g(x) = \\arcsin(x^2+1)$ (c) $h(x) = x\\arccos(x) - \\sqrt{1-x^2}$.",
   sol:[
    "(a) Product rule: $f'(x) = e^x\\arctan(x) + e^x\\cdot\\frac{1}{1+x^2}$.",
    "(b) Chain rule with $u = x^2 + 1$, $u' = 2x$: $g'(x) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x^2+1)^2}}\\cdot 2x$.",
    "Simplify the radicand: $1 - (x^2+1)^2 = 1 - (x^4 + 2x^2 + 1) = -x^4 - 2x^2$, so $g'(x) = \\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{-x^4-2x^2}}$.",
    "(c) $h'(x) = \\left[\\arccos(x) + x\\cdot\\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}\\right] - \\frac12(1-x^2)^{-1/2}(-2x) = \\arccos(x) - \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}} + \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}} = \\arccos(x)$."],
   ans:"(a) $e^x\\arctan(x) + \\frac{e^x}{1+x^2}$; (b) $\\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{1-(x^2+1)^2}} = \\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{-x^4-2x^2}}$; (c) $\\arccos(x)$",
   note:"(b) The official solution prints $\\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{1-x^4}}$, which is the derivative of $\\arcsin(x^2)$, not $\\arcsin(x^2+1)$. The chain rule answer above was checked with sympy. Also note that $x^2 + 1 \\ge 1$, with equality only at $x = 0$, so $\\arcsin(x^2+1)$ is only defined at $x = 0$ and the derivative formula is not defined for any $x$ (the radicand $-x^4-2x^2$ is $0$ at $x = 0$ and negative elsewhere). The item is kept because the question asks for the chain rule computation."},
  {id:"CN2.5.4-EX24", src:"Course notes 2.5, Example 24", lec:"L15", kind:"example", page:"p. 108",
   q:"Find all solutions of the differential equation $y' = \\sec^2(x)$.",
   sol:[
    "The solutions are the antiderivatives of $\\sec^2(x)$: $y = \\int\\sec^2(x)\\,dx$.",
    "Since $\\frac{d}{dx}\\tan(x) = \\sec^2(x)$, $y = \\tan(x) + C$, where $C$ is any constant."],
   ans:"$y = \\tan(x) + C$"},
  {id:"CN2.5.4-XR31", src:"Course notes 2.5, Exercise 31", lec:"L15", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 108",
   q:"Solve the initial value problem $y' = \\sec(x)\\tan(x)$, $y(0) = \\frac{2\\pi}{3}$.",
   sol:[
    "All solutions: $y = \\int\\sec(x)\\tan(x)\\,dx = \\sec(x) + C$.",
    "Use $y(0) = \\frac{2\\pi}{3}$: $\\frac{2\\pi}{3} = \\sec(0) + C = 1 + C$, so $C = \\frac{2\\pi}{3} - 1$."],
   ans:"$y = \\sec(x) + \\frac{2\\pi}{3} - 1$"},
  {id:"CN2.5-P1-a-d", src:"Course notes 2.5, End of Section Problem 1, parts (a) to (d)", lec:"L12", kind:"problem", page:"p. 109",
   q:"Write all angles $\\theta$ with $-2\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi$ such that<br>(a) $\\cos(\\theta) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}$<br>(b) $\\sin(\\theta) = \\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}$<br>(c) $\\tan(\\theta) = \\sqrt3$<br>(d) $\\sec(\\theta) = \\frac{2}{\\sqrt3}$",
   sol:[
    "(a) $\\cos > 0$ in quadrants 1 and 4, reference angle $\\frac{\\pi}{4}$. In $[0, 2\\pi]$: $\\frac{\\pi}{4}, \\frac{7\\pi}{4}$. Subtract $2\\pi$ for $[-2\\pi, 0]$: $-\\frac{7\\pi}{4}, -\\frac{\\pi}{4}$.",
    "(b) $\\sin > 0$ in quadrants 1 and 2, reference angle $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$. In $[0, 2\\pi]$: $\\frac{\\pi}{3}, \\frac{2\\pi}{3}$. Subtract $2\\pi$: $-\\frac{5\\pi}{3}, -\\frac{4\\pi}{3}$.",
    "(c) $\\tan > 0$ in quadrants 1 and 3, reference angle $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$. Solutions $\\frac{\\pi}{3} + k\\pi$ in $[-2\\pi, 2\\pi]$: $-\\frac{5\\pi}{3}, -\\frac{2\\pi}{3}, \\frac{\\pi}{3}, \\frac{4\\pi}{3}$.",
    "(d) $\\sec(\\theta) = \\frac{2}{\\sqrt3}$ means $\\cos(\\theta) = \\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}$. Quadrants 1 and 4, reference angle $\\frac{\\pi}{6}$: $\\frac{\\pi}{6}, \\frac{11\\pi}{6}$, and subtracting $2\\pi$: $-\\frac{11\\pi}{6}, -\\frac{\\pi}{6}$."],
   ans:"(a) $\\theta = -\\frac{7\\pi}{4}, -\\frac{\\pi}{4}, \\frac{\\pi}{4}, \\frac{7\\pi}{4}$; (b) $\\theta = -\\frac{5\\pi}{3}, -\\frac{4\\pi}{3}, \\frac{\\pi}{3}, \\frac{2\\pi}{3}$; (c) $\\theta = -\\frac{5\\pi}{3}, -\\frac{2\\pi}{3}, \\frac{\\pi}{3}, \\frac{4\\pi}{3}$; (d) $\\theta = -\\frac{11\\pi}{6}, -\\frac{\\pi}{6}, \\frac{\\pi}{6}, \\frac{11\\pi}{6}$"},
  {id:"CN2.5-P1-e-h", src:"Course notes 2.5, End of Section Problem 1, parts (e) to (h)", lec:"L12", kind:"problem", page:"p. 109",
   q:"Write all angles $\\theta$ with $-2\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi$ such that<br>(e) $\\cot(\\theta) = \\sqrt3$<br>(f) $\\sin(\\theta) = 1$<br>(g) $\\cos(\\theta) = -1$<br>(h) $\\cot(\\theta) = -1$",
   sol:[
    "(e) $\\cot(\\theta) = \\sqrt3$ means $\\tan(\\theta) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt3}$. Reference angle $\\frac{\\pi}{6}$, solutions $\\frac{\\pi}{6} + k\\pi$: $-\\frac{11\\pi}{6}, -\\frac{5\\pi}{6}, \\frac{\\pi}{6}, \\frac{7\\pi}{6}$.",
    "(f) $\\sin(\\theta) = 1$ only at the top of the unit circle: $\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{2} + 2k\\pi$. In $[-2\\pi, 2\\pi]$: $-\\frac{3\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}$.",
    "(g) $\\cos(\\theta) = -1$ only at the point $(-1, 0)$: $\\theta = \\pi + 2k\\pi$. In $[-2\\pi, 2\\pi]$: $-\\pi, \\pi$.",
    "(h) $\\cot(\\theta) = -1$ means $\\tan(\\theta) = -1$. $\\tan < 0$ in quadrants 2 and 4, reference angle $\\frac{\\pi}{4}$. Solutions $\\frac{3\\pi}{4} + k\\pi$: $-\\frac{5\\pi}{4}, -\\frac{\\pi}{4}, \\frac{3\\pi}{4}, \\frac{7\\pi}{4}$."],
   ans:"(e) $\\theta = -\\frac{11\\pi}{6}, -\\frac{5\\pi}{6}, \\frac{\\pi}{6}, \\frac{7\\pi}{6}$; (f) $\\theta = -\\frac{3\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}$; (g) $\\theta = -\\pi, \\pi$; (h) $\\theta = -\\frac{5\\pi}{4}, -\\frac{\\pi}{4}, \\frac{3\\pi}{4}, \\frac{7\\pi}{4}$"},
  {id:"CN2.5-P1-i-j", src:"Course notes 2.5, End of Section Problem 1, parts (i) to (j)", lec:"L12", kind:"problem", page:"p. 109",
   q:"Write all angles $\\theta$ with $-2\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi$ such that<br>(i) $\\csc(\\theta) = \\sqrt2$<br>(j) $\\sec(\\theta) = 0$",
   sol:[
    "(i) $\\csc(\\theta) = \\sqrt2$ means $\\sin(\\theta) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt2} = \\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}$. Quadrants 1 and 2, reference angle $\\frac{\\pi}{4}$: $\\frac{\\pi}{4}, \\frac{3\\pi}{4}$, and subtracting $2\\pi$: $-\\frac{7\\pi}{4}, -\\frac{5\\pi}{4}$.",
    "(j) $\\sec(\\theta) = \\frac{1}{\\cos(\\theta)}$ is never 0 (a fraction with numerator 1 cannot be 0). The range of secant is $(-\\infty, -1] \\cup [1, \\infty)$."],
   ans:"(i) $\\theta = -\\frac{7\\pi}{4}, -\\frac{5\\pi}{4}, \\frac{\\pi}{4}, \\frac{3\\pi}{4}$; (j) No solutions"},
  {id:"CN2.5-P2", src:"Course notes 2.5, End of Section Problem 2", lec:"L12", kind:"problem", page:"p. 109",
   q:"Given that $\\cos(\\theta) = \\frac23$ and $0 < \\theta < \\frac{\\pi}{2}$, find $\\sin(\\theta)$.",
   sol:[
    "$\\cos(\\theta) = \\frac{a}{h} = \\frac23$: adjacent $a = 2$, hypotenuse $h = 3$.",
    "$o^2 + a^2 = h^2 \\Rightarrow o^2 + 4 = 9 \\Rightarrow o^2 = 5 \\Rightarrow |o| = \\sqrt5$, and $o = \\sqrt5$ in the first quadrant.",
    "$\\sin(\\theta) = \\frac{o}{h} = \\frac{\\sqrt5}{3}$."],
   ans:"$\\sin(\\theta) = \\frac{\\sqrt5}{3}$"},
  {id:"CN2.5-P3", src:"Course notes 2.5, End of Section Problem 3", lec:"L12", kind:"problem", page:"p. 109",
   q:"Given that $\\sin(\\theta) = \\frac15$ and $0 < \\theta < \\frac{\\pi}{2}$, find $\\tan(\\theta)$.",
   sol:[
    "$\\sin(\\theta) = \\frac{o}{h} = \\frac15$: $o = 1$, $h = 5$.",
    "$o^2 + a^2 = h^2 \\Rightarrow 1 + a^2 = 25 \\Rightarrow a^2 = 24 \\Rightarrow |a| = \\sqrt{24}$, and $a = \\sqrt{24}$ in the first quadrant.",
    "$\\tan(\\theta) = \\frac{o}{a} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{24}}$."],
   ans:"$\\tan(\\theta) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{24}} = \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt6}$"},
  {id:"CN2.5-P4", src:"Course notes 2.5, End of Section Problem 4", lec:"L12", kind:"problem", page:"p. 109",
   q:"Given that $\\tan(\\theta) = -4$ and $\\frac{\\pi}{2} < \\theta < \\pi$, find $\\cos(\\theta)$.",
   sol:[
    "In the second quadrant the opposite side is positive and the adjacent side is negative: $\\tan(\\theta) = \\frac{4}{-1}$, so $o = 4$, $a = -1$.",
    "$h^2 = (-1)^2 + 4^2 = 17$, so $h = \\sqrt{17}$ since the hypotenuse is always positive.",
    "$\\cos(\\theta) = \\frac{a}{h} = \\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{17}}$."],
   ans:"$\\cos(\\theta) = -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}$"},
  {id:"CN2.5-P5", src:"Course notes 2.5, End of Section Problem 5", lec:"L12", kind:"problem", page:"p. 109",
   q:"Given that $\\sin(\\theta) = -\\frac14$ and $\\pi < \\theta < \\frac{3\\pi}{2}$, find $\\tan(\\theta)$.",
   sol:[
    "$\\sin(\\theta) = \\frac{o}{h} = \\frac{-1}{4}$: $o = -1$, $h = 4$.",
    "$o^2 + a^2 = h^2 \\Rightarrow 1 + a^2 = 16 \\Rightarrow a^2 = 15 \\Rightarrow |a| = \\sqrt{15}$. In the third quadrant the adjacent side is negative, so $a = -\\sqrt{15}$.",
    "$\\tan(\\theta) = \\frac{o}{a} = \\frac{-1}{-\\sqrt{15}} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{15}}$."],
   ans:"$\\tan(\\theta) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{15}}$"},
  {id:"CN2.5-P6", src:"Course notes 2.5, End of Section Problem 6", lec:"L12", kind:"problem", page:"p. 109",
   q:"Given that $\\cos(\\theta) = \\frac35$ and $\\frac{3\\pi}{2} < \\theta < 2\\pi$, find $\\sin(\\theta)$.",
   sol:[
    "$\\cos(\\theta) = \\frac{a}{h} = \\frac35$: $a = 3$, $h = 5$.",
    "$a^2 + o^2 = h^2 \\Rightarrow 9 + o^2 = 25 \\Rightarrow o^2 = 16 \\Rightarrow |o| = 4$. In the fourth quadrant the opposite side is negative, so $o = -4$.",
    "$\\sin(\\theta) = \\frac{o}{h} = \\frac{-4}{5}$."],
   ans:"$\\sin(\\theta) = -\\frac45$"},
  {id:"CN2.5-P7", src:"Course notes 2.5, End of Section Problem 7", lec:"L12", kind:"problem", page:"p. 109",
   q:"Given that $\\cos(\\theta) = \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x^2+1}}$ and $0 < \\theta < \\pi$, find $\\tan(\\theta)$.",
   sol:[
    "$\\cos(\\theta) = \\frac{a}{h}$: adjacent $a = x$, hypotenuse $h = \\sqrt{x^2+1}$.",
    "$o^2 + a^2 = h^2 \\Rightarrow o^2 + x^2 = x^2 + 1 \\Rightarrow o^2 = 1 \\Rightarrow |o| = 1$.",
    "For $0 < \\theta < \\pi$ (quadrants 1 and 2) the opposite side is positive, so $o = 1$.",
    "$\\tan(\\theta) = \\frac{o}{a} = \\frac{1}{x}$."],
   ans:"$\\tan(\\theta) = \\frac1x$"},
  {id:"CN2.5-P8", src:"Course notes 2.5, End of Section Problem 8", lec:"L12", kind:"problem", page:"p. 109",
   q:"Given that $\\sin(\\theta) = \\frac{3}{x}$ and $0 < \\theta < \\frac{\\pi}{2}$, find $\\cot(\\theta)$.",
   sol:[
    "$\\sin(\\theta) = \\frac{o}{h}$: $o = 3$, $h = x$.",
    "$o^2 + a^2 = h^2 \\Rightarrow 9 + a^2 = x^2 \\Rightarrow a^2 = x^2 - 9 \\Rightarrow |a| = \\sqrt{x^2-9}$, and $a = \\sqrt{x^2-9}$ in the first quadrant.",
    "$\\cot(\\theta) = \\frac{a}{o} = \\frac{\\sqrt{x^2-9}}{3}$."],
   ans:"$\\cot(\\theta) = \\frac{\\sqrt{x^2-9}}{3}$"},
  {id:"CN2.5-P9", src:"Course notes 2.5, End of Section Problem 9", lec:"L12", kind:"problem", page:"p. 109",
   q:"Given that $\\tan(\\theta) = \\frac{x}{2}$ and $0 < \\theta < \\frac{\\pi}{2}$, find $\\csc(\\theta)$.",
   sol:[
    "$\\tan(\\theta) = \\frac{o}{a}$: $o = x$, $a = 2$.",
    "$h^2 = o^2 + a^2 = x^2 + 4$, so $h = \\sqrt{x^2+4}$ since the hypotenuse is always positive.",
    "$\\csc(\\theta) = \\frac{h}{o} = \\frac{\\sqrt{x^2+4}}{x}$."],
   ans:"$\\csc(\\theta) = \\frac{\\sqrt{4+x^2}}{x}$"},
  {id:"CN2.5-P10", src:"Course notes 2.5, End of Section Problem 10", lec:"L12", kind:"problem", page:"p. 109",
   q:"Given that $\\sec(\\theta) = -x$ and $\\pi < \\theta < \\frac{3\\pi}{2}$, find $\\sin(\\theta)$.",
   sol:[
    "$\\sec(\\theta) = \\frac{h}{a}$. The hypotenuse is always positive and in the third quadrant the adjacent side is negative, so write $\\sec(\\theta) = \\frac{x}{-1}$: $h = x$, $a = -1$. (This needs $x > 0$.)",
    "$o^2 + a^2 = h^2 \\Rightarrow o^2 + 1 = x^2 \\Rightarrow o^2 = x^2 - 1 \\Rightarrow |o| = \\sqrt{x^2-1}$.",
    "In the third quadrant the opposite side is negative, so $o = -\\sqrt{x^2-1}$.",
    "$\\sin(\\theta) = \\frac{o}{h} = -\\frac{\\sqrt{x^2-1}}{x}$."],
   ans:"$\\sin(\\theta) = -\\frac{\\sqrt{x^2-1}}{x}$"},
  {id:"CN2.5-P11", src:"Course notes 2.5, End of Section Problem 11", lec:"L12", kind:"problem", page:"p. 109",
   q:"Given that $\\sin(\\theta) = \\frac{2x}{3}$ and $-\\frac{\\pi}{2} < \\theta < \\frac{\\pi}{2}$, find $\\sec(\\theta)$ and $\\tan(\\theta)$.",
   sol:[
    "$\\sin(\\theta) = \\frac{o}{h}$: $o = 2x$, $h = 3$.",
    "$o^2 + a^2 = h^2 \\Rightarrow 4x^2 + a^2 = 9 \\Rightarrow a^2 = 9 - 4x^2 \\Rightarrow |a| = \\sqrt{9-4x^2}$.",
    "On $-\\frac{\\pi}{2} < \\theta < \\frac{\\pi}{2}$ (quadrants 1 and 4) cosine is positive, so $a = \\sqrt{9-4x^2}$.",
    "$\\sec(\\theta) = \\frac{h}{a} = \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{9-4x^2}}$ and $\\tan(\\theta) = \\frac{o}{a} = \\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{9-4x^2}}$."],
   ans:"$\\sec(\\theta) = \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{9-4x^2}}$, $\\tan(\\theta) = \\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{9-4x^2}}$"},
  {id:"CN2.5-P12", src:"Course notes 2.5, End of Section Problem 12", lec:"L12", kind:"problem", page:"p. 109",
   q:"Find all values of $x$ in the interval $[0, 2\\pi]$ that satisfy $\\sin(x)\\cos(x) = 0$.",
   sol:[
    "A product is 0 only when one of its factors is 0, so $\\sin(x) = 0$ or $\\cos(x) = 0$.",
    "$\\sin(x) = 0$ when $x = 0, \\pi, 2\\pi$. $\\cos(x) = 0$ when $x = \\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{3\\pi}{2}$."],
   ans:"$x = 0, \\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\pi, \\frac{3\\pi}{2}, 2\\pi$"},
  {id:"CN2.5-P13", src:"Course notes 2.5, End of Section Problem 13", lec:"L12", kind:"problem", page:"p. 109",
   q:"Find all values of $x$ in the interval $[0, 2\\pi]$ that satisfy $2x\\cos(x) + x = 0$.",
   sol:[
    "Factor: $x(2\\cos(x) + 1) = 0$, so $x = 0$ or $\\cos(x) = -\\frac12$.",
    "$\\cos(x) = -\\frac12$ in quadrants 2 and 3 with reference angle $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$: $x = \\frac{2\\pi}{3}, \\frac{4\\pi}{3}$."],
   ans:"$x = 0, \\frac{2\\pi}{3}, \\frac{4\\pi}{3}$"},
  {id:"CN2.5-P14", src:"Course notes 2.5, End of Section Problem 14", lec:"L12", kind:"problem", page:"p. 109",
   q:"Find all values of $x$ in the interval $[0, 2\\pi]$ that satisfy $\\sin^2(x) = \\frac14$.",
   sol:[
    "Difference of squares: $\\sin^2(x) - \\frac14 = \\left(\\sin(x) - \\frac12\\right)\\left(\\sin(x) + \\frac12\\right) = 0$.",
    "$\\sin(x) = \\frac12$ when $x = \\frac{\\pi}{6}, \\frac{5\\pi}{6}$. $\\sin(x) = -\\frac12$ when $x = \\frac{7\\pi}{6}, \\frac{11\\pi}{6}$."],
   ans:"$x = \\frac{\\pi}{6}, \\frac{5\\pi}{6}, \\frac{7\\pi}{6}, \\frac{11\\pi}{6}$"},
  {id:"CN2.5-P15", src:"Course notes 2.5, End of Section Problem 15", lec:"L12", kind:"problem", page:"p. 109",
   q:"Find all values of $x$ in the interval $[0, 2\\pi]$ that satisfy $2\\cos(x) + \\sin(2x) = 0$.",
   sol:[
    "Use $\\sin(2x) = 2\\sin(x)\\cos(x)$: $2\\cos(x) + 2\\sin(x)\\cos(x) = 0 \\Rightarrow 2\\cos(x)(1 + \\sin(x)) = 0$.",
    "$\\cos(x) = 0$ when $x = \\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{3\\pi}{2}$. $\\sin(x) = -1$ when $x = \\frac{3\\pi}{2}$."],
   ans:"$x = \\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{3\\pi}{2}$"},
  {id:"CN2.5-P16-a-d", src:"Course notes 2.5, End of Section Problem 16, parts (a) to (d)", lec:"L12", kind:"problem", page:"p. 109",
   q:"Sketch one period of the graph of each function, showing each step as in Example 7.<br>(a) $f(\\theta) = 2\\cos(\\theta) + 1$<br>(b) $f(\\theta) = \\sin(2\\theta) - 1$<br>(c) $f(\\theta) = \\tan\\left(\\frac{\\theta}{2}\\right)$<br>(d) $f(\\theta) = -2\\cos(2\\theta)$",
   sol:[
    "(a) Start with $\\cos(\\theta)$ on $[0, 2\\pi]$. Vertical stretch by 2: range $[-2, 2]$. Translate up 1: range $[-1, 3]$.",
    "Key points: $(0, 3)$, $\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}, 1\\right)$, $(\\pi, -1)$, $\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{2}, 1\\right)$, $(2\\pi, 3)$.",
    "(b) Start with $\\sin(\\theta)$. Horizontal compression by 2: period $\\pi$. Translate down 1: range $[-2, 0]$.",
    "Key points on $[0, \\pi]$: $(0, -1)$, $\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}, 0\\right)$, $\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}, -1\\right)$, $\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{4}, -2\\right)$, $(\\pi, -1)$.",
    "(c) Start with $\\tan(\\theta)$ on $\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$. Horizontal stretch by 2: one period on $(-\\pi, \\pi)$ with vertical asymptotes $\\theta = \\pm\\pi$.",
    "Key points: $\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, -1\\right)$, $(0, 0)$, $\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}, 1\\right)$; increasing.",
    "(d) Start with $\\cos(\\theta)$. Horizontal compression by 2: period $\\pi$. Reflect in the $\\theta$-axis and stretch vertically by 2: range $[-2, 2]$.",
    "Key points on $[0, \\pi]$: $(0, -2)$, $\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}, 0\\right)$, $\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}, 2\\right)$, $\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{4}, 0\\right)$, $(\\pi, -2)$."],
   ans:"(a) max 3 at $0$ and $2\\pi$, min $-1$ at $\\pi$, period $2\\pi$; (b) period $\\pi$, max 0 at $\\frac{\\pi}{4}$, min $-2$ at $\\frac{3\\pi}{4}$; (c) increasing branch through the origin on $(-\\pi, \\pi)$, asymptotes $\\theta = \\pm\\pi$; (d) period $\\pi$, min $-2$ at $0$ and $\\pi$, max $2$ at $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$"},
  {id:"CN2.5-P16-e-g", src:"Course notes 2.5, End of Section Problem 16, parts (e) to (g)", lec:"L14", kind:"problem", page:"p. 109",
   q:"Sketch one period of the graph of each function, showing each step as in Example 7.<br>(e) $f(\\theta) = \\arcsin(2\\theta)$<br>(f) $f(\\theta) = -\\arccos\\left(\\frac{\\theta}{2}\\right)$<br>(g) $f(\\theta) = 2\\arctan(\\theta)$",
   sol:[
    "(e) Start with $\\arcsin(\\theta)$: domain $[-1, 1]$, range $\\left[-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$. Horizontal compression by 2: domain $\\left[-\\frac12, \\frac12\\right]$, same range.",
    "Key points: $\\left(-\\frac12, -\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$, $(0, 0)$, $\\left(\\frac12, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$; increasing.",
    "(f) Start with $\\arccos(\\theta)$: from $(-1, \\pi)$ through $\\left(0, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$ to $(1, 0)$. Reflect in the horizontal axis: range $[-\\pi, 0]$. Horizontal stretch by 2: domain $[-2, 2]$.",
    "Key points: $(-2, -\\pi)$, $\\left(0, -\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$, $(2, 0)$; increasing.",
    "(g) Start with $\\arctan(\\theta)$: increasing through the origin with horizontal asymptotes $\\pm\\frac{\\pi}{2}$. Vertical stretch by 2: horizontal asymptotes $y = \\pm\\pi$.",
    "Key points: $\\left(-1, -\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$, $(0, 0)$, $\\left(1, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$."],
   ans:"(e) increasing from $\\left(-\\frac12, -\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$ through $(0,0)$ to $\\left(\\frac12, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$; (f) increasing from $(-2, -\\pi)$ through $\\left(0, -\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$ to $(2, 0)$; (g) increasing through the origin with horizontal asymptotes $y = \\pm\\pi$"},
  {id:"CN2.5-P17-a-d", src:"Course notes 2.5, End of Section Problem 17, parts (a) to (d)", lec:"L14", kind:"problem", page:"p. 109",
   q:"Evaluate<br>(a) $\\arcsin\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}\\right)$<br>(b) $\\arccos\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}\\right)$<br>(c) $\\arccos(0)$<br>(d) $\\arctan(1)$",
   sol:[
    "(a) We want $\\theta \\in \\left[-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$ with $\\sin(\\theta) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}$: $\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{4}$.",
    "(b) We want $\\theta \\in [0, \\pi]$ with $\\cos(\\theta) = \\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}$: $\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{6}$.",
    "(c) We want $\\theta \\in [0, \\pi]$ with $\\cos(\\theta) = 0$: $\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$.",
    "(d) We want $\\theta \\in \\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$ with $\\tan(\\theta) = 1$: $\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{4}$."],
   ans:"(a) $\\frac{\\pi}{4}$; (b) $\\frac{\\pi}{6}$; (c) $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$; (d) $\\frac{\\pi}{4}$"},
  {id:"CN2.5-P17-e-h", src:"Course notes 2.5, End of Section Problem 17, parts (e) to (h)", lec:"L14", kind:"problem", page:"p. 109",
   q:"Evaluate<br>(e) $\\arcsin(-1)$<br>(f) $\\arctan(-\\sqrt3)$<br>(g) $\\sin\\left(\\arcsin\\left(\\frac14\\right)\\right)$<br>(h) $\\arcsin\\left(\\sin\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{5}\\right)\\right)$",
   sol:[
    "(e) We want $\\theta \\in \\left[-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$ with $\\sin(\\theta) = -1$: $\\theta = -\\frac{\\pi}{2}$.",
    "(f) We want $\\theta \\in \\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$ with $\\tan(\\theta) = -\\sqrt3$: $\\theta = -\\frac{\\pi}{3}$.",
    "(g) $\\frac14$ is in $[-1, 1]$, the domain of arcsine, so $\\sin(\\arcsin(x)) = x$ applies: the answer is $\\frac14$.",
    "(h) $\\frac{3\\pi}{5}$ is not in $\\left[-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$, so the functions do not cancel.",
    "Using $\\sin(\\pi - x) = \\sin(x)$: $\\sin\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{5}\\right) = \\sin\\left(\\pi - \\frac{3\\pi}{5}\\right) = \\sin\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{5}\\right)$, and $\\frac{2\\pi}{5}$ is in the principal interval.",
    "So $\\arcsin\\left(\\sin\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{5}\\right)\\right) = \\frac{2\\pi}{5}$."],
   ans:"(e) $-\\frac{\\pi}{2}$; (f) $-\\frac{\\pi}{3}$; (g) $\\frac14$; (h) $\\frac{2\\pi}{5}$"},
  {id:"CN2.5-P17-i-j", src:"Course notes 2.5, End of Section Problem 17, parts (i) to (j)", lec:"L14", kind:"problem", page:"p. 109",
   q:"Evaluate<br>(i) $\\arcsin\\left(\\sin\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{3}\\right)\\right)$<br>(j) $\\arctan\\left(\\tan\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\right)\\right)$",
   sol:[
    "(i) $\\frac{7\\pi}{3}$ is not in the principal interval. $\\sin\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\sin\\left(2\\pi + \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}$.",
    "$\\arcsin\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}\\right) = \\frac{\\pi}{3}$.",
    "(j) $\\frac{2\\pi}{3}$ is not in $\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$. $\\tan\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\sqrt3$ (quadrant 2, reference angle $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$).",
    "$\\arctan(-\\sqrt3) = -\\frac{\\pi}{3}$."],
   ans:"(i) $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$; (j) $-\\frac{\\pi}{3}$"},
  {id:"CN2.5-P18", src:"Course notes 2.5, End of Section Problem 18", lec:"L14", kind:"problem", page:"p. 109",
   q:"Evaluate<br>(a) $\\sin(\\arctan(3))$<br>(b) $\\cos\\left(\\arcsin\\left(\\frac14\\right)\\right)$<br>(c) $\\tan\\left(\\arccos\\left(\\frac12\\right)\\right)$<br>(d) $\\sin\\left(\\arccos\\left(-\\frac23\\right)\\right)$<br>(e) $\\cos(\\arctan(-2))$",
   sol:[
    "(a) Let $\\theta = \\arctan(3)$, so $\\tan(\\theta) = \\frac31$ with $0 < \\theta < \\frac{\\pi}{2}$: $o = 3$, $a = 1$, $h^2 = 9 + 1 = 10$, $h = \\sqrt{10}$. So $\\sin(\\theta) = \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}$.",
    "(b) Let $\\theta = \\arcsin\\left(\\frac14\\right)$, so $\\sin(\\theta) = \\frac14$ with $0 < \\theta < \\frac{\\pi}{2}$: $o = 1$, $h = 4$, $a^2 = 16 - 1 = 15$, $a = \\sqrt{15}$ (first quadrant). So $\\cos(\\theta) = \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}$.",
    "(c) Let $\\theta = \\arccos\\left(\\frac12\\right)$, so $\\cos(\\theta) = \\frac12$: $a = 1$, $h = 2$, $o^2 = 4 - 1 = 3$, $o = \\sqrt3$ (first quadrant). So $\\tan(\\theta) = \\frac{\\sqrt3}{1} = \\sqrt3$.",
    "(d) Let $\\theta = \\arccos\\left(-\\frac23\\right)$, so $\\cos(\\theta) = \\frac{-2}{3}$: $a = -2$, $h = 3$, $o^2 = 9 - 4 = 5$. Since $\\theta \\in [0, \\pi]$ and $\\cos(\\theta) < 0$, $\\theta$ is in quadrant 2, so $o = \\sqrt5$. So $\\sin(\\theta) = \\frac{\\sqrt5}{3}$.",
    "(e) Let $\\theta = \\arctan(-2)$, so $\\tan(\\theta) = \\frac{-2}{1}$ with $-\\frac{\\pi}{2} < \\theta < 0$: $o = -2$, $a = 1$, $h^2 = 4 + 1 = 5$, $h = \\sqrt5$. So $\\cos(\\theta) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt5}$."],
   ans:"(a) $\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}$; (b) $\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}$; (c) $\\sqrt3$; (d) $\\frac{\\sqrt5}{3}$; (e) $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt5}$"},
  {id:"CN2.5-P19-a-d", src:"Course notes 2.5, End of Section Problem 19, parts (a) to (d)", lec:"L15", kind:"problem", page:"p. 110",
   q:"Determine the derivative of<br>(a) $\\sin(2x+1)$<br>(b) $x\\cos(x^2)$<br>(c) $\\frac{1}{2x+3}$<br>(d) $|e^x - 1|$",
   sol:[
    "(a) Chain rule: $f'(x) = \\cos(2x+1)\\cdot 2 = 2\\cos(2x+1)$.",
    "(b) Product and chain rules: $f'(x) = 1\\cdot\\cos(x^2) + x\\cdot(-\\sin(x^2))(2x) = \\cos(x^2) - 2x^2\\sin(x^2)$.",
    "(c) Write $f(x) = (2x+3)^{-1}$. Chain rule: $f'(x) = -(2x+3)^{-2}\\cdot 2 = -\\frac{2}{(2x+3)^2}$.",
    "(d) Use $\\frac{d}{dx}|u| = \\frac{u}{|u|}u'$ with $u = e^x - 1$, $u' = e^x$: $f'(x) = \\frac{e^x - 1}{|e^x - 1|}\\cdot e^x$ (for $x \\ne 0$)."],
   ans:"(a) $2\\cos(2x+1)$; (b) $\\cos(x^2) - 2x^2\\sin(x^2)$; (c) $-\\frac{2}{(2x+3)^2}$; (d) $\\frac{e^x(e^x-1)}{|e^x-1|}$, $x \\ne 0$"},
  {id:"CN2.5-P19-e-h", src:"Course notes 2.5, End of Section Problem 19, parts (e) to (h)", lec:"L15", kind:"problem", page:"p. 110",
   q:"Determine the derivative of<br>(e) $\\sec(x)\\ln(x)$<br>(f) $\\frac{\\tan(e^x)}{x}$<br>(g) $\\csc(2x^2+3x)$<br>(h) $\\arcsin(3x) + x$",
   sol:[
    "(e) Product rule: $f'(x) = \\sec(x)\\tan(x)\\ln(x) + \\sec(x)\\cdot\\frac1x$.",
    "(f) Quotient rule, with $\\frac{d}{dx}\\tan(e^x) = \\sec^2(e^x)e^x$: $f'(x) = \\frac{x\\sec^2(e^x)e^x - \\tan(e^x)}{x^2}$.",
    "(g) Chain rule: $f'(x) = -\\csc(2x^2+3x)\\cot(2x^2+3x)\\cdot(4x+3)$.",
    "(h) $\\frac{d}{dx}\\arcsin(3x) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(3x)^2}}\\cdot 3$, so $f'(x) = \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{1-9x^2}} + 1$."],
   ans:"(e) $\\sec(x)\\tan(x)\\ln(x) + \\frac{\\sec(x)}{x}$; (f) $\\frac{xe^x\\sec^2(e^x) - \\tan(e^x)}{x^2}$; (g) $-(4x+3)\\csc(2x^2+3x)\\cot(2x^2+3x)$; (h) $\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{1-9x^2}} + 1$"},
  {id:"CN2.5-P19-i-l", src:"Course notes 2.5, End of Section Problem 19, parts (i) to (l)", lec:"L15", kind:"problem", page:"p. 110",
   q:"Determine the derivative of<br>(i) $\\arccos(xe^x)$<br>(j) $\\arctan(\\sqrt x)$<br>(k) $\\frac{\\csc(2x)}{\\sqrt x}$<br>(l) $\\arctan(x + x^2)$",
   sol:[
    "(i) Chain rule with $\\frac{d}{du}\\arccos(u) = -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^2}}$ and $u = xe^x$, $u' = e^x + xe^x$: $f'(x) = -\\frac{e^x + xe^x}{\\sqrt{1-(xe^x)^2}}$.",
    "(j) Chain rule: $f'(x) = \\frac{1}{1+(\\sqrt x)^2}\\cdot\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt x} = \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt x(1+x)}$.",
    "(k) Quotient rule: $f'(x) = \\frac{-2\\csc(2x)\\cot(2x)\\sqrt x - \\csc(2x)\\cdot\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt x}}{x}$.",
    "Multiply top and bottom by $2\\sqrt x$: $f'(x) = \\frac{-4x\\csc(2x)\\cot(2x) - \\csc(2x)}{2x^{3/2}}$.",
    "(l) Chain rule: $f'(x) = \\frac{1}{1+(x+x^2)^2}\\cdot(1 + 2x)$."],
   ans:"(i) $-\\frac{e^x + xe^x}{\\sqrt{1-x^2e^{2x}}}$; (j) $\\frac{1}{2(1+x)\\sqrt x}$; (k) $\\frac{-4x\\csc(2x)\\cot(2x) - \\csc(2x)}{2x^{3/2}}$; (l) $\\frac{1+2x}{1+(x+x^2)^2}$"},
  {id:"CN2.5-P19-m-n", src:"Course notes 2.5, End of Section Problem 19, parts (m) to (n)", lec:"L15", kind:"problem", page:"p. 110",
   q:"Determine the derivative of<br>(m) $\\arcsin(x)\\tan(2x)$<br>(n) $\\ln(|\\csc(x)|)$",
   sol:[
    "(m) Product rule: $f'(x) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}\\tan(2x) + \\arcsin(x)\\sec^2(2x)\\cdot 2$.",
    "(n) Use $\\frac{d}{dx}\\ln(|u|) = \\frac{1}{|u|}\\cdot\\frac{u}{|u|}\\cdot u' = \\frac{u'}{u}$ with $u = \\csc(x)$: $f'(x) = \\frac{-\\csc(x)\\cot(x)}{\\csc(x)} = -\\cot(x)$."],
   ans:"(m) $\\frac{\\tan(2x)}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}} + 2\\arcsin(x)\\sec^2(2x)$; (n) $-\\cot(x)$"},
  {id:"CN2.5-P20", src:"Course notes 2.5, End of Section Problem 20", lec:"L15", kind:"problem", page:"p. 110",
   q:"Verify that the given function $y$ is a solution of the given differential equation.<br>(a) $y = x\\ln(x) - x$ for $y' = \\ln(x)$<br>(b) $y = \\frac{x}{2} - \\frac{\\sin(2x)}{4}$ for $y' = \\frac12 - \\frac12\\cos(2x)$<br>(c) $y = e^{\\sin(x)}$ for $y' = \\cos(x)y$<br>(d) $y = \\sec(x)$ for $y' = \\tan(x)y$",
   sol:[
    "(a) $y' = 1\\cdot\\ln(x) + x\\cdot\\frac1x - 1 = \\ln(x)$, which equals the right side, so $y$ is a solution.",
    "(b) $y' = \\frac12 - \\frac{2\\cos(2x)}{4} = \\frac12 - \\frac12\\cos(2x)$, which equals the right side, so $y$ is a solution.",
    "(c) $y' = e^{\\sin(x)}\\cos(x)$. The right side is $\\cos(x)y = \\cos(x)e^{\\sin(x)}$. They are equal, so $y$ is a solution.",
    "(d) $y' = \\sec(x)\\tan(x)$. The right side is $\\tan(x)y = \\tan(x)\\sec(x)$. They are equal, so $y$ is a solution."],
   ans:"All four functions are solutions."},
  {id:"CN2.5-P21-a-d", src:"Course notes 2.5, End of Section Problem 21, parts (a) to (d)", lec:"L15", kind:"problem", page:"p. 110",
   q:"Solve the initial value problem.<br>(a) $y' = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$, $y(0) = 2$<br>(b) $y' = \\sec^2(x)$, $y\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{4}\\right) = 1$<br>(c) $y' = \\frac{-3}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$, $y(1) = 1$<br>(d) $y' = \\frac{1}{1+x^2}$, $y(-1) = 2$",
   sol:[
    "(a) $y = \\int\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}\\,dx = \\arcsin(x) + C$.",
    "$2 = \\arcsin(0) + C = 0 + C$, so $C = 2$.",
    "(b) $y = \\int\\sec^2(x)\\,dx = \\tan(x) + C$.",
    "$1 = \\tan\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{4}\\right) + C = -1 + C$, so $C = 2$.",
    "(c) $y = \\int\\frac{-3}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}\\,dx = 3\\int\\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}\\,dx = 3\\arccos(x) + C$.",
    "Use $y(1) = 1$: $1 = 3\\arccos(1) + C = 3\\cdot 0 + C$, so $C = 1$.",
    "(d) $y = \\int\\frac{1}{1+x^2}\\,dx = \\arctan(x) + C$.",
    "$2 = \\arctan(-1) + C = -\\frac{\\pi}{4} + C$, so $C = 2 + \\frac{\\pi}{4}$."],
   ans:"(a) $y = \\arcsin(x) + 2$; (b) $y = \\tan(x) + 2$; (c) $y = 3\\arccos(x) + 1$; (d) $y = \\arctan(x) + 2 + \\frac{\\pi}{4}$",
   note:"(c) The official solution substitutes $y(0) = 1$ instead of the stated $y(1) = 1$ and gets $y = 3\\arccos(x) + 1 - \\frac{3\\pi}{2}$. With the initial condition as printed in the problem, $y(1) = 1$, the answer is $y = 3\\arccos(x) + 1$ (checked with sympy). An equivalent form is $y = -3\\arcsin(x) + 1 + \\frac{3\\pi}{2}$."},
  {id:"CN2.5-P21-e-h", src:"Course notes 2.5, End of Section Problem 21, parts (e) to (h)", lec:"L15", kind:"problem", page:"p. 110",
   q:"Solve the initial value problem.<br>(e) $y' = e^x + x$, $y(0) = 3$<br>(f) $y' = \\sin(x)$, $y\\left(\\frac{11\\pi}{6}\\right) = 1$<br>(g) $y' = \\sec(x)\\tan(x)$, $y(0) = 2$<br>(h) $y' = \\frac{1}{1+(2x)^2}$, $y(1) = 0$",
   sol:[
    "(e) $y = \\int(e^x + x)\\,dx = e^x + \\frac12x^2 + C$.",
    "$3 = e^0 + 0 + C = 1 + C$, so $C = 2$.",
    "(f) $y = \\int\\sin(x)\\,dx = -\\cos(x) + C$.",
    "$1 = -\\cos\\left(\\frac{11\\pi}{6}\\right) + C = -\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2} + C$, so $C = 1 + \\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}$.",
    "(g) $y = \\int\\sec(x)\\tan(x)\\,dx = \\sec(x) + C$.",
    "$2 = \\sec(0) + C = 1 + C$, so $C = 1$.",
    "(h) Guess $F(x) = \\arctan(2x)$: $F'(x) = \\frac{2}{1+(2x)^2}$ by the chain rule, which is twice what we want. So use $\\frac12\\arctan(2x)$: $y = \\frac12\\arctan(2x) + C$.",
    "$0 = \\frac12\\arctan(2) + C$, so $C = -\\frac12\\arctan(2)$."],
   ans:"(e) $y = e^x + \\frac12x^2 + 2$; (f) $y = -\\cos(x) + 1 + \\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}$; (g) $y = \\sec(x) + 1$; (h) $y = \\frac12\\arctan(2x) - \\frac12\\arctan(2)$"},
  {id:"CN2.5-P21-i", src:"Course notes 2.5, End of Section Problem 21, part (i)", lec:"L15", kind:"problem", page:"p. 110",
   q:"Solve the initial value problem.<br>(i) $y' = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-\\frac{x^2}{4}}}$, $y(1) = 1$",
   sol:[
    "(i) Guess $\\arcsin\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)$: its derivative is $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/2)^2}}\\cdot\\frac12$. So $y = 2\\arcsin\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right) + C$.",
    "$1 = 2\\arcsin\\left(\\frac12\\right) + C = 2\\cdot\\frac{\\pi}{6} + C$, so $C = 1 - \\frac{\\pi}{3}$."],
   ans:"(i) $y = 2\\arcsin\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right) + 1 - \\frac{\\pi}{3}$"},
  {id:"CN2.5-P22-a-d", src:"Course notes 2.5, End of Section Problem 22, parts (a) to (d)", lec:"L12", kind:"problem", page:"p. 110",
   q:"Determine the maximum, minimum, average, amplitude, and period of each function. (For $a\\sin(b\\theta) + d$ or $a\\cos(b\\theta) + d$: average $d$, amplitude $|a|$, max $d + |a|$, min $d - |a|$, period $\\frac{2\\pi}{|b|}$.)<br>(a) $4\\cos(\\theta) - 4$<br>(b) $2\\cos(3\\theta) + 1$<br>(c) $-\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\theta}{2}\\right)$<br>(d) $3\\sin(\\pi\\theta) - 2$",
   sol:[
    "(a) $a = 4$, $b = 1$, $d = -4$: average $-4$, amplitude $4$, max $0$, min $-8$, period $2\\pi$.",
    "(b) $a = 2$, $b = 3$, $d = 1$: average $1$, amplitude $2$, max $3$, min $-1$, period $\\frac{2\\pi}{3}$.",
    "(c) $a = -1$, $b = \\frac12$, $d = 0$: average $0$, amplitude $1$, max $1$, min $-1$, period $\\frac{2\\pi}{1/2} = 4\\pi$.",
    "(d) $a = 3$, $b = \\pi$, $d = -2$: average $-2$, amplitude $3$, max $1$, min $-5$, period $\\frac{2\\pi}{\\pi} = 2$."],
   ans:"(a) max $0$, min $-8$, average $-4$, amplitude $4$, period $2\\pi$; (b) max $3$, min $-1$, average $1$, amplitude $2$, period $\\frac{2\\pi}{3}$; (c) max $1$, min $-1$, average $0$, amplitude $1$, period $4\\pi$; (d) max $1$, min $-5$, average $-2$, amplitude $3$, period $2$"},
  {id:"CN2.5-P22-e-g", src:"Course notes 2.5, End of Section Problem 22, parts (e) to (g)", lec:"L12", kind:"problem", page:"p. 110",
   q:"Determine the maximum, minimum, average, amplitude, and period of each function. (For $a\\sin(b\\theta) + d$ or $a\\cos(b\\theta) + d$: average $d$, amplitude $|a|$, max $d + |a|$, min $d - |a|$, period $\\frac{2\\pi}{|b|}$.)<br>(e) $\\sqrt2\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\theta}{3}\\right) + \\sqrt2$<br>(f) $2\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\theta}{4}\\right) + 2$<br>(g) $-2\\cos(2\\theta) + 4$",
   sol:[
    "(e) $a = \\sqrt2$, $b = \\frac13$, $d = \\sqrt2$: average $\\sqrt2$, amplitude $\\sqrt2$, max $2\\sqrt2$, min $0$, period $6\\pi$.",
    "(f) $a = 2$, $b = \\frac14$, $d = 2$: average $2$, amplitude $2$, max $4$, min $0$, period $8\\pi$.",
    "(g) $a = -2$, $b = 2$, $d = 4$: average $4$, amplitude $2$, max $6$, min $2$, period $\\pi$."],
   ans:"(e) max $2\\sqrt2$, min $0$, average $\\sqrt2$, amplitude $\\sqrt2$, period $6\\pi$; (f) max $4$, min $0$, average $2$, amplitude $2$, period $8\\pi$; (g) max $6$, min $2$, average $4$, amplitude $2$, period $\\pi$"},
  {id:"CN2.5-P23", src:"Course notes 2.5, End of Section Problem 23", lec:"L14", kind:"problem", page:"p. 110",
   q:"Let $f(x) = \\sin(\\arccos(x))$. State the domain and range of $f$ and sketch its graph.",
   sol:[
    "Domain: arccosine accepts $[-1, 1]$, and every output of arccosine is a real number, which sine accepts. So the domain is $[-1, 1]$.",
    "Range: for $-1 \\le x \\le 1$, $\\theta = \\arccos(x)$ takes every value in $[0, \\pi]$, and $\\sin(\\theta)$ on $[0, \\pi]$ takes every value in $[0, 1]$. So the range is $[0, 1]$.",
    "Simplify: let $\\theta = \\arccos(x)$, so $\\cos(\\theta) = \\frac{x}{1}$: $a = x$, $h = 1$. Then $o^2 + x^2 = 1$, $|o| = \\sqrt{1-x^2}$, and $o = \\sqrt{1-x^2}$ since $\\theta$ is in quadrant 1 or 2.",
    "So $f(x) = \\sin(\\theta) = \\sqrt{1-x^2}$. The graph is the upper half of the unit circle, from $(-1, 0)$ up to $(0, 1)$ and down to $(1, 0)$."],
   ans:"Domain $[-1, 1]$, range $[0, 1]$; $f(x) = \\sqrt{1-x^2}$, the top half of the unit circle"},
  {id:"CN2.5-P24", src:"Course notes 2.5, End of Section Problem 24", lec:"L14", kind:"problem", page:"p. 110",
   q:"Let $f(x) = \\tan(\\arcsin(x))$. State the domain and range of $f$ and simplify the equation.",
   sol:[
    "Domain: arcsine accepts $[-1, 1]$ and outputs $\\left[-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$. But $\\tan$ is not defined at $\\pm\\frac{\\pi}{2}$, which are the outputs for $x = \\pm1$. For $x \\in (-1, 1)$, $\\arcsin(x) \\in \\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$, where $\\tan$ is defined. So the domain is $(-1, 1)$.",
    "Range: $\\tan$ on $\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$ takes every real value, so the range is $(-\\infty, \\infty)$.",
    "Simplify: let $\\theta = \\arcsin(x)$, so $\\sin(\\theta) = \\frac{x}{1}$: $o = x$, $h = 1$. Then $x^2 + a^2 = 1$, $|a| = \\sqrt{1-x^2}$, and $a = \\sqrt{1-x^2}$ since $\\theta$ is in quadrant 1 or 4.",
    "So $f(x) = \\tan(\\theta) = \\frac{o}{a} = \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$."],
   ans:"Domain $(-1, 1)$, range $(-\\infty, \\infty)$, $\\tan(\\arcsin(x)) = \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$"},
  {id:"CN2.5-P25", src:"Course notes 2.5, End of Section Problem 25", lec:"L13", kind:"problem", page:"p. 110",
   q:"A boat sails towards a lighthouse. Let $\\theta$ be the angle from the base of the boat to the top of the lighthouse (the angle of elevation). Given that the top of the lighthouse is 20 m above sea level, determine the distance between the boat and the top of the lighthouse as a function of $\\theta$.",
   sol:[
    "Draw a right triangle: the vertical side is the 20 m height, the horizontal side runs along the water from the boat to the lighthouse, and the hypotenuse $d$ is the distance from the boat to the top of the lighthouse.",
    "The 20 m side is opposite $\\theta$, so $\\sin(\\theta) = \\frac{20}{d(\\theta)}$."],
   ans:"$d(\\theta) = \\frac{20}{\\sin(\\theta)} = 20\\csc(\\theta)$ m"},
  {id:"CN2.5-P26", src:"Course notes 2.5, End of Section Problem 26", lec:"L13", kind:"problem", page:"p. 110",
   q:"A steel pipe is being carried down a hallway 3 m wide. At the end of the hall there is a right turn into a narrower hallway 2 m wide. In the figure the pipe touches the outer wall of each hallway and the inner corner, and $\\theta$ is the angle between the pipe and the walls of the 3 m hallway. Find a relationship between the length of the pipe and the angle $\\theta$ when the pipe simultaneously touches both walls and the corner.",
   sol:[
    "The corner splits the pipe into two pieces, $L_1$ in the 3 m hallway and $L_2$ in the 2 m hallway.",
    "Piece in the 3 m hallway: it is the hypotenuse of a right triangle whose side opposite $\\theta$ is the hallway width 3. So $\\sin(\\theta) = \\frac{3}{L_1}$, $L_1 = \\frac{3}{\\sin(\\theta)}$.",
    "Piece in the 2 m hallway: the walls are perpendicular, so by similar triangles the angle there is also $\\theta$, and the 2 m width is adjacent to it. So $\\cos(\\theta) = \\frac{2}{L_2}$, $L_2 = \\frac{2}{\\cos(\\theta)}$.",
    "$L = L_1 + L_2$."],
   ans:"$L(\\theta) = \\frac{3}{\\sin(\\theta)} + \\frac{2}{\\cos(\\theta)}$, for $0 < \\theta < \\frac{\\pi}{2}$"},
  {id:"CN2.5-P27", src:"Course notes 2.5, End of Section Problem 27", lec:"L13", kind:"problem", page:"p. 110",
   q:"A 15 ft long ladder leans against a vertical wall. The top of the ladder starts sliding down the wall. Find a relationship between the distance the top of the ladder is from the floor and the angle the ladder makes with the floor.",
   sol:[
    "Let $\\theta$ be the angle between the ladder and the floor and $d(\\theta)$ the height of the top of the ladder above the floor. The ladder is the hypotenuse, 15 ft.",
    "The height is opposite $\\theta$: $\\sin(\\theta) = \\frac{d(\\theta)}{15}$."],
   ans:"$d(\\theta) = 15\\sin(\\theta)$ ft"},
  {id:"CN2.5-P28", src:"Course notes 2.5, End of Section Problem 28", lec:"L12", kind:"problem", page:"p. 110",
   q:"Show that the following equations are true using the identities on page 90 ($\\sin^2(x) + \\cos^2(x) = 1$, $\\sin(2x) = 2\\sin(x)\\cos(x)$, $\\cos(x+y) = \\cos(x)\\cos(y) - \\sin(x)\\sin(y)$, and the others).<br>(a) $1 + \\cot^2(\\theta) = \\csc^2(\\theta)$<br>(b) $\\cos(2\\theta) = \\cos^2(\\theta) - \\sin^2(\\theta)$<br>(c) $\\cos(2\\theta) = 2\\cos^2(\\theta) - 1$<br>(d) $\\sin(x)\\sin(2x) + \\cos(x)\\cos(2x) = \\cos(x)$",
   sol:[
    "(a) Divide $\\sin^2(\\theta) + \\cos^2(\\theta) = 1$ by $\\sin^2(\\theta)$: $1 + \\frac{\\cos^2(\\theta)}{\\sin^2(\\theta)} = \\frac{1}{\\sin^2(\\theta)}$, that is $1 + \\cot^2(\\theta) = \\csc^2(\\theta)$.",
    "(b) Put $x = y = \\theta$ in $\\cos(x+y) = \\cos(x)\\cos(y) - \\sin(x)\\sin(y)$: $\\cos(2\\theta) = \\cos(\\theta)\\cos(\\theta) - \\sin(\\theta)\\sin(\\theta) = \\cos^2(\\theta) - \\sin^2(\\theta)$.",
    "(c) From (b) and $\\sin^2(\\theta) = 1 - \\cos^2(\\theta)$: $\\cos(2\\theta) = \\cos^2(\\theta) - (1 - \\cos^2(\\theta)) = 2\\cos^2(\\theta) - 1$.",
    "(d) Use $\\sin(2x) = 2\\sin(x)\\cos(x)$ and (b): $\\sin(x)\\cdot 2\\sin(x)\\cos(x) + \\cos(x)(\\cos^2(x) - \\sin^2(x))$",
    "$= \\cos(x)\\left(2\\sin^2(x) + \\cos^2(x) - \\sin^2(x)\\right) = \\cos(x)\\left(\\sin^2(x) + \\cos^2(x)\\right) = \\cos(x)$."],
   ans:"All four identities hold, as shown."},
  {id:"CN2.6.1-EX1", src:"Course notes 2.6, Example 1", lec:"L16", kind:"example", page:"p. 111",
   q:"Evaluate $3!$ and $5!$.",
   sol:[
    "By definition $n! = 1\\cdot 2\\cdot 3\\cdots n$.",
    "$3! = 1\\cdot2\\cdot3 = 6$ and $5! = 1\\cdot2\\cdot3\\cdot4\\cdot5 = 120$."],
   ans:"$3! = 6$, $5! = 120$"},
  {id:"CN2.6.1-EX2", src:"Course notes 2.6, Example 2", lec:"L16", kind:"example", page:"p. 111",
   q:"Simplify $\\frac{5!}{3!\\cdot 2!}$.",
   sol:[
    "$\\frac{5!}{3!\\cdot2!} = \\frac{1\\cdot2\\cdot3\\cdot4\\cdot5}{(1\\cdot2\\cdot3)(1\\cdot2)}$.",
    "Cancel $1\\cdot2\\cdot3$: $= \\frac{4\\cdot5}{2} = 10$."],
   ans:"$10$"},
  {id:"CN2.6.1-XR1", src:"Course notes 2.6, Exercise 1", lec:"L16", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 112",
   q:"Simplify $\\frac{4!}{2!\\cdot 2!}$.",
   sol:[
    "$\\frac{4!}{2!\\cdot2!} = \\frac{1\\cdot2\\cdot3\\cdot4}{(1\\cdot2)(1\\cdot2)} = \\frac{3\\cdot4}{2} = 6$."],
   ans:"$6$"},
  {id:"CN2.6.1-EX3", src:"Course notes 2.6, Example 3", lec:"L16", kind:"example", page:"p. 112",
   q:"Let $f(n) = \\frac{n!}{(n+2)!}$. Evaluate $f(3)$.",
   sol:[
    "$f(3) = \\frac{3!}{5!} = \\frac{1\\cdot2\\cdot3}{1\\cdot2\\cdot3\\cdot4\\cdot5} = \\frac{1}{4\\cdot5} = \\frac{1}{20}$."],
   ans:"$f(3) = \\frac{1}{20}$"},
  {id:"CN2.6.1-EX4", src:"Course notes 2.6, Example 4", lec:"L16", kind:"example", page:"p. 112",
   q:"Simplify $\\frac{n!}{(n+2)!}$.",
   sol:[
    "$n!$ is the product of the integers from 1 to $n$ and $(n+2)!$ is the product from 1 to $n+2$:",
    "$\\frac{n!}{(n+2)!} = \\frac{1\\cdot2\\cdots(n-1)\\cdot n}{1\\cdot2\\cdots(n-1)\\cdot n\\cdot(n+1)\\cdot(n+2)}$.",
    "Cancel the common factors $1\\cdot2\\cdots n$: $\\frac{n!}{(n+2)!} = \\frac{1}{(n+1)(n+2)}$."],
   ans:"$\\frac{1}{(n+1)(n+2)}$",
   note:"Check with Example 3: $n = 3$ gives $\\frac{1}{4\\cdot5} = \\frac{1}{20}$."},
  {id:"CN2.6.1-XR2", src:"Course notes 2.6, Exercise 2", lec:"L16", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 112",
   q:"Simplify $\\frac{n!}{(n-1)!}$.",
   sol:[
    "$\\frac{n!}{(n-1)!} = \\frac{1\\cdot2\\cdots(n-2)(n-1)\\cdot n}{1\\cdot2\\cdots(n-2)(n-1)}$.",
    "Everything cancels except the last factor of the numerator: $= n$."],
   ans:"$n$"},
  {id:"CN2.6.1-XR3", src:"Course notes 2.6, Exercise 3", lec:"L16", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 112",
   q:"Simplify $\\frac{2n!}{(2n)!}$. (Here $2n!$ means $2\\cdot n!$.)",
   sol:[
    "$\\frac{2\\cdot n!}{(2n)!} = \\frac{2\\cdot(1\\cdot2\\cdots n)}{1\\cdot2\\cdots n\\cdot(n+1)\\cdots(2n-1)\\cdot 2n}$.",
    "Cancel $1\\cdot2\\cdots n$: $= \\frac{2}{(n+1)(n+2)\\cdots(2n-1)\\cdot 2n}$.",
    "Cancel the 2 with the $2n$: $= \\frac{1}{n\\cdot(n+1)\\cdot(n+2)\\cdots(2n-1)}$."],
   ans:"$\\frac{1}{n(n+1)(n+2)\\cdots(2n-1)}$",
   note:"Check: $n = 2$ gives $\\frac{2\\cdot2}{24} = \\frac16$ and $\\frac{1}{2\\cdot3} = \\frac16$."},
  {id:"CN2.6-P1-a-d", src:"Course notes 2.6, End of Section Problem 1, parts (a) to (d)", lec:"L16", kind:"problem", page:"p. 113",
   q:"Simplify<br>(a) $\\frac{5!}{3!\\cdot2!}$<br>(b) $\\frac{7!}{6!\\,1!}$<br>(c) $\\frac{6!}{4!\\,2!}$<br>(d) $\\frac{8!}{2!\\,6!}$",
   sol:[
    "(a) $\\frac{1\\cdot2\\cdot3\\cdot4\\cdot5}{(1\\cdot2\\cdot3)(1\\cdot2)} = \\frac{4\\cdot5}{2} = 10$.",
    "(b) $\\frac{7!}{6!\\cdot1!} = \\frac{1\\cdots6\\cdot7}{(1\\cdots6)\\cdot1} = 7$.",
    "(c) $\\frac{1\\cdots4\\cdot5\\cdot6}{(1\\cdots4)(1\\cdot2)} = \\frac{5\\cdot6}{2} = 15$.",
    "(d) $\\frac{1\\cdots6\\cdot7\\cdot8}{(1\\cdot2)(1\\cdots6)} = \\frac{7\\cdot8}{2} = 28$."],
   ans:"(a) $10$; (b) $7$; (c) $15$; (d) $28$"},
  {id:"CN2.6-P1-e-h", src:"Course notes 2.6, End of Section Problem 1, parts (e) to (h)", lec:"L16", kind:"problem", page:"p. 113",
   q:"Simplify<br>(e) $\\frac{8!}{3!\\,5!}$<br>(f) $\\frac{17!}{15!}$<br>(g) $\\frac{23!}{24!}$<br>(h) $\\frac{3!\\,4!}{2!\\,0!}$",
   sol:[
    "(e) $\\frac{1\\cdots5\\cdot6\\cdot7\\cdot8}{(1\\cdot2\\cdot3)(1\\cdots5)} = \\frac{6\\cdot7\\cdot8}{6} = 56$.",
    "(f) $\\frac{1\\cdots15\\cdot16\\cdot17}{1\\cdots15} = 16\\cdot17 = 272$.",
    "(g) $\\frac{1\\cdots23}{1\\cdots23\\cdot24} = \\frac{1}{24}$.",
    "(h) Recall $0! = 1$. $\\frac{3!\\cdot4!}{2!\\cdot0!} = \\frac{6\\cdot24}{2\\cdot1} = 72$."],
   ans:"(e) $56$; (f) $272$; (g) $\\frac{1}{24}$; (h) $72$"},
  {id:"CN2.6-P1-i-j", src:"Course notes 2.6, End of Section Problem 1, parts (i) to (j)", lec:"L16", kind:"problem", page:"p. 113",
   q:"Simplify<br>(i) $\\frac{15!\\,10!}{13!\\,11!}$<br>(j) $\\frac{12!\\,8!}{(9!)^2}$",
   sol:[
    "(i) $\\frac{15!}{13!}\\cdot\\frac{10!}{11!} = (14\\cdot15)\\cdot\\frac{1}{11} = \\frac{210}{11}$.",
    "(j) $\\frac{12!}{9!}\\cdot\\frac{8!}{9!} = (10\\cdot11\\cdot12)\\cdot\\frac19 = \\frac{1320}{9} = \\frac{440}{3}$."],
   ans:"(i) $\\frac{210}{11}$; (j) $\\frac{440}{3}$"},
  {id:"CN2.6-P2", src:"Course notes 2.6, End of Section Problem 2", lec:"L16", kind:"problem", page:"p. 113",
   q:"Let $f(n) = \\frac{(n+1)!}{(n-1)!}$. Evaluate (a) $f(1)$ (b) $f(2)$ (c) $f(3)$ (d) $f(4)$.",
   sol:[
    "(a) $f(1) = \\frac{2!}{0!} = \\frac{2}{1} = 2$.",
    "(b) $f(2) = \\frac{3!}{1!} = 6$.",
    "(c) $f(3) = \\frac{4!}{2!} = \\frac{24}{2} = 12$.",
    "(d) $f(4) = \\frac{5!}{3!} = \\frac{120}{6} = 20$.",
    "(In general $f(n) = n(n+1)$, which matches all four values.)"],
   ans:"(a) $2$; (b) $6$; (c) $12$; (d) $20$"},
  {id:"CN2.6-P3-a-d", src:"Course notes 2.6, End of Section Problem 3, parts (a) to (d)", lec:"L16", kind:"problem", page:"p. 113",
   q:"Simplify<br>(a) $\\frac{n!}{(n+1)!}$<br>(b) $\\frac{n!}{(n+2)!}$<br>(c) $\\frac{(n+1)!}{(n-1)!}$<br>(d) $\\frac{(n-1)!}{(n-3)!}$",
   sol:[
    "(a) $\\frac{1\\cdots n}{1\\cdots n\\cdot(n+1)} = \\frac{1}{n+1}$.",
    "(b) $\\frac{1\\cdots n}{1\\cdots n\\cdot(n+1)(n+2)} = \\frac{1}{(n+1)(n+2)}$.",
    "(c) $\\frac{1\\cdots(n-1)\\cdot n\\cdot(n+1)}{1\\cdots(n-1)} = n(n+1)$.",
    "(d) $\\frac{1\\cdots(n-3)(n-2)(n-1)}{1\\cdots(n-3)} = (n-2)(n-1)$."],
   ans:"(a) $\\frac{1}{n+1}$; (b) $\\frac{1}{(n+1)(n+2)}$; (c) $n(n+1)$; (d) $(n-2)(n-1)$"},
  {id:"CN2.6-P3-e-h", src:"Course notes 2.6, End of Section Problem 3, parts (e) to (h)", lec:"L16", kind:"problem", page:"p. 113",
   q:"Simplify<br>(e) $\\frac{(n+3)!}{(n+1)!}$<br>(f) $\\frac{(n+2)!}{(n+1)!}$<br>(g) $\\frac{(2n+1)!}{(2n)!}$<br>(h) $\\frac{(2n)!}{(2n+2)!}$",
   sol:[
    "(e) $\\frac{1\\cdots(n+1)(n+2)(n+3)}{1\\cdots(n+1)} = (n+2)(n+3)$.",
    "(f) $\\frac{1\\cdots(n+1)(n+2)}{1\\cdots(n+1)} = n+2$.",
    "(g) $\\frac{1\\cdots(2n)(2n+1)}{1\\cdots(2n)} = 2n+1$.",
    "(h) $\\frac{1\\cdots(2n)}{1\\cdots(2n)(2n+1)(2n+2)} = \\frac{1}{(2n+1)(2n+2)}$."],
   ans:"(e) $(n+2)(n+3)$; (f) $n+2$; (g) $2n+1$; (h) $\\frac{1}{(2n+1)(2n+2)}$"},
  {id:"CN2.6-P4", src:"Course notes 2.6, End of Section Problem 4", lec:"L16", kind:"problem", page:"p. 113",
   q:"Find the value of $n$ that makes the expression true: (a) $n! = 24$ (b) $n! = 120$ (c) $(n-1)! = 6$ (d) $n! = 56(n-2)!$.",
   sol:[
    "(a) $1\\cdot2\\cdot3\\cdot4 = 24$, so $4! = 24$ and $n = 4$.",
    "(b) $5! = 4!\\cdot5 = 120$, so $n = 5$.",
    "(c) $3! = 6$, so $n - 1 = 3$ and $n = 4$.",
    "(d) Divide by $(n-2)!$: $\\frac{n!}{(n-2)!} = (n-1)n = 56$. So $n^2 - n - 56 = 0$, $(n-8)(n+7) = 0$, and since $n \\ge 2$ is an integer, $n = 8$ (check: $7\\cdot8 = 56$)."],
   ans:"(a) $n = 4$; (b) $n = 5$; (c) $n = 4$; (d) $n = 8$"},
  {id:"CN2.6-P5", src:"Course notes 2.6, End of Section Problem 5", lec:"L16", kind:"problem", page:"p. 113",
   q:"If two brown-eyed parents have 3 children, the probability that there will be exactly $r$ blue-eyed children is $P(r) = \\frac{3!\\left(\\frac14\\right)^r\\left(\\frac34\\right)^{3-r}}{r!\\,(3-r)!}$, $r = 0, 1, 2, 3$. Find the probability that exactly two of the children will be blue-eyed.",
   sol:[
    "Substitute $r = 2$: $P(2) = \\frac{3!\\left(\\frac14\\right)^2\\left(\\frac34\\right)^{1}}{2!\\cdot1!}$.",
    "$= \\frac{6\\cdot\\frac{1}{16}\\cdot\\frac34}{2} = \\frac{6\\cdot3}{2\\cdot64} = \\frac{9}{64}$."],
   ans:"$P(2) = \\frac{9}{64} \\approx 0.14$"},
  {id:"CN2.7.0-EX1", src:"Course notes 2.7, Example 1", lec:"L16", kind:"example", page:"p. 115",
   q:"The solutions to the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{6x^2}{2y + \\cos(y)}$ are given by the implicitly defined functions $y^2 + \\sin(y) = 2x^3 + C$. Verify this.",
   sol:[
    "Treat $y$ as a function of $x$ and differentiate both sides of $y^2 + \\sin(y) = 2x^3 + C$ with respect to $x$ (chain rule on $y^2$ and $\\sin(y)$):",
    "$2y\\,y' + \\cos(y)\\,y' = 6x^2$.",
    "Factor: $(2y + \\cos(y))\\,y' = 6x^2$, so $y' = \\frac{6x^2}{2y + \\cos(y)}$, which is the differential equation. So every function defined by $y^2 + \\sin(y) = 2x^3 + C$ is a solution."],
   ans:"Implicit differentiation gives $y' = \\frac{6x^2}{2y + \\cos(y)}$, so the curves are solutions.",
   note:"In the notes this example is stated without working. The verification is the same as Example 5 with a general constant $C$."},
  {id:"CN2.7.1-EX3", src:"Course notes 2.7, Example 3", lec:"L16", kind:"example", page:"p. 116",
   q:"Find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ where $x^2 + \\ln(xy) = \\cos(y)$.",
   sol:[
    "Treat $y$ as a function of $x$. $xy = x\\cdot y(x)$ needs the product rule and $\\cos(y) = \\cos(y(x))$ needs the chain rule.",
    "Differentiate: $2x + \\frac{1}{xy}(y + xy') = -\\sin(y)\\,y'$.",
    "Multiply by $xy$: $2x^2y + y + xy' = -xy\\sin(y)\\,y'$.",
    "Collect the $y'$ terms: $xy' + xy\\sin(y)\\,y' = -2x^2y - y$, so $y'(x + xy\\sin(y)) = -2x^2y - y$."],
   ans:"$y' = \\frac{-2x^2y - y}{x + xy\\sin(y)}$"},
  {id:"CN2.7.1-EX4", src:"Course notes 2.7, Example 4", lec:"L16", kind:"example", page:"p. 116",
   q:"Find $y'$ if $x^3y^5 = 8y^3$.",
   sol:[
    "$x^3y^5$ is a product and $y^5$, $y^3$ are compositions. Product and chain rules:",
    "$3x^2y^5 + 5x^3y^4\\,y' = 24y^2\\,y'$.",
    "$5x^3y^4\\,y' - 24y^2\\,y' = -3x^2y^5$, so $y'(5x^3y^4 - 24y^2) = -3x^2y^5$."],
   ans:"$y' = \\frac{-3x^2y^5}{5x^3y^4 - 24y^2}$"},
  {id:"CN2.7.1-XR1", src:"Course notes 2.7, Exercise 1", lec:"L16", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 117",
   q:"Find $y'$ if $xy^2 = \\arctan(y)$.",
   sol:[
    "Differentiate both sides: $1\\cdot y^2 + x\\cdot 2y\\,y' = \\frac{1}{1+y^2}\\,y'$.",
    "$2xy\\,y' - \\frac{1}{1+y^2}\\,y' = -y^2$, so $\\left(2xy - \\frac{1}{1+y^2}\\right)y' = -y^2$.",
    "$y' = \\frac{-y^2}{2xy - \\frac{1}{1+y^2}}$. Multiply top and bottom by $1 + y^2$:"],
   ans:"$y' = \\frac{-y^2(1+y^2)}{2xy(1+y^2) - 1}$"},
  {id:"CN2.7.1-EX5", src:"Course notes 2.7, Example 5", lec:"L16", kind:"example", page:"p. 117",
   q:"Verify that the implicitly defined function $y^2 + \\sin(y) = 2x^3 + 1$ is a solution to the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{6x^2}{2y + \\cos(y)}$.",
   sol:[
    "Differentiate implicitly with respect to $x$: $2y\\,y' + \\cos(y)\\,y' = 6x^2$.",
    "$(2y + \\cos(y))\\,y' = 6x^2$, so $y' = \\frac{6x^2}{2y + \\cos(y)}$.",
    "This is the differential equation, so the function is a solution."],
   ans:"$y' = \\frac{6x^2}{2y + \\cos(y)}$, so it is a solution."},
  {id:"CN2.7.1-EX6", src:"Course notes 2.7, Example 6", lec:"L16", kind:"example", page:"p. 117",
   q:"Find the slope of the tangent line to $x^2 + y^2 = 4$ at the point $(1, -\\sqrt3)$.",
   sol:[
    "Differentiate implicitly: $2x + 2y\\,y' = 0$.",
    "The curve fails the vertical line test, so we need both coordinates. Substitute $x = 1$, $y = -\\sqrt3$: $2 + 2(-\\sqrt3)\\,y' = 0 \\Rightarrow -2\\sqrt3\\,y' = -2$."],
   ans:"$y' = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt3}$"},
  {id:"CN2.7.1-EX7", src:"Course notes 2.7, Example 7", lec:"L16", kind:"example", page:"p. 118",
   q:"Find the equation of the tangent line to $x^2 + y^2 = 4$ at the point $(1, -\\sqrt3)$.",
   sol:[
    "From Example 6 (implicit differentiation gives $2x + 2y\\,y' = 0$), the slope at $(1, -\\sqrt3)$ is $y' = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt3}$.",
    "Tangent line: $y = f(a) + f'(a)(x - a)$ with $a = 1$, $f(a) = -\\sqrt3$, $f'(a) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt3}$."],
   ans:"$y = -\\sqrt3 + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt3}(x - 1)$"},
  {id:"CN2.7.1-XR2", src:"Course notes 2.7, Exercise 2", lec:"L16", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 118",
   q:"Find the tangent line to $\\sin(x + y) = y^2\\cos(x)$ at the point $(0, 0)$.",
   sol:[
    "Differentiate implicitly (chain rule on the left, product rule on the right): $\\cos(x+y)(1 + y') = y^2(-\\sin(x)) + 2y\\,y'\\cos(x)$.",
    "Substitute $x = 0$, $y = 0$: $\\cos(0)(1 + y') = 0 + 0$, so $1 + y' = 0$ and $y' = -1$.",
    "Tangent line through $(0,0)$: $y = 0 + (-1)(x - 0)$."],
   ans:"$y = -x$"},
  {id:"CN2.7.1-XR3", src:"Course notes 2.7, Exercise 3", lec:"L16", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 118",
   q:"Elliptic curve cryptography is the foundation behind the cryptocurrencies Bitcoin and Ethereum. The implicitly defined function $y^2 = x^3 - x + 1$ is an example of an elliptic curve. Find the equation of the tangent line to this curve at $(-1, -1)$.",
   sol:[
    "Differentiate implicitly: $2y\\,y' = 3x^2 - 1$, so $y' = \\frac{3x^2 - 1}{2y}$.",
    "At $(-1, -1)$: $y' = \\frac{3(-1)^2 - 1}{2(-1)} = \\frac{2}{-2} = -1$.",
    "Tangent line: $y = f(a) + f'(a)(x - a) = -1 + (-1)(x + 1)$."],
   ans:"$y = -1 - (x + 1)$, that is $y = -x - 2$"},
  {id:"CN2.7.1-EX8", src:"Course notes 2.7, Example 8", lec:"L16", kind:"example", page:"p. 118",
   q:"Use implicit differentiation and the fact that $\\ln(x)$ is the inverse of $e^x$ to show that $\\frac{d}{dx}\\ln(x) = \\frac1x$.",
   sol:[
    "Let $y = \\ln(x)$. Then $e^y = x$.",
    "Differentiate implicitly with respect to $x$: $e^y\\,y' = 1$, so $y' = \\frac{1}{e^y}$.",
    "Since $e^y = x$, $y' = \\frac1x$. That is, $\\frac{d}{dx}\\ln(x) = \\frac1x$."],
   ans:"$\\frac{d}{dx}\\ln(x) = \\frac1x$"},
  {id:"CN2.7.1-EX9", src:"Course notes 2.7, Example 9", lec:"L16", kind:"example", page:"p. 119",
   q:"Use implicit differentiation and the fact that $\\arcsin(x)$ is the inverse of $\\sin(x)$ to show that $\\frac{d}{dx}\\arcsin(x) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$.",
   sol:[
    "Let $y = \\arcsin(x)$. Then $\\sin(y) = x$ with $-\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\le y \\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}$.",
    "Differentiate implicitly: $\\cos(y)\\,y' = 1$, so $y' = \\frac{1}{\\cos(y)} = \\sec(y)$.",
    "Write $\\sec(y)$ in terms of $x$: triangle for $\\sin(y) = \\frac{x}{1}$ has $o = x$, $h = 1$, so $a^2 = 1 - x^2$ and $|a| = \\sqrt{1-x^2}$.",
    "Since $-\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\le y \\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}$, $\\cos(y) \\ge 0$, so $a = \\sqrt{1-x^2}$ and $\\sec(y) = \\frac{h}{a} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$."],
   ans:"$\\frac{d}{dx}\\arcsin(x) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$"},
  {id:"CN2.7.1-XR4", src:"Course notes 2.7, Exercise 4", lec:"L16", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 119",
   q:"Use implicit differentiation and the fact that $\\arctan(x)$ is the inverse of $\\tan(x)$ to show that $\\frac{d}{dx}\\arctan(x) = \\frac{1}{1+x^2}$.",
   sol:[
    "Let $y = \\arctan(x)$. Then $\\tan(y) = x$ with $-\\frac{\\pi}{2} < y < \\frac{\\pi}{2}$.",
    "Differentiate implicitly: $\\sec^2(y)\\,y' = 1$, so $y' = \\cos^2(y)$.",
    "Triangle for $\\tan(y) = \\frac{x}{1}$: $o = x$, $a = 1$, $h^2 = 1 + x^2$, and $h = \\sqrt{1+x^2}$ since the hypotenuse is positive. (The adjacent side is positive because $\\cos(y) > 0$ on $\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$.)",
    "$\\cos(y) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+x^2}}$, so $y' = \\cos^2(y) = \\frac{1}{(\\sqrt{1+x^2})^2} = \\frac{1}{1+x^2}$."],
   ans:"$\\frac{d}{dx}\\arctan(x) = \\frac{1}{1+x^2}$"},
  {id:"CN2.7.2-EX10", src:"Course notes 2.7, Example 10", lec:"L17", kind:"example", page:"p. 120",
   q:"Determine the derivative of $y = (x+1)^x$, $x > -1$.",
   sol:[
    "Both base and exponent vary, so use logarithmic differentiation. Take $\\ln$ of both sides: $\\ln(y) = \\ln\\left((x+1)^x\\right) = x\\ln(x+1)$.",
    "Differentiate implicitly: $\\frac1y\\,y' = \\ln(x+1) + \\frac{x}{x+1}$.",
    "Multiply by $y = (x+1)^x$."],
   ans:"$y' = (x+1)^x\\left(\\ln(x+1) + \\frac{x}{x+1}\\right)$"},
  {id:"CN2.7.2-EX11", src:"Course notes 2.7, Example 11", lec:"L17", kind:"example", page:"p. 120",
   q:"Determine the derivative of $f(x) = (\\ln(x))^{e^x}$, $x > 1$.",
   sol:[
    "Let $y = (\\ln(x))^{e^x}$. Take $\\ln$: $\\ln(y) = e^x\\ln(\\ln(x))$.",
    "Differentiate implicitly (product rule, chain rule): $\\frac1y\\,y' = e^x\\ln(\\ln(x)) + e^x\\cdot\\frac{1}{\\ln(x)}\\cdot\\frac1x$.",
    "Multiply by $y$."],
   ans:"$f'(x) = (\\ln(x))^{e^x}\\left[e^x\\ln(\\ln(x)) + \\frac{e^x}{x\\ln(x)}\\right]$"},
  {id:"CN2.7.2-XR5", src:"Course notes 2.7, Exercise 5", lec:"L17", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 121",
   q:"Determine the derivative of $y = x^{\\arctan(x)}$, $x > 0$.",
   sol:[
    "Take $\\ln$: $\\ln(y) = \\arctan(x)\\ln(x)$.",
    "Differentiate implicitly: $\\frac{y'}{y} = \\frac{\\ln(x)}{1+x^2} + \\frac{\\arctan(x)}{x}$.",
    "Multiply by $y = x^{\\arctan(x)}$."],
   ans:"$y' = x^{\\arctan(x)}\\left(\\frac{\\ln(x)}{1+x^2} + \\frac{\\arctan(x)}{x}\\right)$"},
  {id:"CN2.7.2-EX12", src:"Course notes 2.7, Example 12", lec:"L17", kind:"example", page:"p. 121",
   q:"Determine the derivative of $y = \\frac{(x-2)^2(x-1)^3}{(x+2)^5(x-6)^4}$, $x > 6$.",
   sol:[
    "Take $\\ln$ and use the log laws (all factors are positive for $x > 6$):",
    "$\\ln(y) = 2\\ln(x-2) + 3\\ln(x-1) - 5\\ln(x+2) - 4\\ln(x-6)$.",
    "Differentiate implicitly: $\\frac1y\\,y' = \\frac{2}{x-2} + \\frac{3}{x-1} - \\frac{5}{x+2} - \\frac{4}{x-6}$.",
    "Multiply by $y$."],
   ans:"$y' = \\frac{(x-2)^2(x-1)^3}{(x+2)^5(x-6)^4}\\left[\\frac{2}{x-2} + \\frac{3}{x-1} - \\frac{5}{x+2} - \\frac{4}{x-6}\\right]$"},
  {id:"CN2.7.2-XR6", src:"Course notes 2.7, Exercise 6", lec:"L17", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 121",
   q:"Use logarithmic differentiation to calculate the derivative of $f(x) = \\frac{x^3\\sqrt{x+1}}{(x+2)^2(x-1)}$, $x > 1$.",
   sol:[
    "Let $y = f(x)$. Take $\\ln$ and use the log laws: $\\ln(y) = 3\\ln(x) + \\frac12\\ln(x+1) - 2\\ln(x+2) - \\ln(x-1)$.",
    "Differentiate implicitly: $\\frac{y'}{y} = \\frac3x + \\frac{1}{2(x+1)} - \\frac{2}{x+2} - \\frac{1}{x-1}$.",
    "Multiply by $y$."],
   ans:"$f'(x) = \\frac{x^3\\sqrt{x+1}}{(x+2)^2(x-1)}\\left[\\frac3x + \\frac{1}{2(x+1)} - \\frac{2}{x+2} - \\frac{1}{x-1}\\right]$"},
  {id:"CN2.7-P1-a-d", src:"Course notes 2.7, End of Section Problem 1, parts (a) to (d)", lec:"L16", kind:"problem", page:"p. 122",
   q:"Find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ by implicit differentiation.<br>(a) $x = y^2$<br>(b) $xy^2 = 1$<br>(c) $\\frac{x}{y} + y = 1$<br>(d) $3x + y^2 = x^2$",
   sol:[
    "(a) $1 = 2y\\,y'$, so $y' = \\frac{1}{2y}$.",
    "(b) $y^2 + x\\cdot 2y\\,y' = 0 \\Rightarrow 2xy\\,y' = -y^2 \\Rightarrow y' = -\\frac{y}{2x}$.",
    "(c) Quotient rule: $\\frac{y - xy'}{y^2} + y' = 0$. Multiply by $y^2$: $y - xy' + y^2y' = 0$.",
    "$y = xy' - y^2y' = (x - y^2)y'$, so $y' = \\frac{y}{x - y^2}$.",
    "(d) $3 + 2y\\,y' = 2x \\Rightarrow y' = \\frac{2x - 3}{2y}$."],
   ans:"(a) $y' = \\frac{1}{2y}$; (b) $y' = -\\frac{y}{2x}$; (c) $y' = \\frac{y}{x - y^2}$; (d) $y' = \\frac{2x-3}{2y}$"},
  {id:"CN2.7-P1-e-h", src:"Course notes 2.7, End of Section Problem 1, parts (e) to (h)", lec:"L16", kind:"problem", page:"p. 122",
   q:"Find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ by implicit differentiation.<br>(e) $x^3 + y^3 = 1$<br>(f) $x^3 + y^3 = 6xy$<br>(g) $y^2 = x^2 - x^4$<br>(h) $y + y^2 = \\sin(x)$",
   sol:[
    "(e) $3x^2 + 3y^2y' = 0 \\Rightarrow y' = -\\frac{x^2}{y^2}$.",
    "(f) $3x^2 + 3y^2y' = 6y + 6xy'$.",
    "$3x^2 - 6y = y'(6x - 3y^2)$, so $y' = \\frac{3x^2 - 6y}{6x - 3y^2} = \\frac{x^2 - 2y}{2x - y^2}$.",
    "(g) $2y\\,y' = 2x - 4x^3 \\Rightarrow y' = \\frac{x - 2x^3}{y}$.",
    "(h) $y' + 2y\\,y' = \\cos(x) \\Rightarrow y'(1 + 2y) = \\cos(x) \\Rightarrow y' = \\frac{\\cos(x)}{1 + 2y}$."],
   ans:"(e) $y' = -\\frac{x^2}{y^2}$; (f) $y' = \\frac{x^2 - 2y}{2x - y^2}$; (g) $y' = \\frac{x - 2x^3}{y}$; (h) $y' = \\frac{\\cos(x)}{1+2y}$"},
  {id:"CN2.7-P1-i-l", src:"Course notes 2.7, End of Section Problem 1, parts (i) to (l)", lec:"L16", kind:"problem", page:"p. 122",
   q:"Find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ by implicit differentiation.<br>(i) $x^2 + xy - y^2 = 4$<br>(j) $4\\cos(x)\\sin(y) = 1$<br>(k) $y\\sin(x^2) = x\\sin(y^2)$<br>(l) $\\ln(y) = \\arccos(x)$",
   sol:[
    "(i) $2x + y + xy' - 2y\\,y' = 0 \\Rightarrow y'(x - 2y) = -2x - y \\Rightarrow y' = \\frac{-2x - y}{x - 2y}$.",
    "(j) Product rule: $-4\\sin(x)\\sin(y) + 4\\cos(x)\\cos(y)\\,y' = 0$.",
    "$y' = \\frac{\\sin(x)\\sin(y)}{\\cos(x)\\cos(y)} = \\tan(x)\\tan(y)$.",
    "(k) Product and chain rules on both sides: $y'\\sin(x^2) + 2xy\\cos(x^2) = \\sin(y^2) + 2xy\\cos(y^2)\\,y'$.",
    "$y'\\left(\\sin(x^2) - 2xy\\cos(y^2)\\right) = \\sin(y^2) - 2xy\\cos(x^2)$.",
    "(l) $\\frac1y\\,y' = -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}} \\Rightarrow y' = -\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$."],
   ans:"(i) $y' = \\frac{-2x-y}{x-2y} = \\frac{2x+y}{2y-x}$; (j) $y' = \\tan(x)\\tan(y)$; (k) $y' = \\frac{\\sin(y^2) - 2xy\\cos(x^2)}{\\sin(x^2) - 2xy\\cos(y^2)}$; (l) $y' = -\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$"},
  {id:"CN2.7-P1-m-n", src:"Course notes 2.7, End of Section Problem 1, parts (m) to (n)", lec:"L16", kind:"problem", page:"p. 122",
   q:"Find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ by implicit differentiation.<br>(m) $x\\sec(y) = 5$<br>(n) $\\frac{x}{x+y} - \\frac{y}{x} = 4$",
   sol:[
    "(m) $\\sec(y) + x\\sec(y)\\tan(y)\\,y' = 0 \\Rightarrow y' = \\frac{-\\sec(y)}{x\\sec(y)\\tan(y)} = \\frac{-1}{x\\tan(y)}$.",
    "(n) Quotient rule on both fractions: $\\frac{(x+y) - x(1+y')}{(x+y)^2} - \\frac{xy' - y}{x^2} = 0$.",
    "Both numerators simplify to $y - xy'$: $(y - xy')\\left(\\frac{1}{(x+y)^2} + \\frac{1}{x^2}\\right) = 0$.",
    "The bracket is a sum of positive terms, so it is not 0. Hence $y - xy' = 0$ and $y' = \\frac{y}{x}$."],
   ans:"(m) $y' = -\\frac{1}{x\\tan(y)}$; (n) $y' = \\frac{y}{x}$"},
  {id:"CN2.7-P2", src:"Course notes 2.7, End of Section Problem 2", lec:"L16", kind:"problem", page:"p. 122",
   q:"Use implicit differentiation to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the given point.<br>(a) $x^2 = y^2 + y$ at $(\\sqrt2, 1)$<br>(b) $x^2 + y^2 = 1$ at $(0, -1)$<br>(c) $x^2 + xy + y^2 = 3$ at $(1, 1)$<br>(d) $x^2 + 2xy - y^3 + x = -2$ at $(1, 2)$<br>(e) $x^2 + y^2 = (2x^2 + 2y^2 - x)^2$ at $\\left(0, \\frac12\\right)$",
   sol:[
    "(a) $2x = 2y\\,y' + y'$. At $(\\sqrt2, 1)$: $2\\sqrt2 = 3y'$, $y' = \\frac{2\\sqrt2}{3}$. Tangent: $y = 1 + \\frac{2\\sqrt2}{3}(x - \\sqrt2)$.",
    "(b) $2x + 2y\\,y' = 0$. At $(0, -1)$: $0 - 2y' = 0$, $y' = 0$. Tangent: $y = -1$.",
    "(c) $2x + y + xy' + 2y\\,y' = 0$. At $(1, 1)$: $3 + 3y' = 0$, $y' = -1$. Tangent: $y = 1 - (x - 1)$.",
    "(d) $2x + 2y + 2xy' - 3y^2y' + 1 = 0$. At $(1, 2)$: $2 + 4 + 2y' - 12y' + 1 = 0$, so $7 = 10y'$, $y' = \\frac{7}{10}$. Tangent: $y = 2 + \\frac{7}{10}(x - 1)$.",
    "(e) $2x + 2y\\,y' = 2(2x^2 + 2y^2 - x)(4x + 4y\\,y' - 1)$. At $\\left(0, \\frac12\\right)$: $y' = 2\\cdot\\frac12\\cdot(2y' - 1) = 2y' - 1$, so $y' = 1$. Tangent: $y = \\frac12 + x$."],
   ans:"(a) $y = 1 + \\frac{2\\sqrt2}{3}(x - \\sqrt2)$; (b) $y = -1$; (c) $y = 1 - (x-1)$; (d) $y = 2 + \\frac{7}{10}(x-1)$; (e) $y = \\frac12 + x$"},
  {id:"CN2.7-P3-a-d", src:"Course notes 2.7, End of Section Problem 3, parts (a) to (d)", lec:"L17", kind:"problem", page:"p. 122",
   q:"Find $y'$.<br>(a) $y = x^x$<br>(b) $y = x^{\\sin(x)}$<br>(c) $y = 2^x$<br>(d) $y = (\\arcsin(x))^3$",
   sol:[
    "(a) $\\ln(y) = x\\ln(x)$. $\\frac{y'}{y} = \\ln(x) + x\\cdot\\frac1x = \\ln(x) + 1$, so $y' = x^x(\\ln(x) + 1)$.",
    "(b) $\\ln(y) = \\sin(x)\\ln(x)$. $\\frac{y'}{y} = \\cos(x)\\ln(x) + \\frac{\\sin(x)}{x}$, so $y' = x^{\\sin(x)}\\left(\\cos(x)\\ln(x) + \\frac{\\sin(x)}{x}\\right)$.",
    "(c) The base is constant, so this is an exponential function: $y' = 2^x\\ln(2)$. (Logarithmic differentiation gives the same: $\\ln(y) = x\\ln(2)$, $\\frac{y'}{y} = \\ln(2)$.)",
    "(d) The exponent is constant, so use the power and chain rules: $y' = 3(\\arcsin(x))^2\\cdot\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$."],
   ans:"(a) $x^x(\\ln(x) + 1)$; (b) $x^{\\sin(x)}\\left(\\cos(x)\\ln(x) + \\frac{\\sin(x)}{x}\\right)$; (c) $2^x\\ln(2)$; (d) $\\frac{3(\\arcsin(x))^2}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$"},
  {id:"CN2.7-P3-e-g", src:"Course notes 2.7, End of Section Problem 3, parts (e) to (g)", lec:"L17", kind:"problem", page:"p. 122",
   q:"Find $y'$.<br>(e) $y = x^{\\ln(x)}$<br>(f) $y = (\\cos(x))^x$<br>(g) $y = (\\sin(x))^{\\ln(x)}$",
   sol:[
    "(e) $\\ln(y) = \\ln(x)\\ln(x) = (\\ln(x))^2$. $\\frac{y'}{y} = \\frac{2\\ln(x)}{x}$, so $y' = x^{\\ln(x)}\\cdot\\frac{2\\ln(x)}{x}$.",
    "(f) $\\ln(y) = x\\ln(\\cos(x))$. $\\frac{y'}{y} = \\ln(\\cos(x)) + x\\cdot\\frac{-\\sin(x)}{\\cos(x)} = \\ln(\\cos(x)) - x\\tan(x)$.",
    "$y' = (\\cos(x))^x\\left(\\ln(\\cos(x)) - x\\tan(x)\\right)$.",
    "(g) $\\ln(y) = \\ln(x)\\ln(\\sin(x))$. $\\frac{y'}{y} = \\frac{\\ln(\\sin(x))}{x} + \\ln(x)\\cdot\\frac{\\cos(x)}{\\sin(x)}$.",
    "$y' = (\\sin(x))^{\\ln(x)}\\left(\\frac{\\ln(\\sin(x))}{x} + \\ln(x)\\cot(x)\\right)$."],
   ans:"(e) $\\frac{2\\ln(x)}{x}\\,x^{\\ln(x)}$; (f) $(\\cos(x))^x\\left(\\ln(\\cos(x)) - x\\tan(x)\\right)$; (g) $(\\sin(x))^{\\ln(x)}\\left(\\frac{\\ln(\\sin(x))}{x} + \\ln(x)\\cot(x)\\right)$"},
  {id:"CN2.7-P4", src:"Course notes 2.7, End of Section Problem 4", lec:"L16", kind:"problem", page:"p. 122",
   q:"Verify that the implicitly defined function is a solution of the given differential equation.<br>(a) $y' = -\\frac{x}{y}$, $x^2 + y^2 = c^2$<br>(b) $y' = x^2y$, $\\ln(y) = \\frac{x^3}{3} + C$<br>(c) $y' = \\frac{6x^2}{2y + \\cos(y)}$, $y^2 + \\sin(y) = 2x^3 + C$",
   sol:[
    "(a) Differentiate $x^2 + y^2 = c^2$: $2x + 2y\\,y' = 0$, so $y' = \\frac{-2x}{2y} = -\\frac{x}{y}$. This matches the DE, so it is a solution.",
    "(b) Differentiate $\\ln(y) = \\frac{x^3}{3} + C$: $\\frac{y'}{y} = x^2$, so $y' = x^2y$. This matches, so it is a solution.",
    "(c) Differentiate $y^2 + \\sin(y) = 2x^3 + C$: $2y\\,y' + \\cos(y)\\,y' = 6x^2$, so $y' = \\frac{6x^2}{2y + \\cos(y)}$. This matches, so it is a solution."],
   ans:"All three are solutions."},
  {id:"CN2.7-P5", src:"Course notes 2.7, End of Section Problem 5", lec:"L16", kind:"problem", page:"p. 122",
   q:"Use implicit differentiation and the fact that $\\arccos(x)$ is the inverse of $\\cos(x)$ to show that $\\frac{d}{dx}\\arccos(x) = \\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$.",
   sol:[
    "Let $y = \\arccos(x)$. Then $\\cos(y) = x$ with $0 \\le y \\le \\pi$.",
    "Differentiate implicitly: $-\\sin(y)\\,y' = 1$, so $y' = -\\frac{1}{\\sin(y)} = -\\csc(y)$.",
    "Triangle for $\\cos(y) = \\frac{x}{1}$: $a = x$, $h = 1$, so $o^2 = 1 - x^2$ and $|o| = \\sqrt{1-x^2}$.",
    "For $0 < y < \\pi$, $\\sin(y) > 0$, so $o = \\sqrt{1-x^2}$ and $\\csc(y) = \\frac{h}{o} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$."],
   ans:"$\\frac{d}{dx}\\arccos(x) = -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$"},
  {id:"CN2.7-P6", src:"Course notes 2.7, End of Section Problem 6", lec:"L16", kind:"problem", page:"p. 122",
   q:"Given that $\\sec(x)$ is invertible on $\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\pi\\right]$, use implicit differentiation and the fact that $\\operatorname{arcsec}(x)$ is the inverse of $\\sec(x)$ on this interval to show that $\\frac{d}{dx}\\operatorname{arcsec}(x) = \\frac{1}{-x\\sqrt{x^2-1}}$.",
   sol:[
    "Let $y = \\operatorname{arcsec}(x)$. Then $\\sec(y) = x$ with $\\frac{\\pi}{2} < y \\le \\pi$, so $x \\le -1$.",
    "Differentiate implicitly: $\\sec(y)\\tan(y)\\,y' = 1$, so $y' = \\frac{1}{\\sec(y)\\tan(y)} = \\cos(y)\\cot(y)$.",
    "$\\cos(y) = \\frac{1}{\\sec(y)} = \\frac1x$.",
    "For $\\cot(y)$: $y$ is in quadrant 2, so the hypotenuse is positive and the adjacent side negative. Write $\\sec(y) = \\frac{x}{1} = \\frac{-x}{-1}$: $h = -x$, $a = -1$. Then $o^2 = h^2 - a^2 = x^2 - 1$, and $o = \\sqrt{x^2-1}$ since $\\sin(y) \\ge 0$ in quadrant 2.",
    "$\\cot(y) = \\frac{a}{o} = \\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{x^2-1}}$, so $y' = \\frac1x\\cdot\\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{x^2-1}}$."],
   ans:"$\\frac{d}{dx}\\operatorname{arcsec}(x) = \\frac{1}{-x\\sqrt{x^2-1}}$ (positive for $x < -1$)"},
  {id:"CN2.7-P7", src:"Course notes 2.7, End of Section Problem 7", lec:"L16", kind:"problem", page:"p. 122",
   q:"Kepler's equation describes the mean anomaly $M$ in terms of the eccentric anomaly $E$ and the eccentricity of the orbit $e$ by $M = E - e\\sin(E)$. Solving for $E$ in terms of $M$ cannot be done without approximation techniques. Use implicit differentiation to find $\\frac{dE}{dM}$.",
   sol:[
    "Treat $E$ as a function of $M$ ($e$ is a constant) and differentiate with respect to $M$: $1 = E' - e\\cos(E)\\,E'$.",
    "Factor: $1 = E'(1 - e\\cos(E))$."],
   ans:"$\\frac{dE}{dM} = \\frac{1}{1 - e\\cos(E)}$"},
  {id:"CN2-ECP1-a-d", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 1, parts (a) to (d)", lec:"L15", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 123",
   q:"Determine the derivative of the following functions.<br>(a) $f(x) = x^2 + \\sqrt x$<br>(b) $f(x) = \\cos(2x)$<br>(c) $f(x) = |\\tan(x)|$<br>(d) $f(x) = e^{x^2}$",
   sol:[
    "(a) Power rule: $f'(x) = 2x + \\frac12x^{-1/2} = 2x + \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt x}$.",
    "(b) Chain rule: $f'(x) = -\\sin(2x)\\cdot 2 = -2\\sin(2x)$.",
    "(c) Use $\\frac{d}{dx}|u| = \\frac{u}{|u|}u'$ with $u = \\tan(x)$: $f'(x) = \\frac{\\tan(x)}{|\\tan(x)|}\\sec^2(x)$ (where $\\tan(x) \\ne 0$).",
    "(d) Chain rule: $f'(x) = e^{x^2}\\cdot 2x$."],
   ans:"(a) $2x + \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt x}$; (b) $-2\\sin(2x)$; (c) $\\frac{\\tan(x)}{|\\tan(x)|}\\sec^2(x)$; (d) $2xe^{x^2}$"},
  {id:"CN2-ECP1-e-h", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 1, parts (e) to (h)", lec:"L15", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 123",
   q:"Determine the derivative of the following functions.<br>(e) $f(x) = \\frac{1}{3-2x}$<br>(f) $f(x) = \\ln(2x+5)$<br>(g) $f(x) = \\int_0^x e^{-t^2}\\,dt$<br>(h) $f(x) = \\arcsin(x^2+1)$",
   sol:[
    "(e) $f(x) = (3-2x)^{-1}$, so $f'(x) = -(3-2x)^{-2}\\cdot(-2) = 2(3-2x)^{-2}$.",
    "(f) Chain rule: $f'(x) = \\frac{1}{2x+5}\\cdot 2$.",
    "(g) Fundamental Theorem of Calculus (derivative of an integral function): $f'(x) = e^{-x^2}$.",
    "(h) Chain rule with $\\frac{d}{du}\\arcsin(u) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^2}}$, $u = x^2+1$: $f'(x) = \\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{1-(x^2+1)^2}}$.",
    "Simplified: $1 - (x^2+1)^2 = -x^4 - 2x^2$, so $f'(x) = \\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{-x^4-2x^2}}$."],
   ans:"(e) $\\frac{2}{(3-2x)^2}$; (f) $\\frac{2}{2x+5}$; (g) $e^{-x^2}$; (h) $\\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{1-(x^2+1)^2}}$",
   note:"(h) The official solution prints $\\frac{2x}{1+(x^2+1)^2}$, which is the derivative of $\\arctan(x^2+1)$, not $\\arcsin(x^2+1)$. The answer above is the chain rule result, checked with sympy. Also, $x^2 + 1 \\ge 1$ with equality only at $x = 0$, so $\\arcsin(x^2+1)$ is only defined at $x = 0$ and the derivative formula is not defined for any real $x$. This is the same function as Exercise 2.5.30(b)."},
  {id:"CN2-ECP1-i-l", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 1, parts (i) to (l)", lec:"L15", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 123",
   q:"Determine the derivative of the following functions.<br>(i) $f(x) = \\int_0^x\\sqrt{t^3+t}\\,dt$<br>(j) $f(x) = \\sec(3-x)$<br>(k) $f(x) = \\int_{-1}^{3x+2}\\ln(t^2+1)\\,dt$<br>(l) $f(x) = x\\ln(2x+1)$",
   sol:[
    "(i) FTC: $f'(x) = \\sqrt{x^3+x}$.",
    "(j) Chain rule: $f'(x) = \\sec(3-x)\\tan(3-x)\\cdot(-1)$.",
    "(k) FTC with the chain rule: $f'(x) = \\ln\\left((3x+2)^2+1\\right)\\cdot 3$.",
    "(l) Product rule: $f'(x) = \\ln(2x+1) + x\\cdot\\frac{2}{2x+1}$."],
   ans:"(i) $\\sqrt{x^3+x}$; (j) $-\\sec(3-x)\\tan(3-x)$; (k) $3\\ln\\left((3x+2)^2+1\\right)$; (l) $\\ln(2x+1) + \\frac{2x}{2x+1}$"},
  {id:"CN2-ECP1-m-p", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 1, parts (m) to (p)", lec:"L15", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 123",
   q:"Determine the derivative of the following functions.<br>(m) $f(x) = \\frac{x+2}{|x|}$<br>(n) $f(x) = xe^{2x}$<br>(o) $f(x) = \\frac{\\sin(x)}{x^2}$<br>(p) $f(x) = \\int_0^{\\tan(x)+\\ln(x)}(t^2+t)\\,dt$",
   sol:[
    "(m) Quotient rule with $\\frac{d}{dx}|x| = \\frac{x}{|x|}$: $f'(x) = \\frac{|x| - (x+2)\\frac{x}{|x|}}{|x|^2}$.",
    "Multiply top and bottom by $|x|$ and use $|x|^2 = x^2$: $f'(x) = \\frac{x^2 - x^2 - 2x}{|x|^3} = \\frac{-2x}{|x|^3}$, for $x \\ne 0$.",
    "(n) Product rule: $f'(x) = e^{2x} + x\\cdot 2e^{2x}$.",
    "(o) Quotient rule: $f'(x) = \\frac{x^2\\cos(x) - 2x\\sin(x)}{x^4} = \\frac{x\\cos(x) - 2\\sin(x)}{x^3}$.",
    "(p) FTC with the chain rule, upper limit $u = \\tan(x) + \\ln(x)$, $u' = \\sec^2(x) + \\frac1x$:",
    "$f'(x) = \\left((\\tan(x)+\\ln(x))^2 + \\tan(x) + \\ln(x)\\right)\\left(\\sec^2(x) + \\frac1x\\right)$."],
   ans:"(m) $\\frac{-2x}{|x|^3}$; (n) $e^{2x} + 2xe^{2x}$; (o) $\\frac{x\\cos(x) - 2\\sin(x)}{x^3}$; (p) $\\left((\\tan(x)+\\ln(x))^2 + \\tan(x)+\\ln(x)\\right)\\left(\\sec^2(x) + \\frac1x\\right)$"},
  {id:"CN2-ECP1-q-t", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 1, parts (q) to (t)", lec:"L15", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 123",
   q:"Determine the derivative of the following functions.<br>(q) $f(x) = x^{2/3}\\csc(x)$<br>(r) $f(x) = \\ln(|\\csc(x)|)$<br>(s) $f(x) = \\arccos(\\sqrt x)$<br>(t) $f(x) = \\cot(1-3x^2)$",
   sol:[
    "(q) Product rule: $f'(x) = \\frac23x^{-1/3}\\csc(x) + x^{2/3}(-\\csc(x)\\cot(x)) = \\frac{2\\csc(x) - 3x\\csc(x)\\cot(x)}{3x^{1/3}}$.",
    "(r) $\\frac{d}{dx}\\ln(|u|) = \\frac{u'}{u}$ with $u = \\csc(x)$: $f'(x) = \\frac{-\\csc(x)\\cot(x)}{\\csc(x)} = -\\cot(x)$.",
    "(s) Chain rule: $f'(x) = -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(\\sqrt x)^2}}\\cdot\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt x} = -\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt x\\sqrt{1-x}}$.",
    "(t) Chain rule: $f'(x) = -\\csc^2(1-3x^2)\\cdot(-6x) = 6x\\csc^2(1-3x^2)$."],
   ans:"(q) $\\frac{2\\csc(x) - 3x\\csc(x)\\cot(x)}{3x^{1/3}}$; (r) $-\\cot(x)$; (s) $-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt x\\sqrt{1-x}}$; (t) $6x\\csc^2(1-3x^2)$"},
  {id:"CN2-ECP1-u-x", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 1, parts (u) to (x)", lec:"L15", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 123",
   q:"Determine the derivative of the following functions.<br>(u) $f(x) = \\int_{-2}^{x^2+x+1}\\arctan(t)\\,dt$<br>(v) $f(x) = \\arctan(\\ln(x))$<br>(w) $f(x) = \\frac{\\arcsin(x)}{x^{1/3}}$<br>(x) $f(x) = \\int_0^{\\arccos(x)+2x}t\\sin(t)\\,dt$",
   sol:[
    "(u) FTC with the chain rule: $f'(x) = \\arctan(x^2+x+1)\\cdot(2x+1)$.",
    "(v) Chain rule: $f'(x) = \\frac{1}{1+(\\ln(x))^2}\\cdot\\frac1x$.",
    "(w) Quotient rule: $f'(x) = \\frac{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}x^{1/3} - \\arcsin(x)\\cdot\\frac13x^{-2/3}}{x^{2/3}}$.",
    "Multiply top and bottom by $3x^{2/3}\\sqrt{1-x^2}$: $f'(x) = \\frac{3x - \\sqrt{1-x^2}\\arcsin(x)}{3x^{4/3}\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$.",
    "(x) FTC with the chain rule, upper limit $u = \\arccos(x) + 2x$, $u' = -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}} + 2$:",
    "$f'(x) = (\\arccos(x)+2x)\\sin(\\arccos(x)+2x)\\left(2 - \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}\\right)$."],
   ans:"(u) $(2x+1)\\arctan(x^2+x+1)$; (v) $\\frac{1}{x(1+(\\ln(x))^2)}$; (w) $\\frac{3x - \\sqrt{1-x^2}\\arcsin(x)}{3x^{4/3}\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$; (x) $(\\arccos(x)+2x)\\sin(\\arccos(x)+2x)\\left(2 - \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}\\right)$"},
  {id:"CN2-ECP1-y-z", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 1, parts (y) to (z)", lec:"L15", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 123",
   q:"Determine the derivative of the following functions.<br>(y) $f(x) = \\frac{1}{(2-5x)^3}$<br>(z) $f(x) = \\tan(2x+5)$",
   sol:[
    "(y) $f(x) = (2-5x)^{-3}$, so $f'(x) = -3(2-5x)^{-4}\\cdot(-5) = 15(2-5x)^{-4}$.",
    "(z) Chain rule: $f'(x) = \\sec^2(2x+5)\\cdot 2$."],
   ans:"(y) $\\frac{15}{(2-5x)^4}$; (z) $2\\sec^2(2x+5)$"},
  {id:"CN2-ECP2-a-d", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 2, parts (a) to (d)", lec:"L15", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 123",
   q:"Evaluate the following integrals.<br>(a) $\\int(x^2 + \\sqrt x)\\,dx$<br>(b) $\\int\\frac1x\\,dx$<br>(c) $\\int\\frac{1}{x^2}\\,dx$<br>(d) $\\int 2\\sin(x)\\,dx$",
   sol:[
    "(a) Power rule for antiderivatives: $\\frac13x^3 + \\frac{x^{3/2}}{3/2} + C = \\frac13x^3 + \\frac23x^{3/2} + C$.",
    "(b) $\\frac{d}{dx}\\ln(|x|) = \\frac1x$ for $x \\ne 0$, so the antiderivative is $\\ln(|x|) + C$.",
    "(c) $\\int x^{-2}\\,dx = \\frac{x^{-1}}{-1} + C = -\\frac1x + C$.",
    "(d) $\\int\\sin(x)\\,dx = -\\cos(x) + C$, so the answer is $-2\\cos(x) + C$."],
   ans:"(a) $\\frac13x^3 + \\frac23x^{3/2} + C$; (b) $\\ln(|x|) + C$; (c) $-\\frac1x + C$; (d) $-2\\cos(x) + C$"},
  {id:"CN2-ECP2-e-h", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 2, parts (e) to (h)", lec:"L15", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 123",
   q:"Evaluate the following integrals.<br>(e) $\\int 5\\cos(x)\\,dx$<br>(f) $\\int x^{-2/3}\\,dx$<br>(g) $\\int 4e^{-x}\\,dx$<br>(h) $\\int_{-2}^{-1}\\frac2x\\,dx$",
   sol:[
    "(e) $\\int\\cos(x)\\,dx = \\sin(x) + C$, so the answer is $5\\sin(x) + C$.",
    "(f) $\\frac{x^{1/3}}{1/3} + C = 3x^{1/3} + C$.",
    "(g) $\\frac{d}{dx}(-e^{-x}) = e^{-x}$, so the answer is $-4e^{-x} + C$.",
    "(h) $2\\ln(|x|)\\Big|_{-2}^{-1} = 2\\ln(1) - 2\\ln(2) = -2\\ln(2)$."],
   ans:"(e) $5\\sin(x) + C$; (f) $3x^{1/3} + C$; (g) $-4e^{-x} + C$; (h) $-2\\ln(2)$"},
  {id:"CN2-ECP2-i-l", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 2, parts (i) to (l)", lec:"L15", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 123",
   q:"Evaluate the following integrals.<br>(i) $\\int_0^{\\pi/4}\\sec^2(x)\\,dx$<br>(j) $\\int_{-\\pi/6}^{\\pi/6}\\tan(x)\\,dx$<br>(k) $\\int\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}\\,dx$<br>(l) $\\int_0^1\\frac{1}{1+x^2}\\,dx$",
   sol:[
    "(i) $\\tan(x)\\Big|_0^{\\pi/4} = \\tan\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) - \\tan(0) = 1$.",
    "(j) $\\ln(|\\sec(x)|)\\Big|_{-\\pi/6}^{\\pi/6} = \\ln\\left(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt3}\\right) - \\ln\\left(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt3}\\right) = 0$, since $\\sec$ is even.",
    "(k) $\\frac{d}{dx}\\arcsin(x) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$, so the answer is $\\arcsin(x) + C$.",
    "(l) $\\arctan(x)\\Big|_0^1 = \\arctan(1) - \\arctan(0) = \\frac{\\pi}{4}$."],
   ans:"(i) $1$; (j) $0$; (k) $\\arcsin(x) + C$; (l) $\\frac{\\pi}{4}$"},
  {id:"CN2-ECP2-m-p", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 2, parts (m) to (p)", lec:"L15", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 123",
   q:"Evaluate the following integrals.<br>(m) $\\int\\sec(x)\\tan(x)\\,dx$<br>(n) $\\int_{2\\pi/3}^{5\\pi/6}\\cos(x)\\,dx$<br>(o) $\\int\\csc^2(x)\\,dx$<br>(p) $\\int_{-1}^1 -x^3\\,dx$",
   sol:[
    "(m) $\\frac{d}{dx}\\sec(x) = \\sec(x)\\tan(x)$, so the answer is $\\sec(x) + C$.",
    "(n) $\\sin(x)\\Big|_{2\\pi/3}^{5\\pi/6} = \\sin\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{6}\\right) - \\sin\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\frac12 - \\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}$.",
    "(o) $\\frac{d}{dx}(-\\cot(x)) = \\csc^2(x)$, so the answer is $-\\cot(x) + C$.",
    "(p) $-\\frac14x^4\\Big|_{-1}^1 = -\\frac14 - \\left(-\\frac14\\right) = 0$."],
   ans:"(m) $\\sec(x) + C$; (n) $\\frac12 - \\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}$; (o) $-\\cot(x) + C$; (p) $0$"},
  {id:"CN2-ECP2-q-t", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 2, parts (q) to (t)", lec:"L15", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 123",
   q:"Evaluate the following integrals.<br>(q) $\\int_1^4\\frac{1}{\\sqrt x}\\,dx$<br>(r) $\\int\\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}\\,dx$<br>(s) $\\int\\sin(3x)\\,dx$<br>(t) $\\int 3\\cos(2x)\\,dx$",
   sol:[
    "(q) $2\\sqrt x\\Big|_1^4 = 2\\sqrt4 - 2\\sqrt1 = 2$.",
    "(r) $\\frac{d}{dx}\\arccos(x) = \\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$, so the answer is $\\arccos(x) + C$.",
    "(s) Guess $-\\cos(3x)$: its derivative is $3\\sin(3x)$. So divide by 3: $-\\frac13\\cos(3x) + C$.",
    "(t) $\\frac{d}{dx}\\sin(2x) = 2\\cos(2x)$, so $\\int 3\\cos(2x)\\,dx = \\frac32\\sin(2x) + C$."],
   ans:"(q) $2$; (r) $\\arccos(x) + C$; (s) $-\\frac13\\cos(3x) + C$; (t) $\\frac32\\sin(2x) + C$"},
  {id:"CN2-ECP2-u", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 2, part (u)", lec:"L15", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 123",
   q:"Evaluate the following integrals.<br>(u) $\\int\\csc(2x)\\cot(2x)\\,dx$",
   sol:[
    "(u) $\\frac{d}{dx}\\csc(2x) = -2\\csc(2x)\\cot(2x)$, so the answer is $-\\frac12\\csc(2x) + C$."],
   ans:"(u) $-\\frac12\\csc(2x) + C$"},
  {id:"CN2-ECP3-a-d", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 3, parts (a) to (d)", lec:"L14", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 124",
   q:"Sketch the graph of<br>(a) $f(x) = 3\\sin(2x) + 1$<br>(b) $f(x) = -2\\cos\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)$<br>(c) $f(x) = \\frac12\\sin(\\pi x) - 1$<br>(d) $f(x) = \\arccos(x)$",
   sol:[
    "(a) Period $\\pi$, amplitude 3, centre line $y = 1$, range $[-2, 4]$.",
    "On $[0, 2\\pi]$ (two periods): starts at $(0, 1)$, max $4$ at $x = \\frac{\\pi}{4}, \\frac{5\\pi}{4}$, min $-2$ at $x = \\frac{3\\pi}{4}, \\frac{7\\pi}{4}$, crosses $y = 1$ at multiples of $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$.",
    "(b) Period $6\\pi$, amplitude 2, reflected cosine, range $[-2, 2]$.",
    "Key points on $[0, 6\\pi]$: $(0, -2)$, $\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{2}, 0\\right)$, $(3\\pi, 2)$, $\\left(\\frac{9\\pi}{2}, 0\\right)$, $(6\\pi, -2)$.",
    "(c) Period $\\frac{2\\pi}{\\pi} = 2$, amplitude $\\frac12$, centre line $y = -1$, range $\\left[-\\frac32, -\\frac12\\right]$.",
    "Key points on $[0, 2]$: $(0, -1)$, $\\left(\\frac12, -\\frac12\\right)$, $(1, -1)$, $\\left(\\frac32, -\\frac32\\right)$, $(2, -1)$.",
    "(d) Domain $[-1, 1]$, range $[0, \\pi]$, decreasing.",
    "Key points: $(-1, \\pi)$, $\\left(0, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$, $(1, 0)$."],
   ans:"(a) sine wave of period $\\pi$ between $-2$ and $4$; (b) reflected cosine of period $6\\pi$ between $-2$ and $2$; (c) sine wave of period 2 between $-\\frac32$ and $-\\frac12$; (d) decreasing from $(-1, \\pi)$ through $\\left(0, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$ to $(1, 0)$",
   note:"(c) The official plot labels the $x$-axis with $\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\pi, \\frac{3\\pi}{2}, 2\\pi$, but the period is 2, not $2\\pi$; use the key points above."},
  {id:"CN2-ECP3-e-h", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 3, parts (e) to (h)", lec:"L14", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 124",
   q:"Sketch the graph of<br>(e) $f(x) = \\arctan(x)$<br>(f) $f(x) = \\begin{cases} x+1 & -2 < x < 0 \\\\ 3 & x = 0 \\\\ 3-x & 0 < x < 3 \\end{cases}$<br>(g) $f(x) = \\begin{cases} \\cos(x) & -\\pi \\le x < 0 \\\\ \\sin(x) & 0 \\le x \\le \\pi \\end{cases}$<br>(h) $f(x) = \\begin{cases} e^x & -2 < x \\le 0 \\\\ \\ln(x) & 0 < x < 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{x+1} & 1 < x \\le 3 \\end{cases}$",
   sol:[
    "(e) Domain $\\mathbb{R}$, range $\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$, increasing through the origin, horizontal asymptotes $y = \\pm\\frac{\\pi}{2}$.",
    "Key points: $\\left(-1, -\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$, $(0, 0)$, $\\left(1, \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$.",
    "(f) Segment of $y = x + 1$ from an open circle at $(-2, -1)$ to an open circle at $(0, 1)$.",
    "A filled point at $(0, 3)$.",
    "Segment of $y = 3 - x$ from $(0, 3)$ (the filled point) down to an open circle at $(3, 0)$.",
    "(g) On $[-\\pi, 0)$: $\\cos(x)$ rising from a filled point $(-\\pi, -1)$ through $\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, 0\\right)$ to an open circle at $(0, 1)$.",
    "On $[0, \\pi]$: $\\sin(x)$ from a filled point $(0, 0)$ up to $\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}, 1\\right)$ and down to a filled point $(\\pi, 0)$.",
    "(h) On $(-2, 0]$: $e^x$ from an open circle at $(-2, e^{-2})$ up to a filled point $(0, 1)$.",
    "On $(0, 1)$: $\\ln(x)$, which goes to $-\\infty$ as $x \\to 0^+$ and rises to an open circle at $(1, 0)$.",
    "On $(1, 3]$: $\\sqrt{x+1}$ from an open circle at $(1, \\sqrt2)$ to a filled point $(3, 2)$. $f(1)$ is not defined."],
   ans:"(e) increasing through the origin with asymptotes $y = \\pm\\frac{\\pi}{2}$; (f) two line segments with a jump at $x = 0$, where $f(0) = 3$; (g) cosine arc then sine arch, with a jump from 1 down to 0 at $x = 0$; (h) three pieces; vertical asymptote $x = 0$ from the right, $f(1)$ undefined"},
  {id:"CN2-ECP4", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 4", lec:"L07", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 124",
   q:"If $x > 0$, show that $\\frac{\\sqrt{x^2+1}}{x} = \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{1}{x^2}}$.",
   sol:[
    "Factor $x^2$ out of the radicand: $\\frac{\\sqrt{x^2+1}}{x} = \\frac{\\sqrt{x^2\\left(1 + \\frac{1}{x^2}\\right)}}{x} = \\frac{\\sqrt{x^2}\\sqrt{1 + \\frac{1}{x^2}}}{x}$.",
    "$\\sqrt{x^2} = |x|$, so this is $\\frac{|x|\\sqrt{1 + \\frac{1}{x^2}}}{x}$.",
    "Since $x > 0$, $|x| = x$, so the expression equals $\\sqrt{1 + \\frac{1}{x^2}}$."],
   ans:"Shown, using $|x| = x$ for $x > 0$."},
  {id:"CN2-ECP5", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 5", lec:"L07", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 124",
   q:"If $x < 0$, show that $\\frac{\\sqrt{x^2+1}}{x} = -\\sqrt{1 + \\frac{1}{x^2}}$.",
   sol:[
    "As in Problem 4: $\\frac{\\sqrt{x^2+1}}{x} = \\frac{\\sqrt{x^2}\\sqrt{1 + \\frac{1}{x^2}}}{x} = \\frac{|x|\\sqrt{1 + \\frac{1}{x^2}}}{x}$.",
    "Since $x < 0$, $|x| = -x$, so this is $\\frac{-x\\sqrt{1 + \\frac{1}{x^2}}}{x} = -\\sqrt{1 + \\frac{1}{x^2}}$."],
   ans:"Shown, using $|x| = -x$ for $x < 0$."},
  {id:"CN2-ECP6", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 6", lec:"L07", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 124",
   q:"Show that $\\frac{\\sqrt{x^2+5x}}{x^2} = \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{x^2} + \\frac{5}{x^3}}$ (for $x \\ne 0$ where both sides are defined).",
   sol:[
    "Factor $x^4$ out of the radicand: $\\frac{\\sqrt{x^2+5x}}{x^2} = \\frac{\\sqrt{x^4\\left(\\frac{1}{x^2} + \\frac{5}{x^3}\\right)}}{x^2} = \\frac{\\sqrt{x^4}\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{x^2} + \\frac{5}{x^3}}}{x^2}$.",
    "$\\sqrt{x^4} = |x^2| = x^2$ since $x^2 > 0$.",
    "So the expression is $\\frac{x^2\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{x^2} + \\frac{5}{x^3}}}{x^2} = \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{x^2} + \\frac{5}{x^3}}$."],
   ans:"Shown, using $\\sqrt{x^4} = x^2$."},
  {id:"CN2-ECP7", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 7", lec:"L08", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 124",
   q:"Let $f(x) = \\int_0^x g(t)\\,dt$. The table gives $g(0) = \\frac13$, $g\\left(\\frac23\\right) = 1$, $g\\left(\\frac43\\right) = \\frac12$. Use it to approximate $f(2)$ using 3 subdivisions.",
   sol:[
    "$f(2) = \\int_0^2 g(t)\\,dt$. With 3 subdivisions, $\\Delta t = \\frac{2 - 0}{3} = \\frac23$, and the left endpoints are $0, \\frac23, \\frac43$.",
    "$f(2) \\approx g(0)\\Delta t + g\\left(\\frac23\\right)\\Delta t + g\\left(\\frac43\\right)\\Delta t = \\frac13\\cdot\\frac23 + 1\\cdot\\frac23 + \\frac12\\cdot\\frac23$.",
    "$= \\frac29 + \\frac69 + \\frac39 = \\frac{11}{9}$."],
   ans:"$f(2) \\approx \\frac{11}{9}$"},
  {id:"CN2-ECP8", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 8", lec:"L08", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 124",
   q:"Let $f(x) = \\int_0^x g(t)\\,dt$. The table gives $g(0) = 0.4$, $g(0.5) = 0.6$, $g(1) = 1.0$, $g(1.5) = 1.4$. Use it to approximate $f(2)$ using 4 subdivisions.",
   sol:[
    "$\\Delta t = \\frac{2 - 0}{4} = \\frac12$, left endpoints $0, 0.5, 1, 1.5$.",
    "$f(2) \\approx (0.4 + 0.6 + 1.0 + 1.4)\\cdot\\frac12 = 3.4\\cdot\\frac12 = 1.7$."],
   ans:"$f(2) \\approx \\frac{17}{10} = 1.7$"},
  {id:"CN2-ECP9", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 9", lec:"L08", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 124",
   q:"Let $f(x) = \\int_2^x g(t)\\,dt$. The table gives $g(2) = -3$, $g(2.5) = -1.5$, $g(3) = 0.5$, $g(3.5) = 0$. Use it to approximate $f(4)$ using 4 subdivisions.",
   sol:[
    "$f(4) = \\int_2^4 g(t)\\,dt$. $\\Delta t = \\frac{4 - 2}{4} = \\frac12$, left endpoints $2, 2.5, 3, 3.5$.",
    "$f(4) \\approx (-3 - 1.5 + 0.5 + 0)\\cdot\\frac12 = (-4)\\cdot\\frac12 = -2$."],
   ans:"$f(4) \\approx -2$"},
  {id:"CN2-ECP10-a-d", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 10, parts (a) to (d)", lec:"L07", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 124",
   q:"Find all values of $x$ satisfying<br>(a) $|x| = 0$<br>(b) $|x| = 5$<br>(c) $|x| < \\frac23$<br>(d) $|x| > 2$",
   sol:[
    "(a) $|x| = 0$ only when $x = 0$.",
    "(b) $|x| = 5$ means $x = 5$ or $x = -5$.",
    "(c) $|x| < a$ means $-a < x < a$.",
    "(d) $|x| > a$ means $x < -a$ or $x > a$."],
   ans:"(a) $x = 0$; (b) $x = \\pm5$; (c) $-\\frac23 < x < \\frac23$; (d) $x < -2$ or $x > 2$"},
  {id:"CN2-ECP10-e-h", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 10, parts (e) to (h)", lec:"L07", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 124",
   q:"Find all values of $x$ satisfying<br>(e) $|x+1| < \\frac12$<br>(f) $|x-1| > 1$<br>(g) $2|x-2| < 1$<br>(h) $3|x+2| \\le 1$",
   sol:[
    "(e) $-\\frac12 < x + 1 < \\frac12$. Subtract 1: $-\\frac32 < x < -\\frac12$.",
    "(f) $x - 1 < -1$ or $x - 1 > 1$, so $x < 0$ or $x > 2$.",
    "(g) $|x - 2| < \\frac12$, so $-\\frac12 < x - 2 < \\frac12$, and adding 2: $\\frac32 < x < \\frac52$.",
    "(h) $|x + 2| \\le \\frac13$, so $-\\frac13 \\le x + 2 \\le \\frac13$, and subtracting 2: $-\\frac73 \\le x \\le -\\frac53$."],
   ans:"(e) $-\\frac32 < x < -\\frac12$; (f) $x < 0$ or $x > 2$; (g) $\\frac32 < x < \\frac52$; (h) $-\\frac73 \\le x \\le -\\frac53$"},
  {id:"CN2-ECP10-i", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 10, part (i)", lec:"L07", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 124",
   q:"Find all values of $x$ satisfying<br>(i) $4|x-3| \\ge 2$",
   sol:[
    "(i) $|x - 3| \\ge \\frac12$, so $x - 3 \\le -\\frac12$ or $x - 3 \\ge \\frac12$, giving $x \\le \\frac52$ or $x \\ge \\frac72$."],
   ans:"(i) $x \\le \\frac52$ or $x \\ge \\frac72$"},
  {id:"CN2-ECP11", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 11", lec:"L14", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 124",
   q:"Find the inverse of each function on the given interval. State the domain and the range of the inverse.<br>(a) $f(x) = x^2 + 2$ on $[0, \\infty)$<br>(b) $f(x) = x^2 + 2$ on $(-\\infty, 0)$<br>(c) $f(x) = e^{2x+1}$ on $(-\\infty, \\infty)$<br>(d) $f(x) = \\ln(x-1)$ on $(1, \\infty)$<br>(e) $f(x) = 2\\tan(3x)$ on $\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{6}, \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)$",
   sol:[
    "Method: write $y = f(x)$ and solve for $x$. The domain of $f^{-1}$ is the range of $f$ on the interval, and the range of $f^{-1}$ is the interval.",
    "(a) $y = x^2 + 2 \\Rightarrow y - 2 = x^2 \\Rightarrow |x| = \\sqrt{y-2} \\Rightarrow x = \\sqrt{y-2}$ since $x \\ge 0$. For $0 \\le x < \\infty$, $2 \\le f(x) < \\infty$.",
    "$f^{-1}(y) = \\sqrt{y-2}$, domain $[2, \\infty)$, range $[0, \\infty)$.",
    "(b) Same steps give $|x| = \\sqrt{y-2}$, and $|x| = -x$ since $x < 0$, so $x = -\\sqrt{y-2}$. For $-\\infty < x < 0$, $2 < f(x) < \\infty$.",
    "$f^{-1}(y) = -\\sqrt{y-2}$, domain $(2, \\infty)$, range $(-\\infty, 0)$.",
    "(c) $y = e^{2x+1} \\Rightarrow \\ln(y) = 2x + 1 \\Rightarrow x = \\frac12(\\ln(y) - 1)$. $e^{2x+1}$ takes every value in $(0, \\infty)$.",
    "$f^{-1}(y) = \\frac12(\\ln(y) - 1)$, domain $(0, \\infty)$, range $(-\\infty, \\infty)$.",
    "(d) $y = \\ln(x - 1) \\Rightarrow e^y = x - 1 \\Rightarrow x = e^y + 1$. For $1 < x < \\infty$, $\\ln(x-1)$ takes every real value.",
    "$f^{-1}(y) = e^y + 1$, domain $(-\\infty, \\infty)$, range $(1, \\infty)$.",
    "(e) $y = 2\\tan(3x) \\Rightarrow \\frac{y}{2} = \\tan(3x)$. Since $3x \\in \\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$, $3x = \\arctan\\left(\\frac{y}{2}\\right)$, so $x = \\frac13\\arctan\\left(\\frac y2\\right)$. $2\\tan(3x)$ takes every real value on this interval.",
    "$f^{-1}(y) = \\frac13\\arctan\\left(\\frac{y}{2}\\right)$, domain $(-\\infty, \\infty)$, range $\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{6}, \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)$."],
   ans:"(a) $\\sqrt{y-2}$, domain $[2,\\infty)$, range $[0,\\infty)$; (b) $-\\sqrt{y-2}$, domain $(2,\\infty)$, range $(-\\infty,0)$; (c) $\\frac12(\\ln(y)-1)$, domain $(0,\\infty)$, range $\\mathbb{R}$; (d) $e^y + 1$, domain $\\mathbb{R}$, range $(1,\\infty)$; (e) $\\frac13\\arctan\\left(\\frac y2\\right)$, domain $\\mathbb{R}$, range $\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{6}, \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)$"},
  {id:"CN2-ECP12-a-d", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 12, parts (a) to (d)", lec:"L17", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 124",
   q:"Use implicit differentiation to find the equation of the tangent line at the given point.<br>(a) $x^2 + 2y^2 = 9$ at $(-1, 2)$<br>(b) $x = \\sin(y)$ at $(0, \\pi)$<br>(c) $2x + xy = y$ at $(2, -4)$<br>(d) $y = x^{2x+1}$ at $(2, 32)$",
   sol:[
    "(a) $2x + 4y\\,y' = 0$. At $(-1, 2)$: $-2 + 8y' = 0$, $y' = \\frac14$.",
    "Tangent: $y = 2 + \\frac14(x + 1)$.",
    "(b) $1 = \\cos(y)\\,y'$. At $(0, \\pi)$: $1 = \\cos(\\pi)\\,y' = -y'$, $y' = -1$.",
    "Tangent: $y = \\pi + (-1)(x - 0)$.",
    "(c) $2 + y + xy' = y'$. At $(2, -4)$: $2 - 4 + 2y' = y'$, so $y' = 2$.",
    "Tangent: $y = -4 + 2(x - 2)$.",
    "(d) Take $\\ln$: $\\ln(y) = (2x+1)\\ln(x)$. Differentiate: $\\frac1y\\,y' = 2\\ln(x) + \\frac{2x+1}{x}$.",
    "At $(2, 32)$: $\\frac{1}{32}y' = 2\\ln(2) + \\frac52$, so $y' = 64\\ln(2) + 80$.",
    "Tangent: $y = 32 + (64\\ln(2) + 80)(x - 2)$."],
   ans:"(a) $y = 2 + \\frac14(x+1)$; (b) $y = \\pi - x$; (c) $y = -4 + 2(x-2)$; (d) $y = 32 + (64\\ln(2) + 80)(x-2)$"},
  {id:"CN2-ECP12-e-h", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 12, parts (e) to (h)", lec:"L17", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 124",
   q:"Use implicit differentiation to find the equation of the tangent line at the given point.<br>(e) $\\frac{x}{y} + 1 = y^2$ at $(0, 1)$<br>(f) $y^2e^x = 1$ at $(0, -1)$<br>(g) $y = x^{\\sin(x)}$ at $(\\pi, 1)$<br>(h) $y = (\\ln(x))^x$ at $(2, (\\ln(2))^2)$",
   sol:[
    "(e) $\\frac{y - xy'}{y^2} = 2y\\,y'$. At $(0, 1)$: $\\frac{1 - 0}{1} = 2y'$, so $y' = \\frac12$.",
    "Tangent: $y = 1 + \\frac12x$.",
    "(f) Product rule: $2y\\,y'e^x + y^2e^x = 0$. At $(0, -1)$: $-2y' + 1 = 0$, so $y' = \\frac12$.",
    "Tangent: $y = -1 + \\frac12x$.",
    "(g) $\\ln(y) = \\sin(x)\\ln(x)$, so $\\frac1y\\,y' = \\cos(x)\\ln(x) + \\frac{\\sin(x)}{x}$.",
    "At $(\\pi, 1)$: $y' = 1\\cdot\\left(-\\ln(\\pi) + 0\\right) = -\\ln(\\pi)$.",
    "Tangent: $y = 1 - \\ln(\\pi)(x - \\pi)$.",
    "(h) $\\ln(y) = x\\ln(\\ln(x))$, so $\\frac1y\\,y' = \\ln(\\ln(x)) + x\\cdot\\frac{1}{\\ln(x)}\\cdot\\frac1x = \\ln(\\ln(x)) + \\frac{1}{\\ln(x)}$.",
    "At $x = 2$, $y = (\\ln(2))^2$: $y' = (\\ln(2))^2\\left(\\ln(\\ln(2)) + \\frac{1}{\\ln(2)}\\right) = (\\ln(2))^2\\ln(\\ln(2)) + \\ln(2)$.",
    "Tangent: $y = (\\ln(2))^2 + \\left((\\ln(2))^2\\ln(\\ln(2)) + \\ln(2)\\right)(x - 2)$."],
   ans:"(e) $y = 1 + \\frac12x$; (f) $y = -1 + \\frac12x$; (g) $y = 1 - \\ln(\\pi)(x - \\pi)$; (h) $y = (\\ln(2))^2 + \\left((\\ln(2))^2\\ln(\\ln(2)) + \\ln(2)\\right)(x-2)$"},
  {id:"CN2-ECP12-i", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 12, part (i)", lec:"L17", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 124",
   q:"Use implicit differentiation to find the equation of the tangent line at the given point.<br>(i) $y = \\cos(x)^{x^2}$ at $(0, 1)$",
   sol:[
    "(i) $\\ln(y) = x^2\\ln(\\cos(x))$, so $\\frac1y\\,y' = 2x\\ln(\\cos(x)) - x^2\\tan(x)$.",
    "At $(0, 1)$: $y' = 1\\cdot(0 - 0) = 0$.",
    "Tangent: $y = 1$."],
   ans:"(i) $y = 1$"},
  {id:"CN2-ECP13-a-d", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 13, parts (a) to (d)", lec:"L16", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 124",
   q:"Simplify<br>(a) $\\frac{(n-2)!}{n!}$<br>(b) $\\ln(x) + \\ln(x+1)$<br>(c) $e^{2\\ln(|x|)}$<br>(d) $\\ln(2x^2) - \\ln(x)$",
   sol:[
    "(a) $\\frac{1\\cdots(n-2)}{1\\cdots(n-2)(n-1)n} = \\frac{1}{(n-1)n}$.",
    "(b) $\\ln(a) + \\ln(b) = \\ln(ab)$: $\\ln(x(x+1))$.",
    "(c) $2\\ln(|x|) = \\ln(|x|^2)$, so $e^{2\\ln(|x|)} = e^{\\ln(|x|^2)} = |x|^2 = x^2$ (for $x \\ne 0$).",
    "(d) $\\ln(a) - \\ln(b) = \\ln\\left(\\frac ab\\right)$: $\\ln\\left(\\frac{2x^2}{x}\\right) = \\ln(2x)$ (for $x > 0$)."],
   ans:"(a) $\\frac{1}{n(n-1)}$; (b) $\\ln(x(x+1))$; (c) $x^2$, $x \\ne 0$; (d) $\\ln(2x)$"},
  {id:"CN2-ECP13-e-g", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 13, parts (e) to (g)", lec:"L16", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 124",
   q:"Simplify<br>(e) $\\frac{(n+1)!}{n!}$<br>(f) $(\\sqrt{x+1})^2$<br>(g) $e^{-\\ln(1/x)}$",
   sol:[
    "(e) $\\frac{1\\cdots n(n+1)}{1\\cdots n} = n + 1$.",
    "(f) $\\sqrt{x+1}$ is only defined for $x + 1 \\ge 0$, and then $(\\sqrt{x+1})^2 = x + 1$.",
    "(g) $-\\ln\\left(\\frac1x\\right) = -\\ln(x^{-1}) = \\ln(x)$, so $e^{-\\ln(1/x)} = e^{\\ln(x)} = x$ (for $x > 0$)."],
   ans:"(e) $n+1$; (f) $x + 1$, $x \\ge -1$; (g) $x$"},
  {id:"CN2-ECP14-a-d", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 14, parts (a) to (d)", lec:"L16", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 125",
   q:"Evaluate<br>(a) $e^{\\ln(3)}$<br>(b) $5!$<br>(c) $\\arcsin\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}\\right)$<br>(d) $\\arctan(\\sqrt3)$",
   sol:[
    "(a) $e^x$ and $\\ln(x)$ are inverses: $e^{\\ln(3)} = 3$.",
    "(b) $1\\cdot2\\cdot3\\cdot4\\cdot5 = 120$.",
    "(c) $\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}$ and $\\frac{\\pi}{3} \\in \\left[-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$.",
    "(d) $\\tan\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\sqrt3$ and $\\frac{\\pi}{3} \\in \\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$."],
   ans:"(a) $3$; (b) $120$; (c) $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$; (d) $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$"},
  {id:"CN2-ECP14-e-h", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 14, parts (e) to (h)", lec:"L14", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 125",
   q:"Evaluate<br>(e) $\\arccos(1)$<br>(f) $\\arcsin(-1)$<br>(g) $\\arccos\\left(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}\\right)$<br>(h) $\\ln(e^3)$",
   sol:[
    "(e) $\\cos(0) = 1$ and $0 \\in [0, \\pi]$.",
    "(f) $\\sin\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = -1$ and $-\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\in \\left[-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$.",
    "(g) We want $\\theta \\in [0, \\pi]$ with $\\cos(\\theta) = -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}$: quadrant 2, reference angle $\\frac{\\pi}{4}$, so $\\theta = \\frac{3\\pi}{4}$.",
    "(h) $\\ln(e^3) = 3$."],
   ans:"(e) $0$; (f) $-\\frac{\\pi}{2}$; (g) $\\frac{3\\pi}{4}$; (h) $3$"},
  {id:"CN2-ECP14-i-l", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 14, parts (i) to (l)", lec:"L16", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 125",
   q:"Evaluate<br>(i) $e^{4\\ln(2)}$<br>(j) $\\frac{5!}{3!}$<br>(k) $\\frac{6!}{4!\\cdot3!}$<br>(l) $e^{-\\ln(1/3)}$",
   sol:[
    "(i) $4\\ln(2) = \\ln(2^4) = \\ln(16)$, so $e^{4\\ln(2)} = 16$.",
    "(j) $\\frac{1\\cdot2\\cdot3\\cdot4\\cdot5}{1\\cdot2\\cdot3} = 4\\cdot5 = 20$.",
    "(k) $\\frac{6!}{4!} = 5\\cdot6 = 30$, and $\\frac{30}{3!} = \\frac{30}{6} = 5$.",
    "(l) $-\\ln\\left(\\frac13\\right) = \\ln(3)$, so $e^{-\\ln(1/3)} = e^{\\ln(3)} = 3$."],
   ans:"(i) $16$; (j) $20$; (k) $5$; (l) $3$"},
  {id:"CN2-ECP14-m-p", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 14, parts (m) to (p)", lec:"L14", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 125",
   q:"Evaluate<br>(m) $\\sin\\left(\\arcsin\\left(\\frac12\\right)\\right)$<br>(n) $\\arccos\\left(\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\right)$<br>(o) $\\sin(\\arctan(2))$<br>(p) $\\arcsin\\left(\\cos\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\right)\\right)$",
   sol:[
    "(m) $\\frac12$ is in the domain of arcsine, so $\\sin(\\arcsin(x)) = x$ gives $\\frac12$.",
    "(n) $\\frac{\\pi}{6} \\in [0, \\pi]$, the range of arccosine, so the functions cancel.",
    "(o) Let $\\theta = \\arctan(2)$, so $\\tan(\\theta) = \\frac21$ with $\\theta$ in quadrant 1: $o = 2$, $a = 1$, $h^2 = 4 + 1 = 5$, $h = \\sqrt5$.",
    "$\\sin(\\theta) = \\frac{o}{h} = \\frac{2}{\\sqrt5}$.",
    "(p) $\\cos\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\frac12$, and $\\arcsin\\left(-\\frac12\\right) = -\\frac{\\pi}{6}$."],
   ans:"(m) $\\frac12$; (n) $\\frac{\\pi}{6}$; (o) $\\frac{2}{\\sqrt5}$; (p) $-\\frac{\\pi}{6}$"},
  {id:"CN2-ECP14-q-r", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 14, parts (q) to (r)", lec:"L14", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 125",
   q:"Evaluate<br>(q) $\\arctan\\left(\\tan\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{6}\\right)\\right)$<br>(r) $\\cos\\left(\\arcsin\\left(\\frac13\\right)\\right)$",
   sol:[
    "(q) $\\frac{7\\pi}{6}$ is not in $\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$. $\\tan$ has period $\\pi$: $\\tan\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{6}\\right) = \\tan\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)$, and $\\frac{\\pi}{6}$ is in the principal interval.",
    "(r) Let $\\theta = \\arcsin\\left(\\frac13\\right)$, so $\\sin(\\theta) = \\frac13$: $o = 1$, $h = 3$, $a^2 = 9 - 1 = 8$. $\\theta$ is in quadrant 1, so $a = \\sqrt8$.",
    "$\\cos(\\theta) = \\frac{a}{h} = \\frac{\\sqrt8}{3}$."],
   ans:"(q) $\\frac{\\pi}{6}$; (r) $\\frac{\\sqrt8}{3} = \\frac{2\\sqrt2}{3}$"},
  {id:"CN2-ECP15", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 15", lec:"L10", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 125",
   q:"Use the definition $\\ln(x) = \\int_1^x\\frac1t\\,dt$ to approximate the following values using 3 subdivisions (left endpoints): (a) $\\ln(2)$ (b) $\\ln(4)$ (c) $\\ln(7)$.",
   sol:[
    "(a) $\\ln(2) = \\int_1^2\\frac1t\\,dt$, $\\Delta t = \\frac{2-1}{3} = \\frac13$, left endpoints $1, \\frac43, \\frac53$.",
    "$\\ln(2) \\approx \\left(1 + \\frac34 + \\frac35\\right)\\cdot\\frac13 = \\frac13 + \\frac14 + \\frac15 = \\frac{47}{60}$.",
    "(b) $\\ln(4) = \\int_1^4\\frac1t\\,dt$, $\\Delta t = 1$, left endpoints $1, 2, 3$: $\\ln(4) \\approx 1 + \\frac12 + \\frac13 = \\frac{11}{6}$.",
    "(c) $\\ln(7) = \\int_1^7\\frac1t\\,dt$, $\\Delta t = 2$, left endpoints $1, 3, 5$: $\\ln(7) \\approx \\left(1 + \\frac13 + \\frac15\\right)\\cdot 2 = \\frac{46}{15}$."],
   ans:"(a) $\\frac{47}{60} \\approx 0.783$; (b) $\\frac{11}{6} \\approx 1.833$; (c) $\\frac{46}{15} \\approx 3.067$",
   note:"These left sums overestimate because $\\frac1t$ is decreasing. The true values are $\\ln(2) \\approx 0.693$, $\\ln(4) \\approx 1.386$, $\\ln(7) \\approx 1.946$."},
  {id:"CN2-ECP16", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 16", lec:"L11", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 125",
   q:"Sound level in decibels is $D = 10\\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{I}{10^{-12}}\\right)$, where $I$ is the intensity. Find the intensity of each sound (as in Example 2.4.18) and use the intensities to determine how many times louder $D_1$ is than $D_2$: (a) $D_1 = 30$ decibels, $D_2 = 10$ decibels (b) $D_1 = 58$ decibels, $D_2 = 33$ decibels.",
   sol:[
    "(a) $30 = 10\\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{I_1}{10^{-12}}\\right) \\Rightarrow 3 = \\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{I_1}{10^{-12}}\\right) \\Rightarrow 10^3 = \\frac{I_1}{10^{-12}} \\Rightarrow I_1 = 10^{-9}$.",
    "$10 = 10\\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{I_2}{10^{-12}}\\right) \\Rightarrow 1 = \\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{I_2}{10^{-12}}\\right) \\Rightarrow I_2 = 10^{-11}$.",
    "$\\frac{I_1}{I_2} = \\frac{10^{-9}}{10^{-11}} = 10^2 = 100$.",
    "(b) $58 = 10\\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{I_1}{10^{-12}}\\right) \\Rightarrow 5.8 = \\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{I_1}{10^{-12}}\\right) \\Rightarrow I_1 = 10^{5.8}\\cdot10^{-12} = 10^{-6.2}$.",
    "$33 = 10\\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{I_2}{10^{-12}}\\right) \\Rightarrow I_2 = 10^{3.3}\\cdot10^{-12} = 10^{-8.7}$.",
    "$\\frac{I_1}{I_2} = \\frac{10^{-6.2}}{10^{-8.7}} = 10^{2.5}$."],
   ans:"(a) $I_1 = 10^{-9}$, $I_2 = 10^{-11}$, $100$ times as intense; (b) $I_1 = 10^{-6.2}$, $I_2 = 10^{-8.7}$, $10^{2.5} \\approx 316$ times as intense"},
  {id:"CN2-ECP17", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 17", lec:"L11", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 125",
   q:"The Richter magnitude of an earthquake is $\\log_{10}$ of its amplitude (relative to a fixed reference). How much larger is the amplitude of a magnitude 4.5 earthquake compared to a magnitude 2.5 earthquake?",
   sol:[
    "Let $A_1$ and $A_2$ be the amplitudes. $\\log_{10}(A_1) = 4.5$ and $\\log_{10}(A_2) = 2.5$, so $A_1 = 10^{4.5}$ and $A_2 = 10^{2.5}$.",
    "$\\frac{A_1}{A_2} = \\frac{10^{4.5}}{10^{2.5}} = 10^2$."],
   ans:"$100$ times larger"},
  {id:"CN2-ECP18", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 18", lec:"L12", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 125",
   q:"Given that $\\cos(\\theta) = \\frac13$ and $0 < \\theta < \\frac{\\pi}{2}$, find $\\sin(\\theta)$.",
   sol:[
    "$\\cos(\\theta) = \\frac{a}{h}$: $a = 1$, $h = 3$.",
    "$o^2 + 1 = 9 \\Rightarrow o^2 = 8 \\Rightarrow o = \\sqrt8$ (first quadrant).",
    "$\\sin(\\theta) = \\frac{o}{h} = \\frac{\\sqrt8}{3}$."],
   ans:"$\\sin(\\theta) = \\frac{\\sqrt8}{3} = \\frac{2\\sqrt2}{3}$"},
  {id:"CN2-ECP19", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 19", lec:"L12", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 125",
   q:"Given that $\\tan(\\theta) = \\frac32$ and $0 < \\theta < \\frac{\\pi}{2}$, find $\\cos(\\theta)$.",
   sol:[
    "$\\tan(\\theta) = \\frac{o}{a}$: $o = 3$, $a = 2$.",
    "$h^2 = 9 + 4 = 13$, $h = \\sqrt{13}$ (hypotenuse always positive).",
    "$\\cos(\\theta) = \\frac{a}{h} = \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}$."],
   ans:"$\\cos(\\theta) = \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}$"},
  {id:"CN2-ECP20", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 20", lec:"L12", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 125",
   q:"Given that $\\sin(\\theta) = \\frac{x}{3}$ and $-\\frac{\\pi}{2} < \\theta < \\frac{\\pi}{2}$, find $\\tan(\\theta)$.",
   sol:[
    "$\\sin(\\theta) = \\frac{o}{h}$: $o = x$, $h = 3$.",
    "$a^2 + x^2 = 9 \\Rightarrow a^2 = 9 - x^2 \\Rightarrow |a| = \\sqrt{9-x^2}$. Cosine is positive in quadrants 1 and 4, so $a = \\sqrt{9-x^2}$.",
    "$\\tan(\\theta) = \\frac{o}{a} = \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-x^2}}$."],
   ans:"$\\tan(\\theta) = \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-x^2}}$"},
  {id:"CN2-ECP21", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 21", lec:"L12", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 125",
   q:"Given that $\\sec(\\theta) = 2x$ and $0 < \\theta < \\frac{\\pi}{2}$, find $\\tan(\\theta)$.",
   sol:[
    "$\\sec(\\theta) = \\frac{h}{a} = \\frac{2x}{1}$: $h = 2x$, $a = 1$.",
    "$o^2 + 1 = 4x^2 \\Rightarrow o^2 = 4x^2 - 1 \\Rightarrow o = \\sqrt{4x^2-1}$ (first quadrant).",
    "$\\tan(\\theta) = \\frac{o}{a} = \\sqrt{4x^2-1}$."],
   ans:"$\\tan(\\theta) = \\sqrt{4x^2-1}$"},
  {id:"CN2-ECP22", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 22", lec:"L12", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 125",
   q:"Given that $\\tan(\\theta) = \\frac{x}{2}$ and $-\\frac{\\pi}{2} < \\theta < \\frac{\\pi}{2}$, find $\\sec(\\theta)$.",
   sol:[
    "$\\tan(\\theta) = \\frac{o}{a}$: $o = x$, $a = 2$ (cosine is positive on this interval, so the adjacent side is positive).",
    "$h^2 = x^2 + 4$, $h = \\sqrt{x^2+4}$ (hypotenuse always positive).",
    "$\\sec(\\theta) = \\frac{h}{a} = \\frac{\\sqrt{x^2+4}}{2}$."],
   ans:"$\\sec(\\theta) = \\frac{\\sqrt{x^2+4}}{2}$"},
  {id:"CN2-ECP23", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 23", lec:"L12", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 125",
   q:"Determine the maximum, minimum, average, amplitude, and period of<br>(a) $f(\\theta) = 2\\sin(\\theta) + 1$<br>(b) $f(\\theta) = \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\theta}{2}\\right) - 1$<br>(c) $f(\\theta) = -\\frac12\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\theta}{\\pi}\\right)$<br>(d) $f(\\theta) = 2\\sin(2\\theta) - 3$",
   sol:[
    "For $a\\sin(b\\theta) + d$ or $a\\cos(b\\theta) + d$: average $d$, amplitude $|a|$, max $d + |a|$, min $d - |a|$, period $\\frac{2\\pi}{|b|}$.",
    "(a) $a = 2$, $b = 1$, $d = 1$: amplitude 2, average 1, max 3, min $-1$, period $2\\pi$.",
    "(b) $a = 1$, $b = \\frac12$, $d = -1$: amplitude 1, average $-1$, max 0, min $-2$, period $4\\pi$.",
    "(c) $a = -\\frac12$, $b = \\frac{1}{\\pi}$, $d = 0$: amplitude $\\frac12$, average 0, max $\\frac12$, min $-\\frac12$, period $\\frac{2\\pi}{1/\\pi} = 2\\pi^2$.",
    "(d) $a = 2$, $b = 2$, $d = -3$: amplitude 2, average $-3$, max $-1$, min $-5$, period $\\pi$."],
   ans:"(a) max 3, min $-1$, avg 1, amp 2, period $2\\pi$; (b) max 0, min $-2$, avg $-1$, amp 1, period $4\\pi$; (c) max $\\frac12$, min $-\\frac12$, avg 0, amp $\\frac12$, period $2\\pi^2$; (d) max $-1$, min $-5$, avg $-3$, amp 2, period $\\pi$"},
  {id:"CN2-ECP24", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 24", lec:"L15", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 125",
   q:"Verify that the given function $y$ is a solution of the differential equation.<br>(a) $y = \\cos(x) + x\\sin(x)$ for $y' = x\\cos(x)$<br>(b) $y = 5e^{x^2/2}$ for $y' = xy$<br>(c) $y = -\\frac{x}{2} + \\frac14$ for $y' - 2y = x - 1$<br>(d) $y = -\\frac{x}{2} + \\frac14 + e^{2x}$ for $y' - 2y = x - 1$",
   sol:[
    "(a) $y' = -\\sin(x) + \\sin(x) + x\\cos(x) = x\\cos(x)$. This equals the right side, so $y$ is a solution.",
    "(b) $y' = 5e^{x^2/2}\\cdot\\frac{2x}{2} = 5xe^{x^2/2}$, and $xy = x\\cdot5e^{x^2/2}$. Equal, so $y$ is a solution.",
    "(c) $y' = -\\frac12$, so $y' - 2y = -\\frac12 - 2\\left(-\\frac{x}{2} + \\frac14\\right) = -\\frac12 + x - \\frac12 = x - 1$. Equal to the right side, so $y$ is a solution.",
    "(d) $y' = -\\frac12 + 2e^{2x}$, so $y' - 2y = -\\frac12 + 2e^{2x} - 2\\left(-\\frac{x}{2} + \\frac14 + e^{2x}\\right) = -\\frac12 + 2e^{2x} + x - \\frac12 - 2e^{2x} = x - 1$. Equal, so $y$ is a solution."],
   ans:"All four functions are solutions."},
  {id:"CN2-ECP25-a-d", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 25, parts (a) to (d)", lec:"L15", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 125",
   q:"Solve the initial value problem.<br>(a) $y' = 2x + 3$, $y(-1) = -3$<br>(b) $y' = \\cos(x)$, $y\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\right) = 1$<br>(c) $y' = \\frac1x$, $y(-2) = 4$<br>(d) $y' = x^2$, $y(-1) = 2$",
   sol:[
    "(a) $y = x^2 + 3x + C$. $-3 = 1 - 3 + C$, so $C = -1$.",
    "(b) $y = \\sin(x) + C$. $1 = \\sin\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\right) + C = \\frac{\\sqrt3}{2} + C$, so $C = 1 - \\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}$.",
    "(c) $y = \\ln(|x|) + C$. $4 = \\ln(|-2|) + C = \\ln(2) + C$, so $C = 4 - \\ln(2)$.",
    "(d) $y = \\frac13x^3 + C$. $2 = -\\frac13 + C$, so $C = \\frac73$."],
   ans:"(a) $y = x^2 + 3x - 1$; (b) $y = \\sin(x) + 1 - \\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}$; (c) $y = \\ln(|x|) + 4 - \\ln(2)$; (d) $y = \\frac13x^3 + \\frac73$"},
  {id:"CN2-ECP25-e-h", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 25, parts (e) to (h)", lec:"L15", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 125",
   q:"Solve the initial value problem.<br>(e) $y' = \\sin(x)$, $y\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{2}\\right) = -1$<br>(f) $y' = \\frac{x}{|x|}$, $y(1) = 3$<br>(g) $y' = 2^x$, $y(0) = \\sqrt2$<br>(h) $y' = \\sec(x)\\tan(x)$, $y\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = 1$",
   sol:[
    "(e) $y = -\\cos(x) + C$. $-1 = -\\cos\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{2}\\right) + C = 0 + C$, so $C = -1$.",
    "(f) $\\frac{d}{dx}|x| = \\frac{x}{|x|}$, so $y = |x| + C$. $3 = |1| + C$, so $C = 2$.",
    "(g) $\\frac{d}{dx}2^x = 2^x\\ln(2)$, so $y = \\frac{2^x}{\\ln(2)} + C$. $\\sqrt2 = \\frac{1}{\\ln(2)} + C$, so $C = \\sqrt2 - \\frac{1}{\\ln(2)}$.",
    "(h) $y = \\sec(x) + C$. $1 = \\sec\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) + C = \\sqrt2 + C$, so $C = 1 - \\sqrt2$."],
   ans:"(e) $y = -\\cos(x) - 1$; (f) $y = |x| + 2$; (g) $y = \\frac{2^x}{\\ln(2)} + \\sqrt2 - \\frac{1}{\\ln(2)}$; (h) $y = \\sec(x) + 1 - \\sqrt2$"},
  {id:"CN2-ECP25-i-j", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 25, parts (i) to (j)", lec:"L15", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 125",
   q:"Solve the initial value problem.<br>(i) $y' = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$, $y(1) = 0$<br>(j) $y' = \\frac2x$, $y(1) = -6$",
   sol:[
    "(i) $y = \\arcsin(x) + C$.",
    "Use $y(1) = 0$: $0 = \\arcsin(1) + C = \\frac{\\pi}{2} + C$, so $C = -\\frac{\\pi}{2}$.",
    "(j) $y = 2\\ln(|x|) + C = \\ln(x^2) + C$. $-6 = \\ln(1) + C$, so $C = -6$."],
   ans:"(i) $y = \\arcsin(x) - \\frac{\\pi}{2}$; (j) $y = \\ln(x^2) - 6$",
   note:"(i) The official solution substitutes $y(0) = 0$ instead of the stated $y(1) = 0$ and gets $y = \\arcsin(x)$. With the condition as printed, $y(1) = 0$, the answer is $y = \\arcsin(x) - \\frac{\\pi}{2}$ (checked with sympy). An equivalent form is $y = -\\arccos(x)$. Same slip as Problem 2.5.21(c)."},
  {id:"CN2-ECP26", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 26", lec:"L15", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 126",
   q:"Solve the initial value problem.<br>(a) $y' = \\tan(x)$, $y\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{6}\\right) = 0$<br>(b) $y' = \\sec^2(x)$, $y\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{6}\\right) = 0$<br>(c) $y' = \\frac{1}{1+x^2}$, $y(3) = 1$<br>(d) $y' = \\frac{1}{x\\ln(3)}$, $y(1) = -2$<br>(e) $y' = \\frac3x$, $y(-1) = \\pi$<br>(f) $y' = -\\cot(x)$, $y\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = 1$",
   sol:[
    "(a) $y = \\int\\tan(x)\\,dx = \\ln(|\\sec(x)|) + C$. $0 = \\ln\\left(\\left|\\sec\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{6}\\right)\\right|\\right) + C = \\ln\\left(\\left|-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt3}\\right|\\right) + C$, so $C = -\\ln\\left(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt3}\\right)$.",
    "$y = \\ln(|\\sec(x)|) - \\ln\\left(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt3}\\right)$.",
    "(b) $y = \\tan(x) + C$. $0 = \\tan\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{6}\\right) + C = -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt3} + C$, so $C = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt3}$. $y = \\tan(x) + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt3}$.",
    "(c) $y = \\arctan(x) + C$. $1 = \\arctan(3) + C$, so $C = 1 - \\arctan(3)$. $y = \\arctan(x) + 1 - \\arctan(3)$.",
    "(d) $\\int\\frac{1}{x\\ln(3)}\\,dx = \\frac{\\ln(|x|)}{\\ln(3)} + C = \\log_3(|x|) + C$. $-2 = \\log_3(1) + C$, so $C = -2$. $y = \\log_3(|x|) - 2$.",
    "(e) $y = 3\\ln(|x|) + C$. $\\pi = 3\\ln(|-1|) + C = C$. $y = 3\\ln(|x|) + \\pi$.",
    "(f) $\\frac{d}{dx}\\ln(|\\csc(x)|) = -\\cot(x)$ (Problem 2.5.19(n)), so $y = \\ln(|\\csc(x)|) + C$. $1 = \\ln\\left(\\csc\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\right) + C = \\ln(1) + C$, so $C = 1$. $y = \\ln(|\\csc(x)|) + 1$."],
   ans:"(a) $y = \\ln(|\\sec(x)|) - \\ln\\left(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt3}\\right)$; (b) $y = \\tan(x) + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt3}$; (c) $y = \\arctan(x) + 1 - \\arctan(3)$; (d) $y = \\log_3(|x|) - 2$; (e) $y = 3\\ln(|x|) + \\pi$; (f) $y = \\ln(|\\csc(x)|) + 1$"},
  {id:"CN2-ECP27", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 27", lec:"L06", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 126",
   q:"A \\$40 monthly cellphone plan includes 200 anytime minutes, and each additional minute costs \\$0.25. Write a piecewise defined function for the cost per minute used.",
   sol:[
    "Let $m \\ge 0$ be the number of minutes used in a month and $C(m)$ the monthly cost in dollars.",
    "Up to 200 minutes the cost is the flat \\$40. Each minute over 200 adds \\$0.25, and there are $m - 200$ such minutes.",
    "$C(m) = \\begin{cases} 40 & 0 \\le m \\le 200 \\\\ 40 + 0.25(m - 200) & m > 200 \\end{cases}$",
    "If the question is read literally as the average cost per minute, divide by $m$ (for $m > 0$): $A(m) = \\frac{C(m)}{m} = \\begin{cases} \\frac{40}{m} & 0 < m \\le 200 \\\\ 0.25 - \\frac{10}{m} & m > 200 \\end{cases}$, since $\\frac{40 + 0.25(m-200)}{m} = \\frac{0.25m - 10}{m}$."],
   ans:"$C(m) = 40$ for $0 \\le m \\le 200$ and $C(m) = 40 + 0.25(m-200)$ for $m > 200$ (total monthly cost). Average cost per minute: $A(m) = \\frac{40}{m}$ for $0 < m \\le 200$, $A(m) = 0.25 - \\frac{10}{m}$ for $m > 200$.",
   note:"The question says 'cost per minute used' but the official answer is the total monthly cost as a function of minutes used, which is almost certainly what is intended. The literal reading (average cost per minute) is given as well; both versions were checked with sympy."},
  {id:"CN2-ECP28", src:"Course notes Ch. 2, End of Chapter Problem 28", lec:"L06", kind:"chapter", page:"p. 126",
   q:"In Mathanada, federal income tax is calculated using a graduated tax system: 15% tax on income up to \\$49 200; the next \\$49 200 (income over \\$49 200 up to \\$98 400) is taxed at 20.5%; the next \\$53 939 of taxable income (over \\$98 400 and up to \\$151 978) is taxed at 26%. For example, an income of \\$100 000 has the first \\$49 200 taxed at 15%, the next \\$49 200 at 20.5% and the remaining \\$1600 at 26%. Write a piecewise function for the tax rate $R$ (as a percent) as a function of taxable income $I$ (in dollars).",
   sol:[
    "Tax rate on each additional dollar (the marginal rate), in percent:",
    "$R(I) = \\begin{cases} 15 & 0 \\le I \\le 49200 \\\\ 20.5 & 49200 < I \\le 98400 \\\\ 26 & 98400 < I \\le 151978 \\end{cases}$",
    "Total tax owed $T(I)$ in dollars (what the official solution gives): add the tax from each full bracket below $I$ to the tax on the part of $I$ in its own bracket. $0.15(49200) = 7380$ and $0.205(49200) = 10086$.",
    "$T(I) = \\begin{cases} 0.15I & 0 \\le I \\le 49200 \\\\ 7380 + 0.205(I - 49200) & 49200 < I \\le 98400 \\\\ 17466 + 0.26(I - 98400) & 98400 < I \\le 151978 \\end{cases}$",
    "Check with the example: $T(100000) = 17466 + 0.26(1600) = 17466 + 416 = 17882$ dollars, which is $7380 + 10086 + 416$."],
   ans:"$R(I) = 15$ for $0 \\le I \\le 49200$, $20.5$ for $49200 < I \\le 98400$, $26$ for $98400 < I \\le 151978$. Total tax: $T(I) = 0.15I$, then $7380 + 0.205(I - 49200)$, then $17466 + 0.26(I - 98400)$ on the same intervals.",
   note:"Two problems in the printed source. (1) The question asks for the tax rate $R$ as a percent, but the official answer gives the total tax owed in dollars, $0.15I$, $0.205(I - 49200) + 0.15(49200)$, $0.26(I - 98400) + 0.205(49200) + 0.15(49200)$. Both are given here; the official total tax formula is correct and equals $T(I)$ above. (2) The text says the third bracket is the next \\$53 939, but $98400 + 53939 = 152339$, not $151978$. The answer uses the stated upper limit $151978$. The question text was also cut off ('at 26' with no % sign)."}
]);
